/* Try Out MBA - en question bank (translation) */
MBALAB.tryoutLang = MBALAB.tryoutLang || {};
MBALAB.tryoutLang.en = {"mk":["Organizational Behavior & Management Practice (OBMP)","Financial Management & Accounting","Marketing Management","Operations & Supply Chain Management (OSCM)","Decision Making & Negotiation","Business Analytics"],"topics":{"1-1":"Strategizing Your Life","1-2":"Identity, Personality, Behavior & DISC","1-3":"Motivation at Work","1-4":"Leadership: Art or Science?","1-5":"Team Dynamics, Communication & Ego State","1-6":"Organizational Dynamics in the Indonesian Context (LCOP)","2-1":"Foundations of Financial Management & Accounting","2-2":"Financial Statement Analysis & Ratio Analysis","2-3":"Cash Flow & Financial Planning (Zutter & Smart Ch. 4)","2-4":"Managerial Accounting & Costing Systems","2-5":"Cost-Volume-Profit & Activity-Based Costing","2-6":"Time Value of Money (Zutter & Smart Ch. 5)","3-1":"Creating Customer Value & Company/Marketing Strategy","3-2":"Analyzing the Marketing Environment","3-3":"Consumer Markets & Consumer Buyer Behavior","3-4":"Business Markets & Business Buyer Behavior","3-5":"Marketing Information & Marketing Research","3-6":"STP Strategy: Segmentation, Targeting, Positioning","4-1":"Introduction to Operations & Supply Chain Management","4-2":"Operations & Supply Chain Strategy (incl. Blue Ocean)","4-3":"Design of Products and Services","4-4":"Project Management","4-5":"Strategic Capacity Planning","4-6":"Process Design and Analysis","5-1":"Introduction to Decision Making","5-2":"Rational Framework of Decision Making","5-3":"Kepner-Tregoe","5-4":"Alternative vs Value-Focused Thinking","5-5":"Decision Analysis: SMART","5-6":"Decision Analysis: Decision Tree","6-1":"Introduction to Business Analytics","6-2":"How to Deal with Data","6-3":"Descriptive & Diagnostic Analytics, Data-Driven Decision Making","6-4":"Predictive Analytics","6-5":"Prescriptive Analytics","6-6":"Marketing Analytics"},"q":[["The three pillars of a life strategy in the material are ...",["Goals, Budget, Timeline","Skills, Network, Capital","Passion, Profit, People","Purpose (Why), Values (How), Self-Awareness (Who)"],"The material states Purpose = Why, Values = How, Self-Awareness = Who."],["In the material, purpose is likened to ...",["A rigid map that must never be changed","A North Star: the 'why' that underlies everything you do","A list of annual financial targets","A five-year career plan"],"Purpose is the North Star, a reason that goes beyond money, status, and external approval."],["The Ikigai framework maps purpose at the intersection of four zones. Which one is NOT one of those zones?",["What you love","What is socially popular","What the world needs","What you're good at"],"The four Ikigai zones: what you love, what you're good at, what the world needs, and what you can be paid for."],["According to the material, what happens when a person's choices are not aligned with their values?",["State of integrity","State of friction","State of flow","State of dominance"],"Choices aligned with values produce integrity; misaligned choices produce friction, described as a signal worth paying attention to."],["Why does the material suggest recalling the moments when you were angriest when exploring your values?",["Because anger reveals a weakness of character","Because it can be used to blame others","Because anger must be eliminated","Because anger is a precise signal of a violated value"],"Anger is seen as an accurate signal of what matters most to you, namely the values that were violated."],["The material states that 85-90% of people lack real self-awareness even though they believe they have it. The most appropriate implication for a manager is ...",["Self-awareness cannot be developed","Only external feedback is relevant; self-reflection is unnecessary","Self-awareness must be built through active reflection and feedback, not just self-assessment","High self-confidence is enough as proof of self-awareness"],"Only 10-15% of people develop real self-awareness through active reflection and feedback."],["A manager understands their own intentions and emotions well, but often surprises the team with their communication style. Which dimension of self-awareness is weak?",["Inner mirror (internal self-awareness)","Outer mirror (external self-awareness)","Both are strong","Purpose"],"Outer mirror = how accurately we understand how others experience us. The gap between the two mirrors is where growth happens."],["According to the material, what happens when someone lets life run on default?",["Life becomes more flexible because it is not bound by a rigid plan","Their career grows faster because they focus on work","Life runs on autopilot and is shaped by other people's expectations","Decisions become more objective because they are free of emotion"],"The Paradox slide says that a life left on default becomes an autopilot life shaped by other people's expectations."],["The material describes a life strategy as ...",["A detailed map set once for a lifetime","A list of annual targets measured with strict KPIs","A career plan drawn up together with one's direct supervisor","A living compass that is continuously calibrated, not a rigid map"],"A life strategy isn't a rigid map, but a living compass you continuously calibrate; it is a direction, not a fixed destination."],["The Self-Awareness (Who) pillar of a life strategy explains ...",["Why you exist and the direction you want to head in","How you live and the boundaries of your path","What you want to achieve over the next five years","Who you are and the coordinates of your current starting point"],"Pillar 3, Self-Awareness (Who), is who you are and the exact coordinates of where you are starting."],["According to the slides, people with a clear sense of purpose are scientifically proven to ...",["Live longer, be happier, and recover faster from setbacks","Earn more and be promoted faster","Experience conflict with coworkers less often","Have a wider and more diverse social network"],"The Scientifically Proven slide: live longer, higher baseline happiness, recover significantly faster from setbacks."],["In the Ikigai framework, the zone 'what you can be paid for' is described as ...",["Problems you are interested in solving","Where passion meets sustainability","Natural strengths and skills you possess","Activities that energize you and make you enthusiastic"],"Ikigai slide: What you can be paid for = where passion meets sustainability."],["Dina loves her job, is highly skilled, and is well paid, but feels her work does not answer anyone's needs. According to Ikigai, her situation is ...",["Already Ikigai, because three of the four zones are fulfilled","Not yet Ikigai, because the reason for being exists only where all four zones intersect","Already Ikigai, because the paid zone is the deciding zone","Not yet Ikigai, because she must first raise her salary"],"Ikigai lives exclusively where all four zones overlap; the 'what the world needs' zone is not yet fulfilled."],["What does the material advise about when to write a purpose statement?",["Write it today, starting with what feels true now without waiting for it to be perfect","Wait until your career is stable so your purpose won't change again","Write it after receiving full input from your family","Write it after completing all personality tests"],"Slide 6: Write yours today. Don't wait until it is perfect; start with what feels true right now."],["Which purpose statement best fits the formula in the material?",["I want to become a director before I turn forty years old","I exist to work hard every day in my current company","I exist to mentor young engineers so that they build safe infrastructure","My goal is to earn enough money to retire very early"],"Formula: I exist to [action] so that [impact]; only this option contains both an action and an impact."],["According to the material, how do most people acquire their values?",["By consciously choosing them through reflection since adolescence","By drafting them together with a mentor early in their career","By absorbing them from the culture of the company where they work","By inheriting them; few choose them consciously"],"Slide 7: Most people inherit values; few consciously choose them."],["According to the material, what is the root of most inner conflict?",["Lack of clear and measurable career targets","Personality differences with supervisors and coworkers","Holding too many values at the same time","Misalignment between stated values and lived values"],"Slide 7: Misalignment between stated and lived values is the root of most inner conflict."],["Which daily question does the material recommend for testing your values?",["What did I achieve today compared with my targets?","Who did I help today and who helped me in return?","Did my actions today reflect what I say I believe?","How much closer am I to my financial goals today?"],"Slide 8: Ask yourself daily, Did my actions today reflect what I say I believe?"],["Step 01 'Reflect on proudest moments' in discovering values aims to ...",["Identify values that others have violated","Compile a list of achievements for a career portfolio","Identify the values you were honoring during your peak experiences","Compare your achievements with those of your peers"],"Step 01 asks: What core values were you honoring during your peak experiences?"],["Raka has written down 12 important values from reflecting on proud moments and angry moments. According to the material, the next step is ...",["Narrow them down to the top 5 non-negotiables that serve as anchors","Add more values so the list is more complete","Ask his boss to choose the values that fit the company","Rank the values by earning potential"],"Step 03: Narrow to your top 5; distill your list into absolute non-negotiables as anchors in every storm."],["According to the material, self-awareness begins with ...",["Taking as many standard personality tests as possible","Comparing yourself with admired successful figures","Setting the ideal self-image you want to achieve","Radical honesty about your patterns, triggers, and the stories you tell yourself"],"Slide 9: It begins with ruthless honesty about who you actually are, your patterns, triggers, and stories, not who you wish you were."],["In the self-awareness toolkit, Journaling is understood as ...",["Writing to discover what you think, not merely recording events","Recording all daily events in chronological order","Writing a daily work plan to be more productive","Documenting formal feedback from your boss"],"Slide 11: Journaling: Write to discover what you think, not just to record what happened."],["The Mindfulness practice in the self-awareness toolkit trains a person to ...",["Eliminate negative emotions completely at work","Observe the brief pause between stimulus and response before reacting","Respond to every problem as fast as possible","Ask others for their opinion before acting"],"Slide 11: Mindfulness: practice pausing before reacting; observe the brief gap between a stimulus and your response."],["Which question is suggested for Feedback Loops?",["What should I do to get promoted faster?","Which of my colleagues performs better than me?","What do you see in me that I might not see?","How do you rate my performance from one to ten?"],"Slide 11: Feedback Loops: proactively ask trusted people, What do you see in me that I might not see?"],["A manager refuses an analytical task because their test result says they are 'not an analytical type'. According to the material, the mistake is ...",["Not taking enough personality tests","Treating a personality framework as a fixed label rather than a mirror for reflection","Not asking their boss to interpret the test results","Not choosing the most popular framework"],"Slide 11: use MBTI, Enneagram, or StrengthsFinder as mirrors for reflection, not as fixed labels."],["Sinta targets a director position solely because her family expects it, even though it conflicts with her values. Which blueprint step was not carried out properly?",["Step 3 Set Aligned Goals","Step 1 Know Yourself","Step 2 Define Your Purpose","Step 4 Review Regularly"],"Step 3: set goals that honor your values and serve your purpose, not society's expectations."],["The closing formula of the Strategizing Your Life material is ...",["[Goals] + [Discipline] + [Time] = A successful career","[Purpose] + [Values] + [Self-Awareness] = A life lived on your own terms","[Passion] + [Skill] + [Money] = A fulfilling life","[Vision] + [Strategy] + [Execution] = A life by design"],"Slide 14 states [Purpose] + [Values] + [Self-Awareness] = A life lived on your own terms."],["Explain the formula of an effective purpose statement according to the material and give one example of your own purpose statement.",0,"Formula: 'I exist to [action] so that [impact]'. A good purpose statement is simple, personal, and actionable. Example from the material: 'I exist to inspire others so that they believe in their own potential.' A good answer contains a specific action and impact and does not wait to be 'perfect'."],["Describe the four steps of the blueprint for integrating the three pillars into a life strategy and explain why the fourth step matters.",0,"(1) Know yourself: honestly audit your values, strengths, and patterns; (2) Define your purpose: a simple, personal purpose statement; (3) Set aligned goals that honor your values and serve your purpose, not society's expectations; (4) Review regularly: strategy is a living practice that is recalibrated, not a one-time event."],["The material states 'Strategy without identity is just a glorified to-do list'. Explain what it means and the difference between 'what do I want to achieve' and 'who do I want to become'.",0,"Goals without identity are just a task list. Identity-based living shifts the question from what you want to achieve to who you want to become, so that goals become the natural outcome of that person."],["The three components of self-awareness according to the material (Scaling People) are ...",["Vision, mission, strategy","Income, position, experience","Values, personality, competencies/capabilities","Education, age, gender"],"Self awareness: understand your values, identify your personality, analyze your competencies/capabilities."],["The modern form of the DISC model was developed by ...",["Campbell & Pritchard","William Moulton Marston (1928)","Eric Berne","Hippocrates"],"Marston (1928) based DISC on perception of the environment and response to the environment."],["Why does the material focus on behavior rather than personality?",["Behavior can be observed, developed, changed, and controlled","Behavior is innate","Personality never affects work","Behavior is not influenced by the environment"],"Behavior is observable, developable, changeable, and controllable."],["The DISC dimension described as 'active, positive movement in an antagonistic environment' is ...",["Influencing","Dominant","Steady","Compliant"],"Dominant = active in an antagonistic environment; Influencing = active in a favorable environment; Steady = passive in a favorable environment; Compliant = cautious in an unfavorable environment."],["The CORRECT pairing of DISC dimension and 'greatest fear' is ...",["Compliant: being taken advantage of","Influencing: criticism","Dominant: rejection","Steady: loss of security"],"Dominant: being taken advantage of; Influencing: rejection; Compliant: criticism; Steady: loss of security."],["Among the three DISC graphs, the 'The core' graph (private self) describes ...",["A supervisor's assessment of performance","The self-image one wants to show in public","Natural reactions under high pressure, the hardest to change","The current self-perception, which is the easiest to change"],"Mask = public self (easily changed); Core = private self (reaction under pressure, least potential to change); Mirror = perceived self."],["You need to present a change plan to a colleague of the Compliant type (High C). The most appropriate approach is ...",["Push for a quick decision so as not to delay","Prepare the case in advance, present accurate data, explain pros and cons, and make sure there are 'no surprises'","Be brief, direct, focus on results, and don't ramble","Allow time for casual chat and avoid details"],"How to deal with Compliant: prepare the case in advance, pros & cons, accurate data, no surprises, precise explanations. Option A is for Dominant, C for Influencing."],["In The 4 Lenses of Self-Reflection, the lens called The Career Compass for vocational interests and job fit is ...",["Big Five (OCEAN)","DISC","RIASEC (Holland)","Locus of Control"],"RIASEC (Holland): The Career Compass, focused on vocational interests and job fit for long-term career planning."],["Which lens is called the most scientifically valid framework (The Deep Blueprint)?",["DISC","RIASEC (Holland)","Locus of Control","Big Five (OCEAN)"],"Big Five (OCEAN): The Deep Blueprint, the most scientifically valid framework."],["An HR professional wants to assess work resilience and accountability in prospective leaders. According to the material, the most suitable lens is ...",["Locus of Control (The Resilience Dial)","DISC (The Collaboration Engine)","RIASEC (The Career Compass)","Big Five (The Deep Blueprint)"],"Locus of Control focuses on mindset toward outcomes; best for evaluating work resilience and accountability."],["In The 4 Lenses of Self-Reflection, DISC is nicknamed ...",["The Deep Blueprint, the most scientifically valid framework","The Career Compass, a tool for career planning","The Collaboration Engine, the most practical tool for team dynamics","The Resilience Dial, a tool for assessing accountability"],"DISC: The Collaboration Engine, focused on behavioral and communication styles; the most practical tool for team dynamics."],["The four temperaments proposed by Hippocrates as the ancient foundation of DISC are ...",["Choleric, Sanguine, Phlegmatic, Melancholic","Dominant, Influencing, Steady, Compliant","Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness","Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social"],"Origins of DISC slide: Hippocrates (460-370 BC) proposed Choleric, Sanguine, Phlegmatic, Melancholic."],["Who created the first DISC-based assessment, Activity Vector Analysis, in the 1940s?",["William Moulton Marston","Thomas Erikson","Walter Clarke","Hippocrates"],"Walter Clarke (1940s) created the first DISC-based assessment (Activity Vector Analysis)."],["The third DISC graph, 'The mirror' (perceived self), describes ...",["The self-image one wants to show to the public","A person's natural reaction in high-pressure situations","A supervisor's assessment of someone's behavior at the office","Self-perception that combines past experiences and behaviors expected by the environment"],"The mirror: how a person perceives themselves, a combination of past experiences and behaviors currently expected by the environment."],["On the DISC map (Surrounded by Idiots), the type in the task-oriented and introvert/passive quadrant is ...",["Compliant (Blue)","Dominant (Red)","Steady (Green)","Influencing (Yellow)"],"The upper-left quadrant (Task-Oriented & Introvert/Passive/Reserved) is Compliant, colored Blue."],["The Influencing (Yellow) type on the DISC map lies at the combination of ...",["Task-oriented and extrovert/active","Relation-oriented and extrovert/active","Relation-oriented and introvert/passive","Task-oriented and introvert/passive"],"Influencing is in the lower-right quadrant: Relation-Oriented and Extrovert/Active/Implementor."],["The Dominant dimension in DISC measures ...",["How a person influences or persuades others","The pace at which a person carries out activities and responsibilities","A person's response to rules made by others","How a person solves problems and responds to challenges"],"DISC: Dominant measures how a person solves problems and responds to challenges."],["The Steady dimension in DISC measures ...",["How a person solves problems and responds to challenges","How a person influences or persuades others","A person's response to rules made by others","The pace at which a person carries out activities and responsibilities"],"DISC: Steady measures the pace at which a person undertakes activities & responsibilities."],["According to the slide, the main challenge of the Influencing type is ...",["Gather data before decision","Non-verbal communication","Independence","Rapid change and fast pace"],"The DISC slide lists Influencing's Challenge: Non-Verbal Communication."],["The following traits: perfectionist, sensitive, accurate, needs many explanations, asks many questions. The DISC type is ...",["Compliant","Dominant","Influencing","Steady"],"This list of traits appears on the DISC slide: Compliant."],["The adjectives 'patient, good listener, conceals feelings, loyal, supportive' best describe the ... type",["Compliant (Blue)","Steady (Green)","Influencing (Yellow)","Dominant (Red)"],"In the DISC adjective table, these words are in the Green Steady row."],["Your High I colleague wants to present a new project idea. The most appropriate approach according to the material is ...",["Get straight to the point, ask 'what', and focus only on business results","Prepare accurate data and pros and cons, guarantee there are no surprises","Leave room to discuss ideas and people, allow social time, write down details without dwelling on them","Give a long adaptation period and define procedures in detail"],"How to deal with Influencing: let them talk about ideas and people, allow for social time, write details but do not dwell on them."],["When meeting a High D supervisor, which behavior should be AVOIDED?",["Stating the core of the problem briefly and directly","Highlighting the logical benefits and the impact on results","Repeating points and engaging in too much social small talk","Agreeing with the facts and ideas, not the person"],"How to deal with Dominant: Do not repeat yourself, be too sociable, or generalize."],["Your team is dominated by High S and will face a change in work systems. The most appropriate step is ...",["Give them time to adapt, explain the goals, procedures, and roles, and minimize risk","Announce it suddenly so they adjust quickly","Pressure them with tight targets so the change happens immediately","Limit discussion so it doesn't slow down implementation"],"How to deal with Steady: give them time to adjust, define goals, procedures and their role, minimize perceived risk; do not be pushy."],["The principle 'Say the thing you think cannot be said' in Scaling People relates to ...",["Hiring Recruiting in the HR Core Framework (MP)","Building Trust in the Basic Operating Principles (OB)","Team Building in the HR Core Framework (MP)","Performance Management in the HR Core Framework (MP)"],"The Scaling People slide places Building Trust, 'Say the thing you think cannot say', under Basic Operating Principles (OB)."],["On the OCEAN Spectrum Dashboard, the two poles of the Agreeableness dimension are ...",["Preference for routine and curiosity & creativity","Solitary recharge and social energy","Calm resilience and emotional sensitivity","Critical objectivity and empathy & harmony"],"OCEAN slide: [A] Agreeableness ranges from Critical objectivity to Empathy & harmony."],["In the DISC Case Study, the candidate described as detail-oriented, less assertive, inclined to avoid conflict, and preferring to work independently is ...",["Daniel, a Programmer with a High I profile","Ryan, a Senior Network Architect with a High C profile","Taufik, a System Analyst with a High D profile","Daniel, a System Analyst with a High S profile"],"Ryan (40 years old, 17 years of experience) has a High C profile, secondary S: detail-oriented, less assertive, may avoid conflicts, prefers working independently."],["Compare effective ways of communicating with the Dominant type and the Steady type. Give at least three differences.",0,"Dominant: brief, direct, to the point; ask 'what', focus on business/results, highlight logical benefits; do not repeat yourself or be too social. Steady: build a comfortable environment, show sincere interest, ask 'how', give time to adapt, define goals/procedures/roles, minimize risk; do not be pushy or aggressive."],["ICT Section Head case: You form a digitalization task force with an external consultant. Candidate A is High D (secondary C), assertive and decisive; candidate B is High I, communicative but less structured; candidate C is High C, detailed and analytical. Determine who is best suited as Project Coordinator, main counterpart to the consultant, and database manager, with reasons.",0,"Coordinator: the High D candidate (decisive, results-oriented, takes control). Consultant counterpart: High I (communicative, builds relationships). Database: High C (detailed, structured, precise). A strong answer mentions the risks: High D is impatient with High C's tempo or High I's loose style, so clear roles, measurable milestones, and data-based communication are needed."],["From the same case, what is the potential conflict if the High D candidate is appointed coordinator, and how can it be mitigated?",0,"Potential conflict: High D is impatient and tends to control, clashing with High C's need for data/detailed explanations and with High I's spontaneous style. Mitigation: define roles, goals, and procedures at the outset; provide data before decisions; give High C room to ask questions; agree on a check-in cadence; direct High D to focus on results without pressuring the process."],["According to Campbell & Pritchard (1976), motivation is a set of variables that explain ...",["Direction, intensity, persistence","Forming, storming, performing","Ability, skill, aptitude","Planning, organizing, controlling"],"Motivation explains the direction, intensity, and persistence of behavior."],["The formula linking performance and motivation in the material is ...",["Performance = f (Opportunity only)","Performance = Motivation - Ability","Performance = Ability + Motivation","Performance = f (Ability x Motivation)"],"Performance = f (Ability x Motivation)."],["The abbreviation AMO in the Lepak et al. (2006) model stands for ...",["Action, Measure, Outcome","Aptitude, Morale, Objective","Attitude, Management, Output","Ability, Motivation, Opportunity"],"AMO links the HR system, HR mechanisms, and organizational performance through Ability, Motivation, Opportunity."],["Which of the following is a component of ABILITY in the performance model?",["Aptitude, skill, understanding of task","Choice to expend effort","Choice to persist","Choice of degree of effort"],"Ability = aptitude, skill, understanding of task; the other three choices are components of motivation."],["The material stresses that 'choice to expend effort' is ...",["A momentary feeling","A decision, not a feeling","An innate talent","The result of salary alone"],"The choice to expend effort is a decision, not a feeling."],["An employee asks 'Is it worth my time and energy? What will I gain if I put in more effort?' Which motivation component is being assessed?",["Choice of degree of effort to expend","Understanding of task","Choice to persist","Choice to expend effort"],"The question about whether the effort is worthwhile reflects the choice of how much effort to expend (intensity)."],["An employee has very high skill and aptitude but is barely motivated. Based on Performance = f (Ability x Motivation), the predicted performance is ...",["Cannot be predicted with this model","Moderate, because they offset each other","Still high because ability is high","Low, because of the multiplicative relationship: motivation near zero depresses the result"],"Because Ability and Motivation are multiplied, a deficiency in one can lower performance even when the other factor is high."],["The Cambridge English Online Dictionary defines motivation as ...",["A set of variables that explain direction, intensity, persistence","The ability to complete a task with skill and understanding","Enthusiasm for doing something; the need or reason for doing something","The opportunity to perform provided by the organization"],"Slide 2 quotes two Cambridge definitions: enthusiasm for doing something and the need or reason for doing something."],["Bayu keeps working on a data migration project for months despite repeatedly encountering obstacles. The Campbell & Pritchard motivation variable that is evident is ...",["Direction","Intensity","Aptitude","Persistence"],"Persistence describes how long effort is sustained, one of the three motivation variables of Campbell & Pritchard (1976)."],["A staff member chooses to spend work hours on a client report instead of other activities. The motivation variable that explains the choice of where effort is directed is ...",["Persistence","Intensity","Direction","Opportunity"],"Direction explains the direction of behavior, that is, where effort is channeled."],["Two employees do the same task, but one works flat out and the other does the bare minimum. This difference mainly concerns ...",["Direction","Persistence","Understanding of task","Intensity"],"Intensity explains how much effort is expended in behavior."],["A manager gives a detailed explanation of the goals and steps of a task so that staff understand it. The performance factor being improved is ...",["Motivation, through choice to persist","Motivation, through choice to expend effort","Opportunity, through access to work tools","Ability, through understanding of task"],"In the formula Performance = f (Ability x Motivation), understanding of task is a component of Ability."],["The formula Performance = f (Ability x Motivation) on the slide is based on the work of ...",["Campbell & Pritchard (1976) in the Handbook of Industrial and Organizational Psychology","Lepak et al. (2006) on HR systems and performance","Cambridge English Online Dictionary","Mehrabian (1967) on personal communication"],"Slide 3: Based on Campbell, J. & Pritchard, R. (1976), Motivation theory in I/O Psychology, in M. Dunnette (ed.) Handbook of I/O Psychology."],["Rina is willing to try and work hard, but stops as soon as the first difficulty appears. Which motivational choice is weak?",["Choice to expend effort","Choice of degree of effort to expend","Choice to persist","Understanding of task"],"Slide 3 lists three choices: expend effort, degree of effort, and persist; stopping at difficulty shows a weak choice to persist."],["HR provides intensive technical training, but performance does not rise. A survey shows employees feel extra effort is not worth what they would get. The most accurate diagnosis is ...",["The problem is in Ability, so more training is needed","The problem is in Motivation, namely the choice of how much effort to expend","The problem is in aptitude, so re-selection is needed","The problem is in the employees' understanding of task"],"The training already targeted Ability; the assessment 'is it worth my time and energy' is the choice of degree of effort, a Motivation component."],["The AMO model on slide 4, titled 'An Integrated Model Linking HR System, HR System Mechanisms, and Organizational Performance', was proposed by ...",["Lepak et al. (2006)","Campbell & Pritchard (1976)","Eric Berne","Yuni Ros Bangun (2010)"],"Slide 4 cites the source Lepak et al, 2006."],["In the diagram of the Lepak et al. (2006) model, the element that comes first, before the HR System, is ...",["Organizational Climate","Strategic Focus","Employee Collective Performance","Psychological Climate"],"The AMO diagram starts from Strategic Focus leading to the HR System, then climate and employee mechanisms."],["In the Lepak et al. (2006) model, the HR System influences two types of climate, namely ...",["Organizational Climate and Psychological Climate","Team Climate and Leadership Climate","Safety Climate and Service Climate","Learning Climate and Innovation Climate"],"The diagram shows Organizational Climate and Psychological Climate between the HR System and employee mechanisms."],["In the AMO model diagram, 'Employee Ability to Perform' is explained in parentheses as ...",["Effort and Persistence","Knowledge and Skill","Authority and Resources","Attitude and Engagement"],"The Employee Ability to Perform box on slide 4 is annotated (Knowledge and Skill)."],["According to the Lepak et al. (2006) diagram, the correct sequence of outcomes from the ability, motivation, and opportunity mechanisms is ...",["Organizational Performance, Employee Performance, Collective Performance","Employee Performance, Employee Collective Performance, Organizational Performance","Employee Collective Performance, Strategic Focus, HR System","Psychological Climate, Organizational Climate, Employee Performance"],"The AMO mechanisms lead to Employee Performance, then Employee Collective Performance, then Organizational Performance."],["Which statement is consistent with both slide 3 and slide 4?",["Both include ability and motivation; the AMO model adds opportunity","Both state that motivation is enough to explain performance","The AMO model removes ability and uses only motivation","Campbell's formula places opportunity as the main multiplier"],"Slide 3: Performance = f (Ability x Motivation); slide 4: AMO = Ability, Motivation, Opportunity."],["Explain the three choices that form motivation according to the material and give an example in a work context.",0,"(1) Choice to expend effort: deciding whether or not to make an effort; (2) Choice of degree of effort: judging whether it is worth putting in more effort; (3) Choice to persist: staying the course when obstacles appear. Contextual examples are valued."],["Your team is skilled and motivated but performs poorly because it lacks authority, tools, and support. Use the AMO model to diagnose and propose actions.",0,"The problem is in Opportunity, not Ability or Motivation. Actions: clarify authority and roles, provide resources/tools, remove structural barriers, give access to information and room to participate, then monitor the impact on performance."],["Why is performance modeled as the product of ability and motivation rather than their sum? What are the implications for managers?",0,"Multiplication means a weakness in one factor depresses the final result. Implication: managers need to address both (selection and development for ability, job design and incentives for motivation), not just one side."],["In the list of HBR-based leadership views, Goleman is associated with ...",["Authentic leadership","Emotional Intelligence","Humility and professional will","Adaptive leadership"],"Item 1: Emotional Intelligence (Goleman)."],["Drucker defined effective leadership as ...",["Inspiring followers","Learning from hardship","Knowing what needs to be done","Accepting oneself as an imperfect human being"],"Item 2: Effective, what needs to be done (Drucker)."],["The combination 'humility and professional will' is associated with ...",["Collins","Kotter and Heifetz","Bennis and Thomas","Ancona"],"Item 5: Collins."],["Leadership 'trained by difficulties and hardship' is associated with ...",["Goffee and Jones","Bennis and Thomas","Goleman","George et al."],"Item 6: Bennis and Thomas."],["Among the six CEO Excellence behaviors (McKinsey) in the material, 'Being a Giver' is quoted from ...",["Jonah Berger","Adam Grant","David Epstein","Christian Busch"],"Giver: Adam Grant; Mobilizing People: Jonah Berger; Orchestrator: Christian Busch; Learner: David Epstein."],["The behavior 'Orchestrator: building bridges', which emphasizes serendipity as the ability to see connections others do not, comes from the thinking of ...",["Adam Grant","Christian Busch","Jonah Berger","Goleman"],"Serendipity is not mere luck, but strategically seeing and combining connections (Christian Busch)."],["The material says 'You cannot decide to be authentic because it is good for business'. The main message is ...",["Purpose must always sacrifice profit","Purpose should be used as a marketing tool","Choose to do the right thing first, then struggle to find the business case","Profit must not be considered"],"Being purpose-driven means exploring the boundary between purpose and profit: choose what is right, then find the business case."],["In the Leadership based on HBR list, the view 'Adaptive and coping with rapid change' is associated with ...",["Goffee, Jones","George et al","Ancona et al","Kotter and Heifetz"],"Item 3: Adaptive and coping with rapid change (Kotter and Heifetz)."],["The view that leaders 'inspire other people to follow them' is associated with ...",["Collins","Drucker","Bennis and Thomas","Goffee, Jones"],"Item 4: Inspire other people to follow them (Goffee, Jones)."],["The view 'Authentic, learn from others but do not imitate' is associated with ...",["Ancona et al","Kotter and Heifetz","George et al","Goleman"],"Item 7: Authentic, learn from others but do not imitate (George et al)."],["The view 'Incomplete, acceptance as a human' is associated with ...",["George et al","Ancona et al","Collins","Goffee, Jones"],"Item 8: Incomplete, acceptance as a human (Ancona et al)."],["The list of eight leadership views on slide 2 comes from the book ...",["HBR's 10 Must Reads On Leadership","CEO Excellence (McKinsey)","Scaling People","Surrounded by Idiots"],"Slide 2 is titled Leadership based on HBR and shows the cover of HBR's 10 Must Reads On Leadership."],["The CEO Excellence (McKinsey) chart on the slide is compiled from ...",["Aggregated results from more than 100 CEOs showing considerable variation in self-assessed performance","A survey of 1,106 office workers in the United States","Interviews with the world's top 10 CEOs according to HBR","Financial performance data of state-owned and private companies"],"Slide 3: Aggregated results from more than 100 CEOs show considerable variation in self-assessed performance by behavior."],["In the CEO Excellence chart, the label 'Be Bold' is shown on the dimension ...",["Engage the board","Align the organization","Mobilize through leaders","Set the direction"],"The Be Bold arrow on slide 3 points to the Set the direction hexagon."],["Vision, Strategy, and Resource allocation are elements of the CEO Excellence dimension ...",["Set the direction","Manage personal effectiveness","Connect with stakeholders","Align the organization"],"In the McKinsey chart, Set the direction consists of Vision, Strategy, Resource allocation."],["Culture, Organization design, and Talent management belong to the CEO Excellence dimension ...",["Mobilize through leaders","Align the organization","Set the direction","Engage the board"],"In the McKinsey chart, Align the organization consists of Culture, Organization design, Talent management."],["Team composition, Teamwork, and Operating rhythm are part of the CEO Excellence dimension ...",["Align the organization","Connect with stakeholders","Manage personal effectiveness","Mobilize through leaders"],"In the McKinsey chart, Mobilize through leaders consists of Team composition, Teamwork, Operating rhythm."],["In the CEO Excellence chart, 'Moments of truth' (best behaviors to prevent and manage crisis) belongs to the dimension ...",["Engage the board","Set the direction","Manage personal effectiveness","Connect with stakeholders"],"Connect with stakeholders contains Social purpose, Stakeholder interactions, and Moments of truth (footnote 2: prevent and manage crisis)."],["The element 'Perspectives' in the Manage personal effectiveness dimension carries a footnote meaning ...",["Keeping yourself in perspective by staying humble","Seeing links that others don't see","Best behaviors to prevent and manage crisis","Exploring the boundary between purpose and profit"],"Footnote 1 of the McKinsey chart: Perspectives = keeping yourself in perspective by staying humble."],["The CEO Excellence behavior 'Mobilizing People' on the slide is quoted from ...",["Jonah Berger","Adam Grant","Christian Busch","David Epstein"],"Slide 6: 3. Mobilizing People, a quote from Jonah Berger."],["The main message of Jonah Berger's quote on the Mobilizing People behavior is ...",["True expertise takes longer than expected","Opportunities arise from connecting seemingly random ideas","The right words help people listen, be persuaded, and be motivated","Leaders must be there for others without expecting anything in return"],"Berger: it is hard to get people to listen and be motivated, but the right words can help; communicate with intention and care."],["The behavior 'A learner: dynamic capability and generalist specialist' is supported by a quote from ...",["Jonah Berger","Christian Busch","David Epstein","Adam Grant"],"Slide 9 quotes David Epstein."],["According to David Epstein's quote, the challenge we face is ...",["Becoming as deep a specialist as possible from the start of one's career","Maintaining breadth and interdisciplinary thinking in a world that demands hyperspecialization","Finding a business case for every right decision","Building a personal reputation through a wide network"],"Epstein: maintain the benefits of breadth, diverse experience, interdisciplinary thinking, and delayed concentration in a world that demands hyperspecialization."],["According to the quote on the 'Grit & Get Things Done' behavior, developing true expertise ...",["Can be accelerated with strong natural talent","Has no shortcuts and takes longer than most people imagine","Depends on the luck of finding opportunities","Can be achieved through short-term intensive training alone"],"Slide 8: there are no shortcuts to excellence... it all takes time, longer than most people imagine."],["A director connects findings from the research team with seemingly unrelated complaints from the sales team to create a new product line. The most fitting CEO Excellence behavior is ...",["Being a Giver","Grit & Get Things Done","Orchestrator: building bridges","Purpose-driven"],"Busch: serendipity is seeing links that others don't and combining them strategically into new opportunities."],["A manager keeps helping a former subordinate who moved to another company, without expecting anything in return. The behavior reflected is ...",["Mobilizing People","A learner","Being a Giver","Orchestrator"],"Adam Grant: you never know where somebody's going to end up; it's not just about reputation but being there for other people."],["Which is NOT one of the dimensions in the CEO Excellence chart (McKinsey)?",["Engage the board","Maximize shareholder value","Connect with stakeholders","Manage personal effectiveness"],"The six dimensions: Set the direction, Engage the board, Align the organization, Mobilize through leaders, Connect with stakeholders, Manage personal effectiveness."],["Explain the 'Grit & Get Things Done' behavior in CEO Excellence and distinguish 'falling in love' from 'staying in love'.",0,"There are no shortcuts to excellence: expertise and solutions to hard problems take longer than expected. Grit is staying faithful to what you care deeply about. Falling in love = initial attraction; staying in love = long-term commitment."],["Compare the leadership views of Drucker (effective), Kotter-Heifetz (adaptive), and Goleman (EI). In what situations is each most needed?",0,"Drucker: focuses on what needs to be done, so effective at priorities and execution. Kotter-Heifetz: adaptive in the face of rapid change, so needed during turbulence. Goleman: emotional intelligence for managing oneself and relationships, important when leading people and handling conflict. A strong answer gives example situations."],["The views 'authentic: learn from others but do not imitate' and 'incomplete: acceptance as a human' have similarities and differences. Explain them and link them to the 'learner' (generalist specialist) behavior in CEO Excellence.",0,"Both emphasize authenticity and accepting limitations. Authentic: learn from others without imitating; Incomplete: leaders need not be good at everything and should complement themselves with others. Learner: maintain breadth and diverse experience in a world that demands hyperspecialization (Epstein)."],["The concept of Ego State in the material was put forward by ...",["Bruce Tuckman","Albert Mehrabian","Peter Drucker","Eric Berne"],"Concept of Ego State (Eric Berne): Parent, Adult, Child."],["The ego state that is rational, seeks facts, and makes decisions without emotion is ...",["Natural Child","Parent","Adult","Adapted Child"],"Adult = rational: objective observer, fact seeker, problem solver."],["Which of the following is NOT one of the five things high-performing teams do according to the material?",["Communicate directly","Make meetings matter","Rely on email to solve problems","Create a culture of appreciation"],"Great teams don't hide behind email; they pick up the phone to resolve problems quickly."],["In the cohesiveness x performance norm matrix, a team with high cohesiveness but low performance norms is called ...",["High performing team","The 'scary' (dangerous) area","The 'agent' area","The gray area with low cohesiveness"],"Cohesive but with low work norms produces low performance; the 'scary' area."],["Regarding the claim that 'communication is 93% non-verbal' (Mehrabian, 1967), the material states that ...",["The claim is an urban legend (Lapakko, 2007)","The claim is always true","Only words matter","Only intonation matters"],"Lapakko (2007) questions the 93% non-verbal claim: if it were true, what would be the point of learning a language?"],["A boss asks 'Where is the report I asked for yesterday?' and the subordinate replies 'But I already put it on your desk, why didn't you look?'. The type of transaction is ...",["Parallel (open) transaction","Complementary adult-adult","Crossed (blocked) transaction","Ulterior transaction"],"The response comes from an unexpected ego state, so the transaction is crossed and potentially leads to conflict."],["Ronny says 'The air is nice and cool' (Adult), but his Child sends a hidden message 'let's get closer'. This is ...",["A Parent-Parent transaction","An ulterior transaction","A crossed transaction","A parallel transaction"],"An ulterior transaction has a social (overt) message and a hidden psychological message at different ego state levels."],["In the cohesiveness x performing norm matrix, the 'Agent' area is a team whose ...",["Members are highly cohesive and work norms are high","Members are highly cohesive but work norms are low","Members are not cohesive (individualistic) but norms and performance are good","Members are not cohesive and performance is low"],"Slide 4: Area Agent = members are not cohesive (individualistic), performance is good, work norms (spirit) are good."],["For a team in the 'gray' area, the slide advises ...",["Immediately increasing cohesiveness to become a high-performing team","Disbanding the team and forming a new one","Not immediately increasing cohesiveness because it may form a scary area","Lowering work norms so the team becomes more cohesive"],"Slide 5: do not increase cohesiveness because a scary area would form; raise work norms toward the agent area."],["The Tuckman stage characterized by 'Conflict & Tension', differing opinions and conflict, is ...",["Storming","Forming","Norming","Adjourning"],"Tuckman Journey: 2. Storming = Conflict & Tension."],["The Tuckman stage 'Celebration & Closure', namely completion of the program, reflection, and farewell, is ...",["Performing","Norming","Forming","Adjourning"],"Tuckman Journey: 5. Adjourning = Celebration & Closure."],["The team starts to gel, rules and norms are agreed, and members trust each other. According to Tuckman, the team is at the ... stage",["Norming","Storming","Performing","Forming"],"Tuckman Journey: 3. Norming = Handshake & Rules, starting to gel, rules and norms agreed, mutual trust."],["Ron Friedman's research data on high-performing teams on the slide was obtained from a survey of ...",["1,106 US office workers","More than 100 global CEOs","341 employees of state-owned and private companies","500 managers in Indonesia"],"Slide 11: Research Data 1,106 US office workers surveyed."],["The three psychological needs for high performance according to the slide are ...",["Autonomy, Competence, Relatedness","Ability, Motivation, Opportunity","Purpose, Values, Self-Awareness","Speed, Innovation, Efficiency"],"Slide 11: 3 psychological needs: Autonomy, Competence, Relatedness (Connectedness)."],["In the 'Make Meetings Matter' point, the figure '39% more' refers to ...",["A meeting agenda distributed in advance","Prework: the team prepares material before the meeting","Regular check-ins to align follow-ups","Phone calls per day between team members"],"Slide 12: 39% more prework, 26% more agenda, 55% more check-in."],["According to the slide, high-performing teams receive appreciation from managers ...",["72% more","44% more","79% more","25% more"],"Slide 14: 72% more from coworkers, 79% more from managers, 44% more expressing appreciation."],["According to the material, the difference between a growth culture and a genius culture is ...",["Growth culture focuses on star performers, genius culture on teams","Growth culture focuses on human potential, genius culture focuses on star performers","Growth culture focuses on targets, genius culture on process","Growth culture focuses on seniority, genius culture on innovation"],"Slide 10: Growth Culture focuses on human potential, exceeding genius culture which focuses on star performers."],["The psychological safety slide shows the book 'The Fearless Organization' by ...",["Ron Friedman","Amy C. Edmondson","Mary C. Murphy","Jonah Berger"],"Slide 15 shows the cover of The Fearless Organization by Amy C. Edmondson."],["In the communication model on the slide, the process by which the receiver interprets the message received is called ...",["Encoding","Noise","Message Channel","Decoding"],"Slide 17: The Sender does Encoding, the message passes through the Message Channel with Noise, the Receiver does Decoding."],["When a colleague tells a story, you immediately ask 'Why did you do that?'. The listening problem that occurs is ...",["Probing","Evaluating","Advising","Interpreting"],"Slide 18: Probing is exemplified by 'Why did you do that?'."],["The SBI framework suggested for fact-based criticism stands for ...",["Skill-Behavior-Intention","Situation-Belief-Interpretation","Strength-Benefit-Improvement","Situation-Behavior-Impact"],"Slide 21: Fact-Based Criticism uses the SBI framework (Situation-Behavior-Impact)."],["According to the Concreteness principle, which sentence is the most effective?",["Let's become a much more innovative team","Let's continuously improve service quality","Let's think outside the box together","Let's spend 30 minutes every Friday trying one new way to serve customers"],"Slide 25: concrete language is more effective; the concrete version: Let's spend 30 minutes every Friday trying one new way to serve customers."],["According to the Similarity slide (The Power of Words), employees whose linguistic style is similar to their coworkers' ...",["Are half as likely to be in conflict","Adapt five times faster","Are three times more likely to be promoted","Lead meetings three times more often"],"Slide 23: employees whose linguistic style was more similar to their coworkers were three times more likely to be promoted."],["A boss says 'Nobody can leave until this report is finished'. The ego state displayed is ...",["Nurturing Parent","Controlling Parent","Adult","Adapted Child"],"Slide 31: an example of Controlling Parent is 'Nobody can leave until this report is finish'."],["Sylvia: 'Can you work overtime tonight so we can finish our task?' Laksmi: 'Ugh, enough already, I'm sick of this.' The correct analysis is ...",["Parallel transaction: both speak from the Adult ego state","Ulterior transaction: there is a hidden message from the Parent","Crossed transaction: an Adult question is answered from the Child ego state","Parallel transaction: both speak from the Child ego state"],"Slide 42 shows this example as a Cross/Blocked Transaction; the response comes from an unexpected ego state."],["The sequence of the three steps in 'The Conscious Shift: Restoring Constructive Dialogue' is ...",["Pause, Breathe, Choose","Analyze, Shift to Adult, Realign to Parallel","Shift to Adult, Analyze, Use ulterior transaction","Realign to Parallel, Analyze, Shift to Parent"],"Slide 50: Step 1 Analyze, Step 2 Shift to Adult, Step 3 Realign to Parallel with the goal I am OK, You are OK."],["In the Pause-Breathe-Choose Toolkit, the 4-7-8 breathing technique means ...",["Inhale 4 times, pause 7 seconds, repeat 8 times","Inhale for 7 seconds, hold for 4 seconds, exhale for 8 seconds","See 4 things, hear 7 sounds, count to 8","Inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 7 seconds, exhale slowly for 8 seconds"],"Slide 52: inhale for 4 seconds through the nose, hold for 7 seconds, exhale slowly for 8 seconds through the mouth."],["Explain active listening, four common listening problems, and three behaviors that show you are truly listening actively.",0,"Active listening: not just hearing but tuning in to the other person's thoughts and feelings. Problems: evaluating, probing, advising, interpreting. Behaviors: eye contact and meaningful nodding, saying 'what I hear is...', clarifying with questions, and pausing before responding."],["Why do the best teams report more mistakes? Explain the role of psychological safety and the role of the leader.",0,"The best teams report more because they dare to speak up, not because they fail more often. Psychological safety makes it possible to admit mistakes, ask questions, and challenge. Without it, silence, underreporting, and repeated losses appear. Leaders create a learning culture, not a blame culture."],["Boss: 'Where is the report I asked for yesterday?' Subordinate: 'I already put it on your desk, it may have been misplaced, let me help you look, sorry, sir.' Analyze the type of transaction, compare it with a defensive response, and explain how to repair a crossed transaction.",0,"This cooperative response maintains a parallel transaction so communication continues constructively. A defensive response ('why didn't you look?') crosses and triggers conflict. Repair: one or both parties shift to the Adult ego state, focusing on facts and solutions; if that fails, an ulterior transaction can be used for face saving."],["The main variables in the LCOP model in the material are ...",["Cost, Volume, Profit","Price, Product, Place, Promotion","Leadership, Culture, Organizational capability, Politics (toward Performance)","Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing"],"LCOP: Leadership, Culture, Organizational capability, and Politics, which predict company performance."],["Intangible organizational capability is defined as ...",["The company's total assets","Elements and factors the organization does extremely well and that predict business performance","Assets, technology, machinery","Number of employees"],"Tangible = assets, technology, machinery; intangible = factors the organization does extremely well as predictors of performance."],["The relationship between organizational political behavior and company performance in the model is ...",["Positive","Negative","None","Always neutral"],"Political behavior predicts performance negatively (combined -0.47; SOEs -0.94; private -0.68)."],["The leadership dimension with the largest loading in the SOE population (1.81) and the private sector (1.93) is ...",["Strategic","Transformational","Transactional","Laissez faire"],"Strategic leadership: SOEs 1.81 and private 1.93."],["Laissez faire leadership has a loading of -0.83 (SOEs) and -0.94 (private). This means ...",["It contributes positively to the leadership construct","It is only relevant in SOEs","It has no effect","It runs in the opposite direction to the effective leadership construct"],"A negative loading indicates the opposite direction to the leadership construct."],["A test of the difference in mean political behavior between SOEs and the private sector yields Sig. (2-tailed) 0.000. The conclusion is ...",["Ho is accepted: there is no difference","Ho is rejected: mean political behavior in SOEs is higher than in the private sector","The private sector mean is higher","The result cannot be interpreted"],"Sig 0.000 < 0.05, so Ho is rejected; the finding supports Vigoda and Kapun (2005)."],["The influence of culture on organizational capability: SOEs 0.60 vs private 0.06, while the influence of leadership in the private sector is 1.58. The most appropriate interpretation is ...",["Leadership is not important in the private sector","Culture is highly dominant in the private sector","Culture is not important in SOEs","In the private sample, capability is almost entirely shaped by leadership, while culture barely contributes directly"],"The culture coefficient of 0.06 is very small compared with leadership at 1.58 in the private population."],["One finding from the opening data matrix is that ROA differs among oil and gas companies, with the lowest ROA at ...",["Shell plc","Pertamina","Saudi Aramco","PLN"],"Slide 8: ROA among companies in oil and gas (Pertamina lowest)."],["In the SOE comparison table on slide 5, the ROA of PT PLN (Persero) is recorded at ...",["1.04%","3.42%","8.24%","5.24%"],"Slide 5 highlights PLN's ROA of 1.04%, compared with Pertamina 3.42% and Telkom 8.24%."],["According to the slide 'DYNAMIC nya', the three dynamics that explain performance differences are ...",["Strategy, structure, systems","Ability, motivation, opportunity","Organizational capability, leadership, culture","Profit, stock price, assets"],"Slide 9 mentions organizational capability, leadership, and culture."],["An example of tangible organizational capability according to the slide is ...",["Speed, innovation, talent","Asset, technology, machines","Leadership, culture, politics","Adaptability, involvement, consistency"],"Slide 10: Organizational Capability Tangible: Asset, Technology, Machines."],["The nine-dimension model of organizational capability used in the material comes from ...",["Lepak et al. (2006)","Yuni Ros Bangun, SBM ITB (2010)","Vigoda and Kapun (2005)","McKinsey Center for CEO Excellence"],"Slide 11: Organizational Capability 9 Dimensions, Yuni Ros Bangun, SBM ITB Model 2010."],["The organizational capability dimension with the highest factor loading in all three populations (combined, SOEs, private) is ...",["Speed","Seeks Change","Talent","Innovation"],"Slide 23: Seeks Change 1.25 (combined), 1.20 (SOEs), 1.26 (private), the highest in all populations."],["The organizational capability dimension with the lowest factor loading in all three populations is ...",["Innovation","Customer connectivity","Efficiency","Strategic Responsiveness"],"Slide 23: Innovation 0.46 (combined), 0.49 (SOEs), 0.50 (private), the lowest in all populations."],["In the influence comparison table, the relationship of the leader to perceptions of organizational politics is ...",["Positive in all populations (combined 1.37)","Positive in SOEs, negative in private","Not significant in all populations","Negative in all populations (combined -1.37)"],"Slide 18: Leader to perception of politics -1.37 (combined), -1.19 (SOEs), -1.47 (private), a negative relationship."],["The structural equation for company performance for the combined population is ...",["1.08 (Organizational Capability) - 0.94 (Political Behavior)","1.26 (Organizational Capability) - 0.47 (Political Behavior)","1.08 (Organizational Capability) - 0.68 (Political Behavior)","1.03 (Leadership) + 0.52 (Organizational Culture)"],"Slide 21: Company Performance, Combined Population = 1.26 (Organizational Capability) - 0.47 (Political Behavior)."],["The organizational capability equation for the SOE population is ...",["1.03 (Leadership) + 0.52 (Organizational Culture)","1.58 (Leadership) + 0.06 (Organizational Culture)","1.44 (Leadership) + 0.6 (Organizational Culture)","1.08 (Leadership) - 0.94 (Organizational Culture)"],"Slide 21: Organizational Capability, SOE Population = 1.44 (Leadership) + 0.6 (Organizational Culture)."],["Based on the influence comparison table, the TRUE statement is ...",["The influence of capability on performance is greatest in the SOE population","The influence of culture on capability is greatest in the private population","The negative influence of politics on performance is smallest in SOEs","The influence of the leader on company culture is greatest in the private population"],"Slide 18: leader to culture 1.59 in private (SOEs 1.34; combined 1.43); the other options contradict the table's figures."],["Which is NOT a culture dimension in the LCOP model on the slide?",["Adaptability","Involvement","Hierarchy","Mission/goals"],"Slide 22: the culture dimensions are Adaptability, Involvement, Consistency, and Mission/goals."],["The political behavior dimension with the lowest loading in all three populations is ...",["Favoritism","External Influence","Power Challenge","Government Regulation"],"Slide 24: Government Regulation 0.24 (combined), 0.21 (SOEs), 0.26 (private)."],["Which political behavior dimension has a much higher loading in the private sector (1.15) than in SOEs (0.65)?",["External Intervention","Resistance to Change","Self Serving Bias","Favoritism"],"Slide 24: External Intervention SOEs 0.65 vs private 1.15; the other three options are actually higher or equal in SOEs."],["In the political behavior hypothesis test (Ho: mu1 = mu2), mu1 represents ...",["The mean perception of political behavior of the private group","The mean company performance of the SOE group","The mean perception of political behavior of the PTPN (SOE) group","The difference in organizational capability between SOEs and private"],"Slide 25: mu1 = mean perception of political behavior of the PTPN (SOE) group; mu2 = the private group."],["In the conceptual framework on slide 15, corporate culture and organizational capability act as ... toward company performance (growth and sustain).",["Control variables","External moderating variables","Mediating variables","Final dependent variables"],"Slide 15 places the MEDIATING Variable block between the individual and Company/Org Performance (1. Growth, 2. Sustain)."],["In the Organizational Capability Mediation Model (Yuni Ros Bangun), Organizational Politics acts as ...",["The final outcome positively influenced by Leadership","A mediator with a positive effect on Capability","An exogenous variable influencing Culture","A mediator with a negative effect on Performance"],"Slide 14: Organizational Politics (Mediator); H4 and H5: politics negatively affects Performance, leadership negatively affects politics."],["According to the Key Observation in the palm oil case study (SOE vs Private), the advantage of private companies is ...",["Agility, innovation, and market/global connectivity","Social impact, smallholder integration, regulatory compliance","Stable employment and a civil-service-oriented culture","Government funding and state financing"],"Slide 13: SOEs excel in social impact, smallholder integration, regulatory compliance; Private excels in agility, innovation, market/global connectivity."],["According to the managerial implications, the three leadership dimensions with the greatest influence in the private population are ...",["Strategic, transformational, and facilitating employees","Strategic, transformational, and transactional","Transformational, transactional, and laissez faire","Strategic, transactional, and laissez faire"],"Slide 26: private = strategic, transformational, leadership that makes things easier for employees; SOEs = strategic, transformational, transactional."],["Besides mission/goals (1.00 in both), which culture dimension has a higher loading in the private sector than in SOEs?",["Adaptability","Involvement","Talent","Consistency"],"Slide 22: Consistency SOEs 0.89 vs private 0.98; Adaptability and Involvement are higher in SOEs; Talent is not a culture dimension."],["Write the structural equation for company performance for the SOE population and interpret its coefficients.",0,"Performance (SOEs) = 1.08 (Organizational Capability) - 0.94 (Political Behavior). Capability increases performance, while political behavior reduces it and can 'offset' the benefit of capability; companies need policies to curb harmful political behavior."],["Compare the three most harmful political behaviors in SOEs and in the private sector, then propose managerial policies for each.",0,"SOEs: resistance to change, self serving bias, power challenge, favoritism. Private: external intervention, self serving bias, power challenge, resistance to change. Policies: change management and communication (resistance), objective performance systems (self serving bias, favoritism), governance and limits on external intervention, role/authority arrangements (power challenge)."],["As a consultant, an SOE with low ROA asks for advice based on the LCOP model. Draw up recommendations on leadership, culture, and political behavior.",0,"Leadership: strengthen strategic, transformational, transactional. Culture: adaptability and involvement. Politics: reduce resistance to change and self serving bias. Relationship: leadership raises capability and culture and lowers political behavior, so performance improves."],["The main goal of a financial manager according to the material is ...",["Minimizing all costs","Maximizing owners' wealth (shareholder wealth) in the long run","Maximizing the amount of assets","Maximizing current-year profit"],"The main goal of a financial manager is to maximize owners' wealth, which in public companies is generally reflected in the share price; not short-term profit."],["The basic accounting equation is ...",["Assets = Liabilities - Equity","Revenue = Assets - Expenses","Equity = Assets + Liabilities","Assets = Liabilities + Equity"],"Every transaction keeps the equation Assets = Liabilities + Equity in balance."],["The three main decisions of a financial manager are ...",["Sales, purchasing, inventory","Tax, audit, reporting","Investment, financing, working capital","Production, marketing, HR"],"Investment (capital budgeting), financing (capital structure), working capital decisions."],["Project Rotor has total EPS of 2.80 (1.40; 1.00; 0.40) and Valve 3.00 (0.60; 1.00; 1.40). Why doesn't the manager automatically choose Valve?",["Because EPS is irrelevant","Because profit maximization ignores timing, cash flow, and risk","Because total EPS may not be summed","Because Rotor is always better"],"Money received earlier is worth more (time value of money); profit is not cash; risk may differ."],["A yacht is sold for Rp1,000,000 on credit with a cost of Rp800,000 paid in cash now. The correct statement is ...",["Both profit and cash are 200,000","Accounting profit is zero because no cash has been received","Accounting profit is 200,000 and the current cash effect is an outflow of 800,000 until collected","Accounting profit is 800,000 and the cash effect is +200,000"],"Accrual basis recognizes revenue when earned, so profit is 200,000, while cash only comes in upon collection."],["The CORRECT cash flow classification is ...",["Sale of old machine: investing","Dividends paid: operating","Proceeds from bank loan: operating","Payments to suppliers: financing"],"Dividends and loans = financing; supplier payments = operating; disposal of fixed assets = investing."],["MiniMart data: opening cash 60, operating cash flow 185, equipment purchases 260, loan proceeds 220, dividends 45. The ending cash balance is ...",["185","100","220","160"],"Change in cash = 185 - 260 + (220 - 45) = +100; ending cash = 60 + 100 = 160."],["According to the material, accounting consists of three main activities, namely ...",["Identify, record, communicate","Budget, forecast, audit","Journalize, post, close","Plan, organize, control"],"Accounting selects relevant economic events (identify), measures and records them chronologically (record), then compiles and explains reports to users (communicate)."],["Which is an internal user of accounting information?",["External auditors","Creditors assessing loan risk","Capital market regulators","Unit managers and project teams who use the information to plan and control"],"Internal users are managers, directors, unit heads, project teams, and budget owners; creditors, regulators, and auditors are external users."],["The correct statement about bookkeeping and accounting is ...",["Bookkeeping and accounting are identical terms","Bookkeeping includes ratio analysis and risk assessment","Accounting only covers daily journal recording","Bookkeeping is the mechanics, while accounting is the information system that turns business activity into reports useful for decisions"],"The material stresses that accounting is not bookkeeping only: bookkeeping is the mechanics of recording, accounting is the information system for decision-useful reports."],["The statement of financial position (balance sheet) reports assets, liabilities, and equity ...",["Over an accounting period","At a specific date","Only at the start of the fiscal year","Based on cash inflows and outflows"],"The balance sheet is a snapshot at a specific date, while the income statement reports performance over a period."],["Current assets are assets expected to be realized, sold, or consumed within ...",["24 months","The economic life of fixed assets","The operating cycle or 12 months","One quarter only"],"Current assets are expected to be realized within the operating cycle or 12 months; examples are cash, receivables, inventory."],["Which accounts are closed to retained earnings through closing entries?",["Cash, receivables, and inventory","Revenues, expenses, and dividends","Share capital and accumulated OCI","Accounts payable and bank loans"],"Closing entries close temporary accounts (revenues, expenses, dividends) to retained earnings; permanent accounts carry on."],["Insurance premiums paid in advance, then moved from asset to expense as they are used, are an example of an adjusting entry of the type ...",["Accrued revenue","Prepaid expense","Deferred revenue","Accrued expense"],"Prepaid expense: insurance paid in advance, moved from asset to expense as used."],["The principle 'incentives matter' in financial decisions relates to ...",["Market prices reflect available information","Higher risk demands higher expected return","A rupiah today is worth more than a rupiah later","Agency problems that arise when managers and owners want different things"],"Incentives matter: agency problems arise when managers and owners want different things. The other options are the principles of time value of money, risk-return tradeoff, and market signals."],["The income statement item that helps users see pricing power and production/purchasing efficiency is ...",["Gross profit","Earnings per share","Income before income tax","Net income"],"Gross profit (revenue − COGS) reflects pricing power and production/purchasing efficiency; net income is the bottom line."],["MiniMart (Rp million): net sales 1,200, COGS 720, selling & administrative 260, finance costs 40. Income before tax is ...",["180","480","135","220"],"Gross profit = 1,200 − 720 = 480; income from operations = 480 − 260 = 220; income before tax = 220 − 40 = 180."],["Which DECREASES equity?",["Gains from other activities","Dividends or distributions to owners","Revenue from normal activities","Owner investment"],"Equity decreases through expenses, losses, and dividends/distributions to owners; owner investment, revenue, and gains increase equity."],["A company buys inventory on credit. The correct transaction analysis is ...",["Accounts Payable increases and Revenue increases","Inventory increases and Equity increases","Inventory increases and Cash decreases","Inventory increases and Accounts Payable increases"],"Buying inventory on credit increases Inventory (debit) and Accounts Payable (credit); there is no cash flow yet."],["In the indirect method, an increase in accounts receivable during the period is treated as ...",["A deduction from net income, because revenue has been recognized but cash has not yet been collected","An addition to net income, because assets increased","An investing cash flow","Not affecting operating cash flow"],"An increase in receivables means revenue not yet collected, so it is deducted from net income to obtain operating cash flow."],["A company reports net income of Rp135 million and other comprehensive income of Rp15 million. Its comprehensive income is ...",["Rp15 million","Rp135 million","Rp150 million","Rp120 million"],"Comprehensive income = Net income + OCI = 135 + 15 = Rp150 million."],["A company receives cash in advance before services are provided. The correct adjusting entry once the services have been provided is ...",["Move the liability (deferred revenue) to revenue","Allocate the cost of fixed assets to expense","Recognize unpaid expenses","Recognize a receivable for services not yet billed"],"Deferred revenue: cash received before services; the liability is moved to revenue when the revenue has been earned."],["Why can a balanced balance sheet still be an incomplete picture of economic value?",["Because the balance sheet only records cash transactions","Because total assets are always smaller than liabilities","Many items are recorded at historical cost, require estimates, and internally built values such as human capital are not recognized","Because equity is always recorded at the market value of shares"],"Limitations of the balance sheet: historical cost, judgment and estimates, and values not recognized because they cannot be measured reliably."],["MiniMart: operating cash flow Rp185 million, capital expenditures Rp260 million, dividends Rp45 million. Free cash flow according to the Week 1 definition is ...",["Rp120 million","−Rp75 million","Rp30 million","−Rp120 million"],"FCF = Operating cash flow − capital expenditures − dividends = 185 − 260 − 45 = −120 (Rp million)."],["A company sells goods on credit. The correct impact on accounts and statements is ...",["Cash and Revenue increase; Inventory unchanged","Receivables and Revenue increase; COGS increases and Inventory decreases; operating cash flow occurs when the receivable is collected","Receivables increase and Equity decreases; recorded as an investing cash flow","Revenue is only recognized when cash is received"],"Per the practice set: Receivables ↑, Revenue ↑; COGS ↑, Inventory ↓; the operating cash impact appears when collected (accrual accounting)."],["Data: net income 200, depreciation 50, increase in receivables 30, increase in accounts payable 20. Using the indirect method, operating cash flow is ...",["300","200","240","260"],"OCF = 200 + 50 (non-cash) − 30 (receivables up) + 20 (accounts payable up) = 240."],["Net cash from operating activities is Rp185 million and average current liabilities are Rp150 million. Current cash debt coverage is ...",["About 0.81x","About 2.23x","About 1.23x","About 1.85x"],"Current cash debt coverage = net cash from operating activities ÷ average current liabilities = 185 ÷ 150 ≈ 1.23x."],["Mini exercise: Sales 950; COGS 570; selling expenses 120; administrative expenses 95; interest expense 25; tax expense 35 (Rp million). Calculate gross profit, income from operations, and net income, and identify operating vs financing items.",0,"Gross profit = 950 - 570 = 380. Income from operations = 380 - 120 - 95 = 165. Net income = 165 - 25 - 35 = 105. Operating: sales, COGS, selling, administrative. Financing: interest expense (tax is calculated separately)."],["Why does a balanced unadjusted trial balance not guarantee correct statements? State the types of adjusting entries and their purpose.",0,"A trial balance only proves debits = credits, not that revenue/expense recognition is accurate. Adjusting entries: accrued revenue, accrued expense, deferred revenue, prepaid expense, and depreciation, to apply accrual and matching."],["Explain how the four financial statements are connected using the MiniMart figures (net income 135, dividends 45, opening retained earnings 210, ending cash 160, total assets 1,200).",0,"Net income of 135 flows into retained earnings: 210 + 135 - 45 = 300. Ending cash of 160 appears in the statement of financial position. Assets of 1,200 = liabilities 600 (payables 180 + loan 420) + equity 600 (share capital 300 + retained earnings 300). All the statements reconcile with one another."],["The main difference between financial reporting and financial statement analysis is ...",["Reporting supplies information; analysis uses information to assess performance and support decisions","They are identical","Reporting is only for auditors; analysis is only for managers","Analysis only calculates ratios"],"Reporting = information supply; analysis = judgment, interpretation, and decision support (for example, capital allocation)."],["Bartlett 2019 data: current assets 1,223 and current liabilities 620 ($000). The current ratio is ...",["3.00x","1.97x","0.51x","2.50x"],"Current ratio = CA / CL = 1,223 / 620 = 1.97x."],["The interest coverage ratio is calculated as ...",["Current assets / Current liabilities","COGS / Average inventory","EBIT / Interest expense","Net income / Equity"],"Interest coverage = EBIT / interest expense, a solvency/leverage ratio."],["Inventory turnover (COGS / average inventory) belongs to the ... ratio category",["Solvency","Liquidity","Activity","Valuation"],"Activity ratios: inventory turnover, average collection period, total asset turnover."],["Revenue grew 19%, receivables grew 38%, and inventory grew 58%. The most relevant explanations to investigate are ...",["Loose credit standards, slowing collections, aggressive revenue recognition, or obsolete inventory","The company must be more efficient","Nothing to investigate because revenue rose","Debt must have fallen"],"Growth in receivables and inventory far above revenue is a red flag that must be traced."],["Three companies have an ROE of 20%: A (margin 10%, turnover 2.0x, leverage 1.0x), B (5%, 2.0x, 2.0x), C (3%, 4.45x, 1.5x). The company that relies most on leverage is ...",["C","A","All are the same","B"],"ROE = margin x turnover x leverage. B has the highest leverage (2.0x), so it is the riskiest."],["Bartlett: net margin 7.5%, asset turnover 0.90x (3,074 / average assets 3,433.5), leverage 1.82x (3,433.5 / average equity 1,887). The estimated ROE is ...",["7.5%","16.9%","12.2%","6.7%"],"ROE = 7.5% x 0.90 x 1.82 = 12.2% (equivalent to 231 / 1,887)."],["The financial statement that is a 'snapshot at one point in time' is ...",["Statement of comprehensive income","Statement of changes in equity","Statement of financial position","Statement of cash flows"],"The statement of financial position captures assets, liabilities, and equity at one point in time; the other statements cover a period."],["The correct statement about the audit report is ...",["It contains accounting policies and debt maturity schedules","It guarantees the company will remain profitable","It gives an independent opinion on whether the statements are fairly presented in accordance with standards, but does not guarantee business success","It is prepared by management to explain strategy"],"The audit report gives an independent opinion on fair presentation; strategy narrative is management commentary, while policies and debt maturities are in the notes."],["The first step in the six-step financial statement analysis framework is ...",["Conclude: draft recommendations","Process: calculate ratios","Data: gather financial evidence","Purpose: define the question and decision"],"The sequence: purpose, data, process, analyze, conclude, follow up."],["The analytical tool used to quantify relationships, for example sales growth as a function of GDP growth for a cyclical company, is ...",["Valuation ratios","Regression","Graphics","Common-size analysis"],"Regression quantifies relationships, for example sales growth as a function of GDP growth for a cyclical company."],["The formula for the debt ratio is ...",["Total debt ÷ Total equity","Current liabilities ÷ Current assets","EBIT ÷ Interest expense","Total liabilities ÷ Total assets"],"Debt ratio = Total liabilities ÷ Total assets; total debt ÷ total equity is debt-to-equity."],["The ratio category that answers the question 'Can long-term obligations be serviced?' is ...",["Valuation","Activity","Liquidity","Solvency"],"Solvency assesses the ability to meet long-term obligations; liquidity assesses short-term obligations."],["The ROA formula according to the material is ...",["Net income ÷ Average assets","Operating income ÷ Sales","Sales ÷ Average total assets","Net income ÷ Average equity"],"ROA = Net income ÷ Average assets; NI ÷ average equity is ROE, sales ÷ average total assets is total asset turnover."],["The P/CF (price to cash flow) ratio is used mainly as an alternative when ...",["The company's sales are negative","The company has no debt","Earnings quality is in doubt","The share price is unavailable"],"P/CF = Price per share ÷ Cash flow per share, used as an alternative when earnings quality is a concern."],["Bartlett 2019 ($000): total liabilities 1,643 and total assets 3,597. Its debt ratio is ...",["45.7%","54.3%","31.6%","84.1%"],"Debt ratio = 1,643 ÷ 3,597 = 45.7%, a moderate use of liabilities to finance assets."],["Bartlett 2019 ($000): gross profit 986 and sales revenue 3,074. Its gross profit margin is ...",["32.1%","27.4%","7.5%","67.9%"],"Gross profit margin = Gross profit ÷ Sales = 986 ÷ 3,074 = 32.1%."],["Bartlett: sales revenue was 2,567 in 2018 and 3,074 in 2019 ($000). Using horizontal common-size, sales growth is ...",["About 16.5%","About 50.7%","About 19.8%","About 83.5%"],"Horizontal = Current period ÷ Base period − 1 = 3,074 ÷ 2,567 − 1 ≈ 19.8%."],["The share price is $30 and EPS is $2. The P/E ratio is ...",["0.07x","15x","60x","32x"],"P/E = Price per share ÷ EPS = 30 ÷ 2 = 15x; it must still be interpreted with attention to earnings quality and growth expectations."],["A company's EBIT is $500 and interest expense is $125. Its interest coverage is ...",["3.75x","0.25x","4.0x","5.0x"],"Interest coverage = EBIT ÷ Interest expense = 500 ÷ 125 = 4.0x."],["Accounts receivable is $300 and average daily sales are $10. The average collection period is ...",["3 days","30 days","10 days","300 days"],"Average collection period = Accounts receivable ÷ Average daily sales = 300 ÷ 10 = 30 days."],["Profit margin 5%, asset turnover 1.6x, and financial leverage 2.5x. ROE based on DuPont is ...",["9.1%","12.5%","8%","20%"],"ROE = 5% × 1.6 × 2.5 = 20%."],["Beginning cash 100, CFO 250, CFI −180, and CFF −40. Ending cash is ...",["130","−30","210","570"],"Ending cash = Beginning cash + CFO + CFI + CFF = 100 + 250 − 180 − 40 = 130."],["Bartlett: net income 231, total assets 3,597 (2019) and 3,270 (2018). ROA based on average assets is ...",["About 7.1%","About 6.7%","About 7.5%","About 6.4%"],"Average assets = (3,597 + 3,270) ÷ 2 = 3,433.5; ROA = 231 ÷ 3,433.5 ≈ 6.7%. The figure 6.4% appears if year-end assets are used."],["A company's asset turnover has declined. The diagnosis and follow-up most consistent with the material is ...",["Surely a performance improvement; no follow-up needed","Assets are not generating enough revenue, possibly inventory build-up or slowing receivables; review inventory aging, collection policy, and capex plans","Growth was financed by debt; review covenants and maturity schedules","Price pressure; review supplier contracts and segment margins"],"Declining turnover is associated with less productive assets, inventory buildup, or receivable slowdown; the other options are diagnoses for falling margins and rising leverage."],["A company has a very high current ratio (for example 4.0). The most appropriate interpretation according to the material is ...",["It shows ROE must be high","It is always better than a current ratio of 1.5 without needing further information","It means the company surely cannot pay its long-term debt","Short-term safety is high, but excess cash or inventory may signal inefficient use of capital"],"High liquidity usually means better short-term safety, but too much cash or inventory may imply inefficient capital use; additional information is needed."],["A company's ROE rises from 12% to 18%, while profit margin and asset turnover are unchanged. If the initial leverage was 1.5x, the new leverage is ...",["2.25x","3.0x","1.5x","1.8x"],"Margin × turnover = 12% ÷ 1.5 = 8%; new leverage = 18% ÷ 8% = 2.25x. The ROE increase comes entirely from leverage, so risk rises."],["Explain vertical and horizontal common-size analysis along with their formulas and uses.",0,"Vertical: statement items as a percentage of a base (balance sheet to total assets; income statement to revenue) = item / base. Horizontal: change relative to a base period = current period / base period - 1. Useful for comparing companies of different sizes and spotting unusual changes."],["Can a company increase ROE while becoming riskier? Explain how DuPont analysis reveals it.",0,"Yes, if the ROE increase comes from higher leverage (rising debt) without improvement in margin or turnover. DuPont breaks ROE = margin x turnover x leverage so the source of the increase is visible. A high ROE is not automatically good if driven by excessive leverage."],["Revenue rose 19%, receivables 38%, inventory 58%. Interpret this signal, state possible causes, and the additional evidence you would request from management.",0,"Signal: asset efficiency and earnings quality may be deteriorating. Causes: loose credit, slowing collections, aggressive revenue recognition, channel stuffing, obsolete inventory, or stockpiling ahead of rising demand. Follow-up evidence: receivables aging, credit policy, notes to the financial statements, inventory aging, operating cash flow, peer comparison."],["Baker Corporation buys a machine for $38,000 with installation costs of $2,000 and an estimated salvage value of $1,000. The depreciable value of the machine under MACRS is ...",["$40,000","$39,000","$38,000","$37,000"],"Depreciable value = full cost including installation ($38,000 + $2,000 = $40,000), without taking salvage value into account."],["Which of the following is a cash OUTFLOW (use of cash) on the statement of cash flows?",["Depreciation","Increase in accounts payable","Increase in assets (for example receivables)","Decrease in inventory"],"Increase in assets = outflow; decrease in assets, increase in liabilities, net income, and noncash expenses = inflow."],["The operating cash flow (OCF) formula in the material is ...",["NOPAT + Depreciation","EBIT + Interest","Sales - COGS","Net income - Depreciation"],"OCF = NOPAT + Depreciation = EBIT x (1 - T) + Depreciation."],["Baker Corporation: EBIT $370,000, tax rate 21%, depreciation $100,000. Its OCF is ...",["$392,300","$292,300","$370,000","$470,000"],"NOPAT = 370 x 0.79 = 292.3; OCF = 292.3 + 100 = 392.3 ($000)."],["Coulson Industries: sales Sept $200, Oct $400, Nov $300 (thousand). Cash sales 20%, collected after 1 month 50%, collected after 2 months 30%. Total cash receipts in November are ...",["$340,000","$210,000","$320,000","$418,000"],"Nov: cash sales 60 + 50% x Oct 400 = 200 + 30% x Sept 200 = 60, total 320 ($000)."],["Coulson: opening cash balance in November $47,000, net cash flow -$98,000, minimum cash balance $25,000. Required total financing in November is ...",["$98,000","$25,000","$76,000","$51,000"],"Ending cash = 47 - 98 = -51. To reach the minimum of 25, financing of 25 + 51 = 76 ($000) is needed."],["Baker: OCF $392.3; net fixed asset investment $300; net current asset investment $57 (change in current assets 157 minus change in A/P + accruals 100). Free cash flow and its interpretation are ...",["FCF $35.3; enough to cover operating costs and investment but smaller than the $70 interest expense, so investor returns are not yet adequate","FCF $392.3; very healthy","FCF -$35.3; the company is losing cash","FCF $57; equal to net current asset investment"],"FCF = 392.3 - 300 - 57 = 35.3. Because 2019 interest was 70, FCF is not enough to provide an adequate return to investors."],["In MACRS, the 3-year property class includes ...",["Office furniture and most manufacturing equipment","Office buildings","Computers and light vehicles","Research equipment and certain special tools"],"Table 4.1: 3 years = research equipment and certain special tools; computers and cars fall in 5 years; office furniture falls in 7 years."],["Office furniture and most manufacturing equipment belong to MACRS property class ...",["7 years","10 years","3 years","5 years"],"Table 4.1 places office furniture, fixtures, and most manufacturing equipment in the 7-year recovery period."],["An example of a noncash charge according to the material is ...",["Tax payments","Purchasing inventory on credit","Depreciation, amortization, and depletion","Cash dividends and interest"],"A noncash charge is an expense deducted on the income statement without an actual cash outlay in that period: depreciation, amortization, depletion."],["Which is a cash flow from financing activities?",["Sale of products to customers","Repurchase of shares","Sale of fixed assets","Purchase of equity investments in other companies"],"Financing includes receipts and repayments of debt, sale of shares, share repurchases, and dividends; fixed assets and equity investments fall under investing activities."],["Pro forma statements are ...",["Daily cash flow reports","Projected (forecast) income statements and balance sheets","Audited historical financial statements","The company's tax return"],"Pro forma statements = projected, or forecast, income statements and balance sheets, with the prior year's statements as the starting point and the sales forecast as the key input."],["In a cash budget, the excess cash balance is assumed to be ...",["Invested in marketable securities","Added to the minimum cash balance","Kept as notes payable","Used to pay dividends"],"The excess cash balance is the surplus above the minimum cash balance, assumed to be invested in marketable securities."],["The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 changed the top US corporate tax rate from 35% to ...",["Flat 21%","Flat 25%","Flat 15%","Progressive 21% to 35%"],"According to the material, the top marginal corporate tax rate fell from 35% to a flat 21%."],["Ending cash in a cash budget is defined as ...",["Beginning cash plus the net cash flow for that period","Net cash flow minus required financing","Total cash receipts minus the minimum cash balance","Total cash disbursements plus beginning cash"],"Ending cash = the sum of beginning cash and the net cash flow for that period; net cash flow = receipts − disbursements."],["An asset costing $100,000 falls into the MACRS 7-year class. First-year depreciation is ...",["$33,000","$14,000","$10,000","$20,000"],"Table 4.2: year 1 of the 7-year class = 14%; $100,000 × 14% = $14,000."],["Coulson Industries: sales Oct $400, Nov $300, Dec $200 ($000); 20% cash, 50% collected after 1 month, 30% collected after 2 months; there is $30 of dividends received in December. Total cash receipts for December are ...",["$310,000","$290,000","$370,000","$340,000"],"Dec = cash sales 0.2 × 200 = 40 + 0.5 × Nov 300 = 150 + 0.3 × Oct 400 = 120 + other 30 = 340 ($000)."],["Coulson: purchases = 70% of sales; 10% paid in cash, 70% paid 1 month later, 20% paid 2 months later. Purchases August $70, September $140, October $280 ($000). Total payments for purchases in October are ...",["$213,000","$126,000","$154,000","$140,000"],"Oct = cash 0.1 × 280 = 28 + 0.7 × Sept 140 = 98 + 0.2 × Aug 70 = 14; total 140 ($000)."],["Coulson has a fixed salary of $8,000 per month plus wages of 10% of monthly sales. If November sales are $300,000, November wages and salaries are ...",["$30,000","$38,000","$48,000","$8,300"],"Wages and salaries = 8,000 + 10% × 300,000 = $38,000."],["Fair Traders uses the percent-of-sales method with COGS at 80% of sales. If projected 2020 sales are $135,000, pro forma COGS is ...",["$100,000","$80,000","$108,000","$27,000"],"COGS 2019 ÷ sales = 80,000 ÷ 100,000 = 80%; pro forma COGS = 0.80 × 135,000 = $108,000."],["Fair Traders assumes accounts receivable average about 45 days of sales (about 1/8 of a year). With projected sales of $135,000, pro forma accounts receivable is ...",["$33,750","$16,875","$13,000","$11,250"],"AR = 1/8 × 135,000 = $16,875."],["Baker's inventory fell from $900 to $600 ($000). How is it treated in the statement of cash flows?",["Not recorded because it is not a cash transaction","An outflow of $300 in operating activities","An inflow of $300 in investing activities","An inflow of $300 in cash flow from operating activities"],"A decrease in assets is a cash inflow; Baker records 'Decrease in inventories 300' under operating activities."],["Why is the change in retained earnings not shown directly in the financing section of Baker's statement of cash flows?",["Because the change is already reflected in the net profit after taxes and dividends paid items","Because retained earnings is a current asset","Because retained earnings is a noncash charge","Because retained earnings is always constant"],"Note to Table 4.6: retained earnings is excluded because its change is already reflected in the combination of net profit after taxes and dividends paid."],["Data: EBIT $500, tax rate 21%, depreciation $80, change in net fixed assets $120, change in current assets $60, change in (accounts payable + accruals) $25. Free cash flow is ...",["$275","$475","$320","$240"],"OCF = 500 × 0.79 + 80 = 475; NFAI = 120 + 80 = 200; NCAI = 60 − 25 = 35; FCF = 475 − 200 − 35 = 240."],["Fair Traders separates costs: fixed COGS $40,000 + variable 0.40 × sales; operating expenses fixed $5,000 + variable 0.05 × sales; fixed interest $1,000. If sales are $150,000, net profit before taxes is ...",["$28,250","$45,000","$36,500","$13,500"],"Gross profit = 150,000 − (40,000 + 60,000) = 50,000; operating profit = 50,000 − (5,000 + 7,500) = 37,500; less interest 1,000 = 36,500."],["Fair Traders: purchases are about 30% of sales of $135,000 and accounts payable are paid on average in 73 days. Pro forma accounts payable is ...",["$27,000","$40,500","$8,100","$7,000"],"Purchases = 0.30 × 135,000 = 40,500; 73 ÷ 365 = 1/5; AP = 1/5 × 40,500 = $8,100."],["Fair Traders 2020 pro forma balance sheet: total assets $105,875; total liabilities $38,255; common stock $30,000; retained earnings $29,327. External financing required (plug) is ...",["$6,327","$37,582","$8,293","$15,875"],"Total liabilities + equity before the plug = 38,255 + 30,000 + 29,327 = 97,582; plug = 105,875 − 97,582 = $8,293."],["Explain the components and steps of preparing a cash budget, and the difference between an external forecast and an internal forecast in a sales forecast.",0,"The sales forecast is the key input. External forecast: based on the relationship between sales and external economic indicators; internal forecast: a consensus/buildup from internal sales channels. Cash budget: total cash receipts, total cash disbursements, net cash flow, ending cash, then compare with the minimum balance to determine required financing or excess cash balance."],["In Coulson's scenario analysis, the pessimistic peak financing need of $190,000 is far above the most likely estimate of $76,000. Explain the benefits of scenario analysis and your recommendations for the financial manager, including the limitations of a monthly cash budget.",0,"Scenario analysis (pessimistic, most likely, optimistic) shows the range of risk in cash needs. Recommendations: arrange a loan facility/line of credit sized to the peak need, keep a cash reserve, and monitor daily cash patterns because a monthly cash budget does not guarantee solvency within the month."],["Fair Traders projects 2020 sales of $135,000. With percent-of-sales (all costs variable) profit before tax is $12,150, but with fixed/variable cost separation it is $28,250. Explain the difference and why the second approach is more accurate.",0,"Percent-of-sales assumes all costs are variable so the margin stays at 9% and understates profit when sales rise. Cost separation: fixed COGS 40,000 + variable 0.40 x sales; fixed operating expenses 5,000 + 0.05 x sales; fixed interest 1,000, so operating leverage is reflected, with profit before tax of 28,250."],["Prime cost is defined as ...",["Direct labor + Manufacturing overhead","Direct materials + Direct labor","Direct materials + Manufacturing overhead","Selling + Administrative costs"],"Prime cost = DM + DL (direct inputs); conversion cost = DL + manufacturing overhead."],["Which of the following is a period cost?",["Factory supervisor's salary","Depreciation of factory machinery","Sales commissions","Direct materials"],"Manufacturing costs are product costs; selling and administrative costs (including sales commissions) are period costs expensed immediately."],["The correct equation for a mixed cost is ...",["Y = aX - b","Y = a / bX","Y = a x b","Y = a + bX"],"Y = total mixed cost, a = fixed cost, b = variable cost per unit of activity, X = activity level."],["A beverage manufacturer continuously produces the same bottled tea. The most suitable costing system is ...",["Process costing","Differential costing","Operation costing","Job-order costing"],"Homogeneous products with continuous production are costed with process costing by department."],["Estimated total manufacturing overhead is $480,000 and estimated machine hours are 120,000. Job X uses 35 machine hours. The overhead applied to Job X is ...",["$140","$35","$480","$4,000"],"POHR = 480,000 / 120,000 = $4 per MH; applied overhead = 35 x $4 = $140."],["High-low method: highest activity is 10,000 hours with a cost of $50,000, lowest activity is 4,000 hours with a cost of $32,000. The variable cost per hour is ...",["$3","$1","$5","$8"],"Variable cost per unit = change in cost / change in activity = 18,000 / 6,000 = $3."],["A department has 5,000 units transferred out and 800 units of ending WIP (material 60%, conversion 25%). Equivalent units of production for material and conversion are, respectively, ...",["5,000 and 5,000","5,480 and 5,200","480 and 200","5,800 and 5,800"],"EUP = units transferred out + EU of ending WIP. Material 5,000 + 480 = 5,480; conversion 5,000 + 200 = 5,200."],["Direct labor is often also called ...",["Overhead labor","Touch labor","Indirect labor","Conversion labor"],"Direct labor is labor cost that is easily traced to units of product, often called 'touch labor'."],["Which is a selling cost?",["Depreciation of production machinery","Factory supervisor's salary","Finished goods warehouse costs","Raw materials that become part of the product"],"Selling costs include advertising, sales commissions, shipping, sales travel, and finished goods warehouse costs."],["The salary of head-office accounting staff is classified as ...",["A selling cost that is inventoried","Direct labor","Manufacturing overhead","An administrative cost (period cost)"],"Administrative costs include executive, organizational, and clerical costs such as accounting; they are period costs expensed immediately."],["How does variable cost per unit behave within the relevant range?",["Constant per unit","Decreases as activity rises","Zero when activity is low","Increases as activity rises"],"Total variable cost changes in proportion to activity, but per unit it is constant; it is fixed cost per unit that falls as activity rises."],["The situation most suited to job-order costing is ...",["A construction project to a client's order","A cement factory","A paper producer","An oil refinery"],"Job-order costing is for products made to order (aircraft, construction, consulting, film); refineries, cement, and paper are examples of process costing."],["The source document for recording direct labor on a job cost sheet is ...",["Materials requisition form","Employee time ticket","Department production report","Sales invoice"],"Direct labor is recorded from the employee time ticket; direct materials from the materials requisition form."],["The cost that is a potential benefit given up when one alternative is chosen, and is often not recorded in the accounting system, is called ...",["Period cost","Opportunity cost","Differential cost","Sunk cost"],"Opportunity cost is the potential benefit given up when one alternative is chosen; often not recorded in the accounting system."],["The mixed-cost estimation method that uses all data points to fit a line by minimizing squared errors is ...",["Job-order costing","Least-squares regression","Scattergraph","High-low method"],"Least-squares regression uses all the data; high-low uses only two points; a scattergraph is visual and subjective."],["Factory electricity used jointly by many products is most appropriately classified as ...",["Direct materials","Direct labor","Selling cost","An indirect cost that goes into manufacturing overhead"],"A cost that cannot easily be traced to a single cost object is an indirect cost; factory utilities are part of manufacturing overhead."],["The journal entry for the use of indirect materials in job-order costing is ...",["Debit Raw Materials, credit Accounts Payable","Debit Manufacturing Overhead, credit Raw Materials","Debit Finished Goods, credit Work in Process","Debit Work in Process, credit Raw Materials"],"Indirect materials are collected in Manufacturing Overhead (Dr MOH, Cr Raw Materials); direct materials go straight to WIP."],["Beginning raw materials $20,000, purchases $80,000, ending raw materials $15,000. Raw materials used is ...",["$85,000","$65,000","$75,000","$115,000"],"Raw materials used = 20,000 + 80,000 − 15,000 = $85,000."],["Total manufacturing costs $300,000, beginning WIP $40,000, ending WIP $55,000. Cost of goods manufactured (COGM) is ...",["$395,000","$260,000","$285,000","$315,000"],"COGM = Total manufacturing costs + Beginning WIP − Ending WIP = 300,000 + 40,000 − 55,000 = $285,000."],["Beginning finished goods $50,000, COGM $285,000, ending finished goods $35,000. Unadjusted COGS is ...",["$285,000","$370,000","$300,000","$270,000"],"Unadjusted COGS = 50,000 + 285,000 − 35,000 = $300,000."],["Why can average unit product cost be misleading in pricing or special-order decisions?",["Because unit product cost does not include direct materials","Because the job cost sheet does not record overhead","Because unit product cost is always lower than incremental cost","Because average cost is not the cost of making one additional unit; fixed overhead may not change for one more unit"],"Unit product cost = total job cost ÷ units completed is an average, not incremental cost, because fixed overhead does not necessarily change."],["3,000 units in ending WIP are only 40% complete. Their equivalent units are ...",["7,500 units","1,800 units","3,000 units","1,200 units"],"Equivalent units = partially completed units × percentage completion = 3,000 × 40% = 1,200."],["Direct materials $50,000, direct labor $30,000, manufacturing overhead $45,000. Conversion cost is ...",["$80,000","$95,000","$75,000","$125,000"],"Conversion cost = DL + MOH = 30,000 + 45,000 = $75,000 (prime cost = DM + DL = $80,000)."],["Process costing: materials cost (beginning WIP $12,000 + added $108,000) with 6,000 EU; conversion cost (beginning WIP $9,000 + added $81,000) with 5,000 EU. Total cost per equivalent unit is ...",["$38","$35","$18","$20"],"Materials CPEU = 120,000 ÷ 6,000 = $20; conversion CPEU = 90,000 ÷ 5,000 = $18; total = $38."],["Estimated fixed MOH $300,000, variable MOH $2 per machine hour, estimated 100,000 MH. Job Z uses 40 MH. Applied overhead for Job Z is ...",["$80","$120","$500","$200"],"Estimated MOH = 300,000 + 2 × 100,000 = 500,000; POHR = 500,000 ÷ 100,000 = $5/MH; applied = 40 × 5 = $200."],["Cost per EU: materials $20, conversion $18. Ending WIP is 500 units, 100% complete for materials and 40% for conversion. The cost of ending WIP is ...",["$10,000","$7,600","$13,600","$19,000"],"Materials EU = 500 → 500 × 20 = 10,000; conversion EU = 200 → 200 × 18 = 3,600; total $13,600."],["Unadjusted COGS is $300,000 and overhead is overapplied by $8,000. Adjusted COGS is ...",["$292,000","$308,000","$8,000","$300,000"],"Adjusted COGS = Unadjusted COGS ± under/overapplied overhead; overapplied means the overhead applied was too large so COGS is reduced: 300,000 − 8,000 = $292,000."],["Why is overhead applied using a predetermined overhead rate (POHR) rather than traced directly? Write the formula and an example.",0,"Reasons: overhead is hard to trace to a specific job, consists of many items, and actual costs are only known at the end of the period and fluctuate. POHR = estimated total MOH / estimated total allocation base; applied overhead = POHR x actual base. Example: $640,000 / 160,000 DLH = $4; Job A-143 uses 8 DLH so overhead is $32."],["Choose the appropriate costing system and explain why: (A) an advertising agency designing a campaign for a single client, (B) a continuous bottled-tea producer, (C) a factory with a common process followed by packaging customization for each customer.",0,"A: job-order costing (unique, custom, the job as the cost object). B: process costing (homogeneous, continuous, cost per department with equivalent units). C: operation costing (hybrid: part common process, part customization)."],["Explain the cost flow in job-order costing from Raw Materials to Cost of Goods Sold, including the main journal entries and the COGM formula.",0,"Raw Materials -> WIP (direct material: Dr WIP, Cr Raw Materials; direct labor: Dr WIP, Cr Wages Payable; overhead applied: Dr WIP, Cr Manufacturing Overhead) -> Finished Goods when the job is completed (COGM) -> COGS when sold. COGM = total manufacturing costs + beginning WIP - ending WIP. The overhead variance is applied to COGS (adjusted COGS)."],["Racing Bicycle Company sells bicycles at $500/unit with variable costs of $300/unit. Its contribution margin ratio is ...",["60%","40%","20%","30%"],"Unit CM = 500 - 300 = 200; CM ratio = 200 / 500 = 40%."],["Java Roasters: price $45, variable cost $27, annual fixed costs $162,000. The break-even point in units is ...",["3,600 boxes","9,000 boxes","6,000 boxes","14,000 boxes"],"Unit CM = $18; break-even = 162,000 / 18 = 9,000 boxes (equivalent to sales of $405,000)."],["In the ABC activity hierarchy, 'machine setup' and 'shipping customer orders' belong to the ... level",["Product-level","Batch-level","Unit-level","Organization-sustaining"],"Batch-level activities occur each time a batch is handled, for example machine setup and shipping orders."],["Java Roasters targets an operating profit of $90,000. The number of boxes that must be sold is ...",["18,000","12,000","9,000","14,000"],"Q = (Target profit + Fixed expenses) / Unit CM = (90,000 + 162,000) / 18 = 14,000 boxes."],["Java Roasters sells 12,000 boxes and its break-even is 9,000 boxes. The margin of safety as a percentage is ...",["15%","33%","75%","25%"],"MOS = 12,000 - 9,000 = 3,000 boxes; MOS% = 3,000 / 12,000 = 25%."],["At 12,000 boxes, total CM is $216,000 and NOI is $54,000 (DOL = 4.0). If sales rise 15%, the new NOI is ...",["$62,100","$86,400","$72,000","$108,000"],"%ΔNOI = DOL x %ΔSales = 4.0 x 15% = 60%; NOI = 54,000 x 1.60 = 86,400 (verified: 13,800 x 18 - 162,000 = 86,400)."],["Meridian Furniture: Custom Desks uses 600 of 800 setups (75%) but only 6,000 of 30,000 machine-hours (20%). With a single plantwide rate based on machine-hours ($29/MH), Custom Desks is charged $174,000, whereas ABC gives $330,000. The correct conclusion is ...",["Custom Desks is overcosted because it uses few MH","Custom Desks is undercosted because the MH rate ignores frequent setup activity and small batches","There is no meaningful difference","Standard Chairs is undercosted"],"The traditional MH-based system ignores the consumption of setup and order activities by custom products; Standard Chairs becomes overcosted ($696,000 vs $540,000)."],["Contribution margin is essentially ...",["Net income after tax","Total variable costs per period","The difference between sales and fixed costs","The amount available to cover fixed costs and then generate profit"],"CM = sales − variable expenses; CM covers fixed costs first, then becomes profit."],["On the Racing Bicycle CVP graph, the intersection of the total sales revenue line and the total expenses line is at ...",["200 bicycles or $100,000 sales","900 bicycles or $450,000 sales","400 bicycles or $200,000 sales","160 bicycles or $80,000 sales"],"The intersection is the break-even point: 400 bicycles, $200,000 sales; 900 bicycles is the $100,000 target profit point."],["A company with a high fixed-cost structure tends to have ...",["More stable profit","A lower degree of operating leverage","More volatile net operating income","An always lower break-even point"],"High fixed-cost structure: higher profit in good years, lower in bad years, more volatile NOI."],["In the ABC activity hierarchy, product design and product advertising are ...",["Customer-level activity","Product-level activity","Unit-level activity","Batch-level activity"],"Product-level activities are performed to support specific products, for example product design and advertising."],["Sales calls and catalog mailings in ABC are ...",["Customer-level activity","Unit-level activity","Organization-sustaining activity","Product-level activity"],"Customer-level activities support specific customers, for example sales calls and catalog mailings."],["The relationship between ABC and the formal external reporting system according to the material is ...",["ABC is a GAAP standard","ABC is usually a supplement, not a replacement","ABC is used only for tax reports","ABC must replace the external reporting system"],"ABC is usually a supplement, not a replacement, for the formal external reporting system."],["The cost flow in ABC is ...",["Resources → cost objects → activities","Cost objects → activities → resources","Resources → activities → cost objects","Activities → resources → cost objects"],"ABC traces resource costs to activities, then to cost objects according to activity consumption."],["Which is NOT one of the five components that CVP analysis links?",["Sales mix","Income tax rate","Fixed cost","Selling price"],"The five CVP components: selling price, sales volume, variable cost, fixed cost, and sales mix."],["A product sells for $80 per unit, variable cost is $56 per unit, fixed costs are $120,000. Break-even sales in dollars are ...",["$171,429","$5,000","$300,000","$400,000"],"Unit CM = 24; CM ratio = 24 ÷ 80 = 30%; break-even sales = 120,000 ÷ 0.30 = $400,000 (5,000 units)."],["With a unit CM of $24 and fixed costs of $120,000, how many units must be sold to reach a target profit of $60,000?",["2,500 units","5,000 units","7,500 units","10,000 units"],"Q = (60,000 + 120,000) ÷ 24 = 7,500 units."],["Budgeted sales are $500,000 and break-even sales are $400,000. The margin of safety as a percentage is ...",["20%","25%","80%","125%"],"MOS = 500,000 − 400,000 = 100,000; MOS % = 100,000 ÷ 500,000 = 20%."],["High fixed-cost structure: sales $400,000, CM ratio 40%, fixed costs $120,000 (DOL 4.0). If sales fall 20%, net operating income becomes ...",["$32,000","$8,000","$20,000","$40,000"],"Initial NOI = 160,000 − 120,000 = 40,000; %ΔNOI = 4.0 × (−20%) = −80%; NOI = 40,000 × 0.2 = 8,000 (check: 0.4 × 320,000 − 120,000 = 8,000)."],["Classic Brass: the activity cost pool 'Order size' is $380,000 with 20,000 machine-hours. The activity rate is ...",["$52.63 per MH","$320 per MH","$1.90 per MH","$19 per MH"],"Activity rate = 380,000 ÷ 20,000 MH = $19 per machine-hour."],["The main benefit of ABC-based customer profitability analysis according to the material is ...",["Identifying customers who consume many service activities, so customers with equal sales are not assumed to be equally profitable","Eliminating all customer fixed costs","Raising prices to all customers uniformly","Replacing external income statements"],"The material: identify customers with high service consumption, redesign services/prices/minimum orders, and avoid treating all customers with equal sales as equally profitable."],["One CVP assumption for a manufacturing company is ...",["Units produced equal units sold, so inventory does not change","Selling price rises with volume","Sales mix always changes","All costs are fixed"],"CVP assumptions: constant price, linear and separable costs, constant sales mix, and units produced equal units sold."],["Why might a company that uses ABC still need two costing systems?",["Because ABC eliminates all overhead","Because ABC does not necessarily conform to GAAP for external reporting","Because ABC is only for service companies","Because ABC cannot calculate product costs"],"One limitation of ABC: it does not necessarily conform to GAAP; two costing systems may be needed."],["Classic Brass: Standard Stanchions has 600 orders ($320/order), 0 designs ($630/design), and 17,500 MH ($19/MH). The ABC overhead applied is ...",["$524,500","$332,500","$615,750","$427,500"],"600 × 320 = 192,000; 0 × 630 = 0; 17,500 × 19 = 332,500; total = $524,500."],["Two products: A with CM $30/unit and B with CM $15/unit, sales mix 1A : 2B, fixed costs $240,000. Break-even in units is ...",["8,000 units of A and 4,000 units of B","8,000 units of A and 16,000 units of B","5,333 units of A and 10,667 units of B","4,000 units of A and 8,000 units of B"],"CM per bundle = 1 × 30 + 2 × 15 = 60; break-even = 240,000 ÷ 60 = 4,000 bundles = 4,000 A and 8,000 B."],["Setup activity pool $60,000 for 300 setups, and inspection $40,000 for 500 inspections. Product X uses 50 setups and 100 inspections. The ABC overhead for Product X is ...",["$20,000","$16,667","$10,000","$18,000"],"Setup rate = 60,000 ÷ 300 = $200; inspection rate = 40,000 ÷ 500 = $80; Product X = 50 × 200 + 100 × 80 = 10,000 + 8,000 = $18,000."],["Contribution margin is $300,000 and net operating income is $60,000. If sales rise 10%, the new NOI is estimated at ...",["$66,000","$75,000","$90,000","$330,000"],"DOL = 300,000 ÷ 60,000 = 5; %ΔNOI = 5 × 10% = 50%; new NOI = 60,000 × 1.5 = $90,000."],["Explain break-even for a multiproduct company using sales mix. Use an example: CM of Product A $20, Product B $10, bundle 2A:3B, fixed cost $350,000. What is the effect of a mix shift toward the low-margin product?",0,"CM per bundle of 5 units = 2 x 20 + 3 x 10 = 70. Break-even bundles = 350,000 / 70 = 5,000 bundles = 10,000 units of A and 15,000 units of B (25,000 units). If the mix shifts toward the low-margin product, the weighted-average CM falls and break-even rises even though each product's price/cost does not change."],["Classic Brass: ABC changes the margin of Standard Stanchions from $615,750 to $906,250 and of Custom Compass Housings from $258,000 to ($49,500). Explain the cause and state the limitations of ABC.",0,"The custom product uses only 2,500 MH but absorbs all the product design activity (400 designs x $630 = $252,000) and 400 orders. The traditional (volume-based) system charges too much to the standard product. Limitations: requires large resources, resistance, risk of misreading costs as relevant for all decisions, does not necessarily conform to GAAP, subjective allocations."],["Blue Harbor Fittings: sales $9,800; DM + DL + shipping $3,850; 15 orders, 3 designs, 40 MH, 1 customer (rates $320/order, $630/design, $19/MH, $1,470/customer). Calculate the customer margin and propose at least two actions.",0,"Activity costs: 15 x 320 = 4,800; 3 x 630 = 1,890; 40 x 19 = 760; 1 x 1,470 = 1,470; total 8,920. Total cost = 3,850 + 8,920 = 12,770; margin = 9,800 - 12,770 = -2,970 (a loss). Actions: reprice or set a minimum order, bill special designs separately, reduce services, or evaluate whether to continue the relationship."],["The time value of money refers to the observation that ...",["Inflation is always zero","Money does not change in value over time","Receiving money sooner is better than later","Interest is only paid on the initial principal"],"The basic principle: it is better to receive money now than later because it can be invested."],["All else equal, the relationship of discount rate and waiting time to present value is ...",["A higher discount rate and longer waiting time reduce PV","A higher discount rate raises PV","A longer waiting time raises PV","Neither has any effect"],"The higher the discount rate and the longer the wait, the lower the present value."],["Fred Moreno saves $100 at 8% interest compounded annually. The value after 2 years is ...",["$118.00","$116.00","$116.64","$120.00"],"FV = 100 x (1.08)^2 = $116.64 (compound interest: interest on interest)."],["The difference between an ordinary annuity and an annuity due is ...",["Ordinary annuity: cash flows at the end of the period; annuity due: at the start of the period, so its value is always larger","Annuity due: cash flows at the end of the period","They are always equal in value","Ordinary annuity is worth more"],"An annuity due has earlier cash flows so both its FV and PV are always larger than an identical ordinary annuity."],["Pam Valenti will receive $1,700 eight years from now and requires a 4% return. The maximum value worth paying now is ...",["$1,700.00","$1,600.00","$1,242.17","$2,326.60"],"PV = 1,700 / (1.04)^8 = 1,700 / 1.36857 = $1,242.17."],["Fran Abrams deposits $1,000 at the end of each year for 5 years at 7% interest. The future value of the ordinary annuity is ...",["$4,100.20","$6,153.29","$5,000.00","$5,750.74"],"FV = 1,000 x [(1.07)^5 - 1] / 0.07 = $5,750.74."],["If Fran chooses an annuity due ($1,000 at the start of each year, 5 years, 7%), the future value is ...",["$5,750.74","$6,153.29","$6,500.00","$5,000.00"],"FV of annuity due = FV of ordinary annuity x (1 + r) = 5,750.74 x 1.07 = $6,153.29."],["A timeline in the time value of money is ...",["A MACRS rate table","A vertical line with the largest cash flow at the top","A horizontal line with time zero at the far left and future periods to the right","A chart of compound interest against inflation"],"Timeline: a horizontal line, time zero at the far left, future periods marked from left to right."],["On a financial calculator or spreadsheet, a cash outflow is entered as ...",["A percentage","A negative value","A positive value","A zero value"],"Cash inflows are entered as positive values and cash outflows as negative values."],["A stream of cash flows that does not follow an annuity pattern is called ...",["A perpetuity","A mixed stream","An ordinary annuity","A single amount"],"A mixed stream is a stream of cash flows that is not an annuity."],["Interest calculated only on the initial principal, not on accumulated interest, is called ...",["Discount rate","Annuity due","Compound interest","Simple interest"],"Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal; compound interest adds interest to the principal."],["Principal in the context of the time value of money is ...",["The annual interest rate","The number of investment periods","The amount of money on which interest is paid","The value of money in the future"],"Principal = the amount of money on which interest is paid."],["All else equal, how does future value change?",["The higher the interest rate and the longer the investment period, the larger the FV","The longer the period, the smaller the FV","The higher the interest rate, the smaller the FV","FV is not affected by the interest rate"],"Figure 5.4: a higher interest rate and a longer investment period produce a higher future value."],["A growing perpetuity is ...",["A finite-lived annuity with fixed cash flows","An annuity with cash flows at the start of the period","An infinite-lived annuity with cash flows that grow at a constant rate each year","A single amount received in the future"],"Growing perpetuity: infinite life with cash flows that grow at a constant annual rate; PV0 = CF1 ÷ (r − g)."],["The correct statement about annuity cash flows is ...",["The cash flows always grow each period","The cash flows occur only once","The cash flows are equal and periodic, and can be inflows or outflows","The cash flows are always inflows"],"Annuity: a stream of equal periodic cash flows; these cash flows can be inflows or outflows."],["You save $2,000 at 6% interest compounded annually for 3 years. The future value is ...",["$2,360.00","$2,382.03","$1,679.24","$2,120.00"],"FV = 2,000 × (1.06)^3 = 2,000 × 1.191016 = $2,382.03 ($2,360 is simple interest)."],["The present value of $5,000 received 5 years from now at a discount rate of 8% is ...",["$3,000.00","$7,346.64","$4,629.63","$3,402.92"],"PV = 5,000 ÷ (1.08)^5 = 5,000 ÷ 1.469328 = $3,402.92."],["Deposits of $500 at the end of each year for 4 years at 5% interest. The future value of the ordinary annuity is ...",["$2,000.00","$1,772.98","$2,262.82","$2,155.06"],"FV = 500 × [(1.05)^4 − 1] ÷ 0.05 = 500 × 4.310125 = $2,155.06 (an annuity due would be $2,262.82)."],["The present value of an ordinary annuity of $2,000 per year for 3 years at a discount rate of 10% is ...",["$6,620.00","$4,973.70","$5,471.07","$6,000.00"],"PV = (2,000 ÷ 0.10) × [1 − 1 ÷ (1.10)^3] = 20,000 × 0.248685 = $4,973.70."],["A deposit of $1,000 at 10% interest for 2 years. The difference between the final value with compound interest and with simple interest is ...",["$10","$21","$100","$0"],"Compound: 1,000 × 1.1^2 = 1,210; simple: 1,000 + 2 × 100 = 1,200; difference $10 (interest on interest)."],["The present value of an annuity due of $1,000 per year for 3 years at a discount rate of 6% is ...",["$2,833.39","$2,673.01","$3,183.62","$3,000.00"],"PV ordinary = (1,000 ÷ 0.06) × [1 − 1 ÷ 1.06^3] = 2,673.01; PV annuity due = 2,673.01 × 1.06 = $2,833.39."],["In Table 5.2 (annuity of $1,000 for 5 years), when does the first cash flow of the annuity due (Annuity B) occur?",["Year 5","Year 1","Year 0","Year 6"],"An annuity due pays at the start of the period: cash flows in years 0 to 4, while the ordinary annuity is in years 1 to 5."],["A company can invest $15,000 today for total cash flows of $17,000 over 5 years. Why is it not enough just to compare $17,000 with $15,000?",["Because the total cash flows already include tax","Because future cash flows are always smaller than the initial cost","Because an investment must always be compared with inflation only","Cash flows must be compared at a single point in time through compounding or discounting"],"For a correct decision, managers must compare cash flows at a single point in time, by compounding to FV or discounting to PV."],["A growing perpetuity with year 1 cash flow of $200, discount rate of 12%, and growth of 4% per year. Its present value is ...",["$2,500","$1,666.67","$1,250","$5,000"],"PV0 = CF1 ÷ (r − g) = 200 ÷ (0.12 − 0.04) = 200 ÷ 0.08 = $2,500 (without growth: 200 ÷ 0.12 = $1,666.67)."],["How much must be deposited today to become $10,000 in 6 years at 5% interest compounded annually?",["$13,400.96","$7,462.15","$7,692.31","$7,000.00"],"PV = 10,000 ÷ (1.05)^6 = 10,000 ÷ 1.340096 = $7,462.15."],["Option A: receive $10,000 today. Option B: receive $12,000 three years from now. Opportunity cost 7%. The correct decision is ...",["Choose A, because the PV of option B is only about $9,795.57","Both are worth the same","Choose B, because its PV is about $10,857","Choose B, because $12,000 is greater than $10,000"],"PV of B = 12,000 ÷ (1.07)^3 = 12,000 ÷ 1.225043 = $9,795.57 < $10,000, so A is worth more."],["A $2,000 deposit is made at the start of each year for 4 years at 8% interest. The future value of this annuity due is ...",["$8,640.00","$9,012.22","$10,512.00","$9,733.20"],"FV ordinary = 2,000 × [(1.08)^4 − 1] ÷ 0.08 = 9,012.22; FV annuity due = 9,012.22 × 1.08 = $9,733.20."],["Paul Shorter will receive $300 one year from now and his opportunity cost is 2%. What is the maximum he should pay today, and why is paying more unwise?",0,"PV = 300 / 1.02 = $294.12. Paying more means a return below the 2% opportunity cost; paying less means a return above 2%."],["Explain why the value of an annuity due is always greater than that of an ordinary annuity (both PV and FV) and state the relationship between their formulas.",0,"Each annuity-due cash flow arrives one period earlier, so it earns interest for one more period (FV) or is discounted one period less (PV). Relationship: annuity-due value = ordinary annuity value × (1 + r)."],["A project yields a year-1 cash flow of $100 and continues forever. The discount rate is 10%. Compute the PV if the cash flow is constant and if it grows 4% per year, then explain the difference.",0,"Perpetuity: PV = CF1 / r = 100 / 0.10 = $1,000. Growing perpetuity: PV = CF1 / (r − g) = 100 / (0.10 − 0.04) = $1,666.67. Growth raises PV because future cash flows are larger; this requires r > g."],["Marketing is defined as the process by which a company ...",["Creates value for customers and builds strong relationships in order to capture value from customers in return","Sells as many products as possible through promotion","Lowers production costs","Controls distributors"],"Marketing = the process of creating customer value and building strong relationships to capture value in return."],["Wants that are backed by buying power are called ...",["Values","Expectations","Demands","Needs"],"Needs = states of felt deprivation; wants = forms of needs shaped by culture/personality; demands = wants backed by buying power."],["Why is \"To bring inspiration and innovation to every athlete in the world\" (Nike) better than \"To sell sports products\"?",["Because a mission statement should be market-oriented and meet customer needs","Because it is shorter","Because it lowers costs","Because it mentions the product"],"A good mission statement is market-oriented and focuses on customer needs, not on products."],["Customer satisfaction is defined as ...",["The level of brand loyalty","The extent to which perceived performance matches the buyer's expectations","The number of repeat purchases","The difference between total customer benefits and total customer costs"],"Customer-perceived value = the difference between benefits and costs; satisfaction = the match between perceived performance and expectations."],["The difference between a value chain and a value delivery network is ...",["Value chain: a series of internal departments that create value; value delivery network: the company, suppliers, distributors, and customers partnering together","The value chain is only external","They are the same","The value delivery network is only internal"],"Value chain = partnering with internal partners; value delivery network = external partners."],["The societal marketing concept emphasizes ...",["Aggressive short-term selling","Product quality only","Low-cost mass production","Sustainable marketing that considers customer well-being, not just short-term economic profit"],"Societal marketing concept = sustainable marketing that cares about customer well-being."],["A marketer promises far more than the product can actually deliver. Based on the principle of managing expectations, the most likely impact is ...",["High satisfaction because expectations are high","Disappointment because perceived performance falls below expectations","No impact","Delight"],"Expectations must be set appropriately, neither too high nor too low; performance < expectations leads to disappointment."],["According to the material, marketing management is the art and science of ...",["Pushing production costs as low as possible so selling prices can compete in the mass market","Selling all existing product inventory through aggressive promotion and selling","Choosing target markets and getting, keeping, and growing customers through superior customer value","Preparing marketing financial reports for shareholders"],"The slide defines marketing management as the art and science of choosing target markets and getting, keeping, and growing customers by creating, delivering, and communicating superior customer value."],["The need for belonging and affection is a type of ... needs",["Economic needs","Physical needs","Social needs","Individual needs"],"The slide divides needs into physical (food, clothing, warmth, safety), social (belonging and affection), and individual (knowledge and self-expression)."],["The question \"How can we best serve these customers?\" in marketing management is answered by determining the ...",["Target market","Value proposition","Marketing control","Business portfolio"],"The slide pairs \"What customers will we serve?\" with the target market and \"How can we best serve these customers?\" with the value proposition."],["In the comparison of the 4Ps and the 4Cs, the counterpart of Place is ...",["Convenience","Customer solution","Communication","Customer cost"],"Pairs on the slide: Product - Customer solution, Price - Customer cost, Place - Convenience, Promotion - Communication."],["In marketing planning, the question \"Where do we want to go?\" is answered by the ...",["Performance plan","Marketing strategy","Executive summary","Situation analysis"],"The slide maps Situation analysis = Where are we now?, Marketing strategy = Where do we want to go?, Performance plan = Where will this take us?"],["In the customer relationship groups matrix, customers with high potential profitability and long-term projected loyalty are called ...",["Butterflies","Barnacles","True friends","Strangers"],"True friends sit in the high-profitability, long-term-loyalty quadrant; butterflies are profitable but have short-term loyalty."],["Measuring and evaluating the results of marketing strategies and then taking corrective action is called ...",["Marketing implementation","Marketing analysis","Strategic planning","Marketing control"],"The slide defines marketing control as measuring and evaluating the results of marketing strategies and taking corrective actions."],["In the business portfolio analysis matrix, a business unit with a low market growth rate but a high relative market share is called a ...",["Question mark","Star","Cash cow","Dog"],"Matrix on the slide: Star (high growth, high share), Question mark (high growth, low share), Cash cow (low growth, high share), Dog (low growth, low share)."],["Under Armour entered the digital personal health and fitness market by acquiring several fitness app companies. On the Product/Market Expansion Grid, this move is ...",["Diversification","Market penetration","Market development","Product development"],"The slide cites this Under Armour example as diversification, i.e., growing by starting or buying businesses outside the company's current products and markets."],["A company sells its existing products to international markets and new consumer segments. On the Product/Market Expansion Grid, this strategy is ...",["Market penetration","Market development","Diversification","Product development"],"Market development is growing by selling existing products in new markets; the slide uses Under Armour entering new markets, including international ones, as an example."],["A company starts from its existing factories and products, then pursues profit through sales volume with intensive selling and promoting. This orientation is the ...",["Marketing concept","Selling concept","Product concept","Societal marketing concept"],"On the Selling vs. Marketing slide, the selling concept starts with the factory, focuses on existing products, uses selling and promoting, and aims at profits through sales volume."],["On the customer relationship groups matrix, the customer segment that is highly profitable but only short-term loyal is called ...",["Barnacles","Butterflies","True friends","Strangers"],"Butterflies sit in the high potential profitability, short-term projected loyalty quadrant."],["Which of the following does the slide classify as a marketing objective, not a business objective?",["Invest in research","Build profitable customer relationships","Improve profits","Increase market share"],"The slide separates business objectives (build profitable customer relationships, invest in research, improve profits) from marketing objectives (increase market share, create local partnerships, increase promotion)."],["According to the material, the goal of customer-engagement marketing is ...",["To make the brand a meaningful part of consumers' conversations and lives","To reduce interaction with consumers so customer service costs fall","To cut prices periodically so consumers keep repurchasing","To replace all mass-media marketing with paid social media advertising"],"The slide states that the goal of customer-engagement marketing is to make the brand a meaningful part of consumers' conversations and lives; marketers combine mass media with online, mobile, and social media."],["In the SWOT analysis on the slide, internal limitations that may interfere with a company's ability to achieve its objectives are called ...",["Opportunities","Weaknesses","Strengths","Threats"],"Weaknesses are internal limitations that may interfere with a company's ability to achieve its objectives; threats are external."],["In the modern marketing system, a company and its competitors reach final consumers directly or through ...",["The internal value chain","Marketing intermediaries","The business portfolio","Marketing control"],"The modern marketing system figure shows suppliers, the company and competitors, marketing intermediaries, and then final consumers, all affected by major environmental forces."],["In the Return on Marketing Investment model on the slide, which of the following is NOT part of marketing returns?",["Increased customer attraction and retention","Cost of marketing investment","Improved customer value and engagement","Increased customer lifetime values and customer equity"],"The ROMI model compares marketing returns (customer value and engagement, attraction, retention, lifetime value, and customer equity) against the cost of marketing investment; cost is the comparator, not a return."],["In the contents of a marketing plan on the slide, the element that comes right after \"Threats and opportunities\" is ...",["Budgets","Action programs","Objectives and issues","Executive summary"],"Order on the slide: executive summary, current marketing situation, threats and opportunities, objectives and issues, marketing strategy, action programs, budgets, controls."],["A sweet-drink maker greatly satisfies customers' tastes and earns big profits, but ignores the long-term health impact on its customers. The most accurate assessment is ...",["It applies the production concept because it focuses on cost efficiency","It applies the selling concept because it starts from customer needs","It fulfills the marketing concept but not yet the societal marketing concept","It fulfills the societal marketing concept because customers feel satisfied"],"The societal marketing concept requires sustainable marketing that cares about customer well-being, not just short-term economic profit; merely satisfying wants is only the marketing concept."],["A company has strong production capabilities but keeps ignoring changing market opportunities. According to the definition of strategic planning in the material, what is missing is ...",["Marketing control to measure results and take corrective action","A business portfolio consisting of stars, cash cows, question marks, and dogs","A value delivery network among the company, suppliers, distributors, and customers","Strategic fit between the organization's goals and capabilities and changing market opportunities"],"Strategic planning is defined as the process of developing and maintaining a strategic fit between the organization's goals and capabilities and its changing marketing opportunities."],["Explain how a company chooses which customers to serve and sets its value proposition.",0,"The company decides whom to serve through segmentation and targeting, then differentiates and positions itself with a value proposition, i.e., the set of benefits offered to satisfy customer needs."],["Describe the four stages of company-wide strategic planning and the role of marketing within them.",0,"(1) Define a market-oriented mission; (2) Set business and marketing objectives (e.g., profit, research, market share, local partnerships); (3) Design the business portfolio by analyzing current businesses; (4) Plan marketing by partnering with internal partners (value chain) and external partners (value delivery network), then build the marketing strategy and marketing mix. Marketing aligns goals and capabilities with market opportunities."],["Compare the production, product, selling, marketing, and societal marketing concepts. Why is the selling concept problematic when customers often do not know what they want?",0,"Production: focus on availability/low cost; product: focus on quality; selling: focus on aggressive selling; marketing: focus on customer needs; societal marketing: adds customer/societal well-being. The marketing concept uncovers latent needs and creates solutions, whereas the selling concept only pushes existing products."],["The three components of a good strategy in the material are ...",["Plan, Do, Check","Diagnosis (analysis), guiding policy (formulation), coherent actions (implementation)","Product, price, place","Mission, vision, value"],"A good strategy: a diagnosis of the challenge, a guiding policy, and a set of coherent actions."],["The letters in VRIO analysis stand for ...",["Vision, Resource, Innovation, Operation","Variable, Real, Internal, Open","Value, Revenue, Income, Output","Valuable, Rare, Inimitable, Organized"],"VRIO = Valuable, Rare, Inimitable, Organized."],["Which of the following is NOT part of the 5Cs of marketing?",["Collaborators","Currency","Company","Climate"],"The 5Cs: Company, Collaborators, Customers, Competitors, Climate."],["A resource that is valuable and rare but easily imitated by competitors most likely produces ...",["Competitive disadvantage","No effect","Temporary competitive advantage","Sustained competitive advantage"],"If it is not inimitable, the advantage is only temporary because competitors can copy it."],["In the 5C analysis, the C that represents an INTERNAL factor is ...",["Climate","Customers","Competitors","Company"],"Company = internal; collaborators, customers, competitors, and climate = external."],["The purpose of 5C analysis is ...",["To identify internal and external patterns, trends, and challenges","To calculate net profit","To prepare the advertising budget","To set product prices"],"5C analysis is used to find internal and external patterns, trends, and challenges."],["A company's resource is valuable, rare, and inimitable, but the company is not yet organized to exploit it. According to VRIO logic, the result is ...",["Competitive disadvantage","Full sustained competitive advantage","Unused competitive advantage (not yet exploited)","Competitive parity"],"The Organized dimension determines whether the potential advantage is actually captured."],["The two basic paths to competitive advantage shown on the slide are ...",["Being big and being fast","Being cheap and being different","Being local and being global","Being first and being last"],"The Competitive Advantage slide shows two options: being \"cheap\" and being different, then maps them to lower costs and differentiation."],["Among the three elements of a good strategy, \"a guiding policy to address the competitive challenge\" relates to the stage of ...",["Formulation","Analysis","Implementation","Control"],"The slide pairs diagnosis with analysis, guiding policy with formulation, and coherent actions with implementation."],["According to the \"What do my customers want?\" slide, besides solutions, what can set a company apart when competitors can also provide solutions is ...",["The most advertising","The largest production capacity","A meaningful experience","The cheapest price"],"The slide shows \"Solutions!\" and then answers the question \"but my competitors can do it too?\" by adding a meaningful experience."],["In the first step of VRIO analysis, resources and capabilities are grouped into ...",["Users, influencers, buyers, deciders","Product, price, place, promotion","Financial, human, material, non-material","Political, economic, social, technological"],"Step 1 of VRIO on the slide: list the resources and capabilities, namely financial, human, material, and non-material."],["In SWOT analysis, opportunities and threats are ... factors",["Organizational","Financial","Internal","External"],"The Situation Analysis slide places strengths and weaknesses as internal, opportunities and threats as external."],["According to the 5C slide, \"Climate\" refers to ...",["External factors such as the economic and political environment","The customers that are the company's primary target","The company's internal culture and work atmosphere","Partners that help run the company's operations"],"Slide on Climate: climate refers to the external factors, such as the economic and political environment; climate analysis evaluates macro factors."],["Which of the following is an example of collaborators according to the slide?",["Regulations and social trends","Agencies, suppliers, distributors, and business partners","Product lines and company culture","Direct competitors and new competitors"],"Slide on Collaborators: agencies, suppliers, distributors, and business partners are typical collaborators."],["A characteristic of blue ocean strategy on the slide is ...",["Creating uncontested market space and making the competition irrelevant","Exploiting existing demand in the market","Competing in existing market space and beating the competition","Choosing between differentiation or low cost only"],"Blue ocean: create uncontested market space, make the competition irrelevant, create and capture new demand, break the value-cost trade-off."],["A company targets a broad market with a cost advantage over competitors. On the competitive advantage matrix, this is ...",["Cost leadership","Differentiation","Cost focus","Differentiation focus"],"Matrix: lower costs + broad target = cost leadership; lower costs + narrow target = cost focus."],["A small fashion brand serves only the hiking community with a unique design different from competitors. On the competitive advantage matrix, its position is ...",["Cost leadership","Differentiation","Cost focus","Differentiation focus"],"Differentiation with a narrow target gives differentiation focus."],["According to the VRIO flowchart, a resource judged not valuable results in ...",["Competitive disadvantage","Unused competitive advantage","Temporary competitive advantage","Competitive parity"],"If the answer to Valuable is No, the flowchart leads to competitive disadvantage."],["In the Kano customer satisfaction model, a feature that increases satisfaction when present but does not cause dissatisfaction when absent is called ...",["Indifferent attributes","Basic factors","Performance factors","Excitement factors"],"Slide: excitement factors increase customer satisfaction if delivered but do not cause dissatisfaction if not delivered."],["Mobile phone battery life: the higher the performance, the more satisfied customers are; the lower it is, the more dissatisfied they are. According to the Kano model, this is a ...",["Excitement factor","Basic factor","Indifferent attribute","Performance factor"],"Performance factors create satisfaction when performance is high and dissatisfaction when performance is low."],["The question \"Are there any new or proposed laws or regulations that may affect my business?\" belongs to which C of the analysis?",["Collaborators","Competitors","Company","Climate"],"Questions about regulations, social trends, the economy, and technology are listed under Questions to Ask for Climate."],["The questions \"Who processes my credit card payments?\" and \"Who is my shipping provider?\" belong to the analysis of ...",["Collaborators","Customers","Climate","Company"],"Both questions are listed under Questions to Ask for Collaborators, i.e., external parties who help run the business."],["Which of the following is a characteristic of a red ocean strategy according to the slide?",["Beating the competition and exploiting existing demand","Breaking the value-cost trade-off","Creating and capturing new demand","Making the competition irrelevant"],"Red ocean: compete in existing market space, beat the competition, exploit existing demand, make the value-cost trade-off."],["According to the VRIO flowchart, for a resource/capability that gets a \"No\" answer, the next question is ...",["Whether the resource should be sold immediately","Whether competitors have a cheaper resource","Whether there is potential to improve the resource/capability","Whether the resource is a Climate factor"],"The flowchart asks \"Is there the potential to improve resources/capabilities with a No answer?\"; if not, re-plan when conditions change; if yes, find ways to maintain/improve."],["On the situation analysis diagram, which of the following belongs to the micro environment, not the macro environment?",["Natural","Technological","Marketing intermediaries","Demographic"],"The macro diagram contains demographic, economic, natural, technological, political, cultural; the micro diagram contains company, suppliers, marketing intermediaries, competitors, publics, customers."],["Hotel guests are very disappointed if a room is not clean, but a clean room does not make them more satisfied. According to the Kano model, room cleanliness is a ...",["Basic factor","Excitement factor","Indifferent attribute","Performance factor"],"Basic factors are minimum requirements: they cause dissatisfaction if unmet but do not create satisfaction when met."],["A company immediately launches discounts, new products, and an advertising campaign without understanding the competitive challenge it faces. The element of a good strategy most clearly missed is ...",["Diagnosis of the competitive challenge","Set of coherent actions","Marketing control","Implementation plan"],"A good strategy starts with a diagnosis (analysis) of the competitive challenge; actions without a diagnosis have no basis for a guiding policy."],["Explain the three elements of a good strategy and give a short example in the marketing of a product.",0,"Diagnosis: understanding the competitive challenge (e.g., many cheap competitors). Guiding policy: the direction for facing the challenge (e.g., differentiating on experience). Coherent actions: integrated actions that implement the policy (product, price, channel, and communication aligned)."],["Apply VRIO analysis to the Unilever in Brazil example: explain the steps for assessing each resource and the strategic implications.",0,"List the resources and capabilities, then assess each item: Valuable? Rare? Inimitable? Organized? Implications: not valuable = disadvantage; valuable only = parity; valuable and rare = temporary advantage; plus inimitable = sustained advantage if the organization is able to exploit it."],["Use 5C analysis to evaluate a plan to enter a new market. State the key questions for each C.",0,"Company: capabilities, resources, goals. Collaborators: partners, distributors, suppliers. Customers: needs, segments, behavior. Competitors: position, strengths, strategies. Climate: political, economic, social, technological, and regulatory factors."],["The four factors that influence consumer buying behavior are ...",["Need, want, demand, value","Cultural, social, personal, psychological","Price, product, place, promotion","Economic, political, legal, technological"],"Four factors: cultural, social, personal, psychological."],["The correct order of the stages in the buyer decision process is ...",["Information search, purchase decision, need recognition, evaluation, postpurchase","Evaluation, need recognition, purchase decision, search, postpurchase","Purchase decision, need recognition, information search, evaluation, postpurchase","Need recognition, information search, evaluation of alternatives, purchase decision, postpurchase behavior"],"The five stages of the consumer buying decision process."],["If perceived product performance exceeds expectations, the consumer feels ...",["Satisfaction","Delight","Disappointment","Dissonance"],"Performance < expectations = disappointment; = satisfaction; > expectations = delight."],["A consumer is highly involved in an expensive, infrequent, and risky purchase but sees little difference among brands. This behavior is called ...",["Dissonance-reducing buying behavior","Habitual buying behavior","Variety-seeking buying behavior","Complex buying behavior"],"Dissonance-reducing buying: high involvement, little difference among brands."],["A consumer with low involvement who sees significant brand differences and therefore often switches brands shows ...",["Variety-seeking buying behavior","Dissonance-reducing buying behavior","Habitual buying behavior","Complex buying behavior"],"Variety-seeking: low involvement, significant brand differences, a lot of brand switching."],["The order of the stages in the adoption process for a new product is ...",["Awareness, Interest, Evaluation, Trial, Adoption","Awareness, Trial, Interest, Adoption, Evaluation","Interest, Awareness, Trial, Evaluation, Adoption","Trial, Awareness, Interest, Evaluation, Adoption"],"Awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption."],["An innovative product is hard to understand, does not fit the target market's values, and cannot be tried on a small scale. The characteristics that slow adoption are ...",["Promotion only","Price only","High relative advantage and high communicability","Complexity, low compatibility, and low divisibility"],"Adoption is slower when complexity is high, compatibility is low, and divisibility is low."],["According to the material, the consumer market consists of ...",["Distributors who buy for resale","Government agencies that buy for public services","Individuals and households who buy goods and services for personal consumption","Organizations that buy goods to produce other goods"],"Consumer buyer behavior is the buying behavior of final consumers, i.e., individuals and households who buy for personal consumption; they make up the consumer market."],["Reference groups and roles and status are ... factors",["Social","Personal","Cultural","Psychological"],"Social factors on the slide: reference groups, family, roles and status."],["Occupation and economic situation are ... factors",["Psychological","Cultural","Personal","Social"],"Personal factors: age and life-cycle stage, occupation, economic situation, lifestyle, personality and self-concept."],["Subculture and social class are ... factors",["Personal","Cultural","Psychological","Social"],"Cultural factors on the slide: culture, subculture, social class."],["The process by which people select, organize, and interpret information to form a meaningful picture of the world is called ...",["Attitude","Motivation","Perception","Learning"],"That is the definition of perception on the psychological factors slide."],["Changes in an individual's behavior arising from experience are called ...",["Motive","Perception","Belief","Learning"],"Slide: learning is changes in an individual's behavior arising from experience."],["In Maslow's hierarchy of needs, the highest level of need is ...",["Esteem","Self-actualization","Love/Belonging","Safety"],"Maslow's pyramid on the slide, from the bottom: physiological, safety, love/belonging, esteem, self-actualization."],["On the adopter categories curve, the first group to adopt an innovation, making up 2.5%, is called ...",["Innovators","Early adopters","Laggards","Early majority"],"Adopter categories slide: innovators 2.5%, early adopters 13.5%, early majority 34%, late majority 34%, laggards 16%."],["Rina always buys the same brand of bottled water without much thought because she thinks all brands are almost the same. Her buying behavior type is ...",["Variety-seeking buying behavior","Complex buying behavior","Habitual buying behavior","Dissonance-reducing buying behavior"],"Habitual buying behavior occurs with low consumer involvement and little significant brand difference."],["Andi buys an expensive laptop, carefully compares specifications, and sees big differences among brands. His buying behavior type is ...",["Variety-seeking buying behavior","Complex buying behavior","Dissonance-reducing buying behavior","Habitual buying behavior"],"Complex buying behavior: consumers are highly involved and see significant differences among brands, e.g., for expensive, risky, infrequently bought products."],["Someone buys a luxury watch because it reflects who they are. The concept that best explains this behavior is ...",["Self-concept","Learning","Derived demand","Perception"],"Self-concept holds that people's possessions contribute to and reflect their identities."],["Watches, shoes, wallets, and jewelry are grouped on the slide as ... products",["Ego-expressive","Habitual","Functional","Low-involvement"],"Different product types slide: low-involvement (cigarettes, water, parking), functional (motorbike, hair dryer, charger), ego-expressive (watch, shoes, wallet, jewelry)."],["A consumer buys a new shampoo in a sachet to test the results before buying the large size. This is the ... stage of the adoption process",["Adoption","Trial","Evaluation","Interest"],"Trial: the consumer tries the new product on a small scale to improve his or her estimate of its value."],["The new-product characteristic that asks \"Can results be easily observed or described to others?\" is ...",["Communicability","Compatibility","Divisibility","Relative advantage"],"That is the definition of communicability on the influence of product characteristics on rate of adoption slide."],["Which of the following is NOT a component of total customer cost according to the slide?",["Psychic cost","Energy cost","Psychological benefits","Time cost"],"Total customer cost includes monetary, time, energy, and psychic costs; psychological benefits are part of total customer value."],["On the adopter categories slide, the group described as skeptical and of below-average social status is ...",["Early adopters","Early majority","Innovators","Late majority"],"Slide: late majority - skeptical, below average social status; early majority - deliberate, many informal social contacts."],["In the buyer's black box model, \"purchase behavior: what the buyer buys, when, where, and how much\" belongs to ...",["Marketing stimuli","Other stimuli","Buyer responses","Buyer's characteristics"],"The model: environment (marketing and other stimuli), buyer's black box (characteristics and decision process), then buyer responses, which include buying attitudes, purchase behavior, and relationship behavior."],["In the consumer perceived value diagram, the question \"What could go wrong?\" represents the component ...",["Promise as part of perceived value","Differentiation as part of perceived value","Effort as part of perceived value","Risk as part of perceived cost"],"The diagram weighs perceived value (promise, differentiation, reasons-to-believe) against perceived cost (price, risk \"What could go wrong?\", effort)."],["On the Theory of Choice slide, the AISAS model adds two stages that are not in AIDA, namely ...",["Desire and Memory","Interest and Action","Attention and Desire","Search and Share"],"AISAS = Attention, Interest, Search, Action, Share; the slide also distinguishes passive and active stages."],["A consumer is a role model in their community, slightly above average in education, and adopts new products earlier than the majority. Their adopter category is ...",["Innovators","Early majority","Early adopters","Late majority"],"Slide: early adopters (13.5%) are leaders in a social setting with slightly above-average education; innovators are more venturesome and use many information sources."],["Explain the concept of multiple selves in self-concept (actual self, ideal self, social self, ideal social self).",0,"Self-concept is multidimensional: actual self (how I see myself now), ideal self (how I would like to see myself), social self/looking-glass self (how I think others see me), ideal social self (how I would like others to see me). Product ownership reflects these identities."],["Explain total customer value and total customer cost, then how marketers can increase customer perceived value.",0,"Total customer value: the perceived monetary value of the economic, functional, and psychological benefits. Total customer cost: the monetary, time, energy, and psychic costs of evaluating, obtaining, using, and disposing. Perceived value can rise by increasing benefits or reducing costs."],["How are the theory of choice and theory of use frameworks used to analyze consumer behavior? State their main elements.",0,"Theory of choice: decision-making unit, buying motivation, decision characteristics (trigger, depth/breadth of information search, alternatives evaluated, final decision), place/product. Theory of use: consumption experience, consumer-product relationship, relationship disruptions, positive surprises, satisfaction, product meaning, and brand."],["The business market consists of ...",["All organizations that buy goods/services for use in producing other products that are sold, rented, or supplied","Households that buy for personal consumption","Governments only","Retailers only"],"The definition of the business market in the material."],["The four main factors that influence business buyer behavior are ...",["Environmental, organizational, interpersonal, individual","Economic, legal, ethical, philanthropic","Price, product, place, promotion","Cultural, social, personal, psychological"],"Different from consumers (cultural, social, personal, psychological)."],["The role in the buying center that controls the flow of information is called ...",["Influencers","Users","Gatekeepers","Buyers"],"Gatekeepers control the flow of information."],["A purchase that requires research because the buyer wants to modify specifications, prices, terms, or suppliers is called a ...",["Straight rebuy","Modified rebuy","Impulse buy","New task"],"Straight rebuy = routine; modified rebuy = needs research and modification; new task = thorough research."],["Demand in the business market that originates from demand for consumer products is called ...",["Derived demand","Primary demand","Elastic demand","Latent demand"],"Derived demand is one characteristic of the structure of business markets."],["A purchase decision that requires thorough research because the product is new to the buyer is called ...",["Reciprocal buying","Modified rebuy","New task","Straight rebuy"],"New task = a purchase decision that requires thorough research."],["An engineer sets technical specifications and provides information for evaluating alternatives, but has no authority to choose suppliers. Their role in the buying center is ...",["Gatekeeper","Buyer","Influencer","Decider"],"Influencers help define specifications and provide information; deciders choose/approve suppliers; buyers have formal authority to arrange purchase terms."],["According to the slide \"The Complexity of B2B Decision\", the average number of people involved in a single B2B buying decision is ...",["5.4 people","3 people","10 people","1.5 people"],"The slide states that 5.4 people are now involved in the average B2B buying decision."],["In business buyer behavior, the participants who will use the product or service are called ...",["Gatekeepers","Users","Buyers","Deciders"],"Slide: users are those that will use the product or service."],["The purchasing department reorders photocopy paper from the same supplier without any changes. This buying situation is a ...",["Derived demand","Modified rebuy","New task","Straight rebuy"],"A straight rebuy is a routine purchase decision such as a reorder without any modification."],["On the Major Influencers on Business Buyers slide, the personal factor shown is ...",["Emotion","Cost of money","Price","Service"],"The slide divides economic factors (price and service) from personal factors (emotion)."],["Which of the following is an organizational factor influencing business buyers?",["Persuasiveness","Procedures","Education","Economic outlook"],"Organizational factors: objectives, policies, procedures, structure, systems. Economic outlook = environmental, persuasiveness = interpersonal, education = individual."],["The first stage in the business buying process is ...",["General need description","Supplier search","Problem recognition","Product specification"],"Order on the slide: problem recognition, general need description, product specification, supplier search, proposal solicitation, supplier selection, order-routine specification, performance review."],["The last stage in the business buying process is ...",["Proposal solicitation","Supplier selection","Order-routine specification","Performance review"],"Performance review is the eighth stage, after order-routine specification."],["According to the B2B vs. B2C differences table, relationship development in B2B tends to occur through ...",["Mass advertising","Unilateral","Joint negotiation","Open auction"],"Slide table: relationship development - B2B tends toward joint negotiation, consumer marketing toward unilateral."],["A procurement manager has formal authority to select suppliers and arrange purchase terms. Their role in the buying center is ...",["Influencer","Buyer","Gatekeeper","User"],"Buyers have formal authority to select the supplier and arrange terms of purchase."],["A director is not listed on the procurement team, but their approval determines the final supplier chosen. Their role is ...",["Decider","User","Buyer","Gatekeeper"],"Deciders have formal or informal power to select and approve final suppliers."],["A rise in loan interest rates causes a company to postpone buying new machinery. The factor influencing this purchase is ...",["Individual factors (income)","Environmental factors (cost of money)","Organizational factors (procedures)","Interpersonal factors (status)"],"Cost of money is listed under environmental factors, along with demand for product, economic outlook, supply of materials, technology, culture, politics, competition."],["A purchasing manager is reluctant to switch to a new supplier because of a risk-averse attitude. The factor at work is ...",["Environmental factors","Organizational factors","Individual factors","Interpersonal factors"],"Attitude toward risk is an individual factor, along with motives, perceptions, preferences, age, income, education."],["According to the B2B vs. B2C differences table, the professionalism of those involved in a B2B buying decision tends to be ...",["Shorter","More rational","More unilateral","More emotive"],"Table: professionalism of those involved - B2B more rational, consumer marketing more emotive."],["In the business buying process, the stage that comes right after product specification is ...",["Proposal solicitation","Supplier selection","Supplier search","General need description"],"Order: ... product specification, supplier search, proposal solicitation, supplier selection, ..."],["In the business buying process, the stage that comes right after proposal solicitation is ...",["Supplier search","Performance review","Order-routine specification","Supplier selection"],"After soliciting proposals, the buyer selects suppliers (supplier selection), then order-routine specification."],["On the Market Structure & Demand slide, inelastic demand and fluctuating demand are grouped as characteristics of ...",["Fewer and larger buyers","Straight rebuy","Derived demand","Buyer and seller dependency"],"The slide shows derived demand with sub-points inelastic demand and fluctuating demand."],["In the Dell example, which products are offered to the B2B segment?",["PCs for individuals and households","Simple service agreements for households","Enterprise storage, servers, and complex service offerings","Printers and consumer electronics"],"The Dell diagram separates B2C (individuals and households: PCs, printers, consumer electronics, simple service agreements) from B2B (businesses, institutions, government: PCs, enterprise storage, servers, complex service offerings)."],["In the Different STP for B2B customers example, the services of sharing downstream data (POS) and co-managed inventory programs are given to ...",["Top-tier","Mid-tier","All tiers equally","Lowest-tier"],"High-tier services include sharing downstream data, sharing replenishment plans and sales forecasts, and co-managed inventory; the lowest tier gets only basic services."],["In the business buyer behavior model, which of the following is NOT a buyer response?",["Supplier choice","Order quantities","Delivery terms and times","Economic and technological stimuli"],"Buyer responses: product or service choice, supplier choice, order quantities, delivery terms and times, service terms, payment. Economic and technological are other stimuli in the environment."],["In a buying center meeting, the final decision leans toward the proposal of the most persuasive, high-status member. The group of factors most at work is ...",["Economic factors","Environmental factors","Organizational factors","Interpersonal factors"],"Interpersonal factors on the slide: authority, status, empathy, persuasiveness."],["Explain the key differences between B2B and B2C that affect marketing strategy.",0,"Business markets have fewer but larger buyers, larger transaction values, derived demand, inelastic and fluctuating demand, buyer-seller dependency, and a more complex buying process with many participants. Strategy: a relationship approach, services, and differentiated STP."],["Explain the four groups of factors that influence business buyers, with an example of each.",0,"Environmental: demand outlook, economic conditions, cost of money, supply of materials, technology, politics, culture, competition. Organizational: objectives, policies, procedures, structure, systems. Interpersonal: the roles of buying center participants. Individual: motives, perceptions, preferences, age, income, education, attitude toward risk."],["How should marketers treat economic and personal factors in business buying, and how do strategies differ for straight rebuy, modified rebuy, and new task?",0,"Economic factors (price, service) and personal factors (emotion) are both important. Straight rebuy: maintain quality and the relationship as the in-supplier. Modified rebuy: offer improvements in specifications/price/terms. New task: provide complete information and support evaluation because thorough research is involved."],["The correct order of the six steps of the marketing research process is ...",["Research design, problem definition, fieldwork, approach, report, analysis","Problem definition, approach, research design, fieldwork, data preparation & analysis, report","Report, analysis, fieldwork, design, approach, problem definition","Fieldwork, problem definition, report, design, analysis, approach"],"Steps 1-6: problem definition, development of an approach, research design formulation, fieldwork/data collection, data preparation and analysis, report preparation and presentation."],["Secondary data is data that ...",["Is collected by the researcher specifically for the current research problem","Comes only from surveys","Was collected for purposes other than the problem at hand","Is always more accurate than primary data"],"Primary data originates with the researcher for the specific problem; secondary data is collected for other purposes."],["Qualitative research in the material is described as ...",["A full census","An unstructured, exploratory methodology with a small sample that provides insights","A causal experiment","A structured methodology with a large sample and statistics"],"Qualitative = unstructured, exploratory, small samples; quantitative = quantifies data with statistics."],["The management decision problem \"Should the price of the brand be increased?\" is best translated into a marketing research problem of ...",["Determining the number of stores","Determining the effectiveness of the current advertising campaign","Determining consumer preferences for a new product","Determining the price elasticity of demand and the impact of various levels of price change on sales and profit"],"A decision problem asks what should be done; a research problem determines what information is needed and how to obtain it."],["A population is divided into mutually exclusive groups (e.g., age groups), then a random sample is drawn from each group. This is a ...",["Stratified random sample","Cluster sample","Quota sample","Simple random sample"],"Stratified: a random sample from each group; cluster: sample groups and then interview their members; quota is nonprobability."],["A research design with a fixed sample measured repeatedly over time is a ...",["Longitudinal design","Causal design","Census","Cross-sectional design"],"A longitudinal design provides a series of pictures of change over time; cross-sectional is done only once."],["A study uses a convenience sample and then claims the results represent the entire population. The main problem is ...",["The sample is too random","A nonprobability sample risks selection bias and reduces the generalizability of results","The sample size is certainly too large","There is no problem"],"A convenience sample picks the members easiest to reach, so representativeness and generalizability are limited."],["Which of the following does the slide name as one approach companies take to conduct marketing research?",["Delaying research until the product is launched","Hiring outside research specialists","Relying only on managers' intuition","Handing all research to competitors"],"Slide: use own research departments, hire outside research specialists, purchase data collected by outside firms."],["Which is an example of an internal data source in developing marketing information?",["Competitor analyses","Market research reports","Industry publications","Sales records"],"Internal sources: sales records, customer databases, website analytics. External: market research reports, competitor analyses, industry publications."],["An unproven statement or proposition about a factor or phenomenon of interest to the researcher is called a ...",["Research question","Analytical model","Theory","Hypothesis"],"Slide: a hypothesis is an unproven statement or proposition about a factor or phenomenon that is of interest to the researcher."],["A complete enumeration of all elements of a population is called a ...",["Sampling unit","Target population","Sample","Census"],"A census is a complete enumeration of the elements of a population; a sample is only a subgroup of the population."],["A company runs its own survey to test the new packaging of its product. The resulting data is ...",["Secondary data","Census data","Syndicated data","Primary data"],"Primary data is originated by the researcher for the specific purpose of addressing the research problem."],["Which of the following is a mechanical instrument in research according to the slide?",["Checkout scanners","Focus groups","Closed-ended questions","Open-ended questions"],"Mechanical instruments: people meters, checkout scanners, neuromarketing; closed/open-ended questions are part of a questionnaire."],["The simplest type of analysis, which computes means, medians, modes, frequency distributions, and percentages, is ...",["Multivariate analysis","Descriptive analysis","Inferential analysis","Predictive analysis"],"Slide: descriptive analysis is the simplest form, computing basic metrics such as mean, median, mode."],["In the classification of marketing research, pricing research is part of ...",["Exploratory design","Secondary research","Problem-solving research","Problem-identification research"],"Problem-solving research includes segmentation, product, pricing, promotion, distribution research; problem-identification includes market potential, market share, image, and others."],["A researcher selects respondents so that every member of the population has a known and equal chance of being selected. This technique is a ...",["Quota sample","Convenience sample","Simple random sample","Stratified random sample"],"Simple random sample: every member of the population has a known and equal chance of selection."],["Interviewers are told to interview exactly 50 men and 50 women in each age group, without random selection. This sampling technique is a ...",["Cluster sample","Quota sample","Judgment sample","Stratified random sample"],"Quota sample: the researcher finds and interviews a prescribed number of people in each category; it is nonprobability."],["A researcher divides a city into area blocks, selects a few blocks, then interviews residents in the selected blocks. This technique is a ...",["Simple random sample","Stratified random sample","Cluster (area) sample","Quota sample"],"Cluster sample: the population is divided into mutually exclusive groups such as blocks, then the researcher samples groups to interview."],["In an experiment, the question \"does the manipulation of the independent variable really cause the effect on the dependent variable?\" relates to ...",["External validity","Sample size","Data coding","Internal validity"],"Internal validity concerns the causality of the manipulation; external validity concerns the generalizability of experimental results."],["A supermarket tests two price levels in different stores to see the effect on sales. This type of research is ...",["Secondary research","Descriptive research","Causal research","Exploratory research"],"Causal research aims to obtain evidence of cause-and-effect relationships, using the experiment method."],["Analysis that uses t-tests, chi-square tests, and ANOVA to draw conclusions about a population from a sample is ...",["Exploratory data analysis","Descriptive analysis","Prescriptive analysis","Inferential analysis"],"Slide: inferential analysis involves making inferences about a population based on a sample using statistical tests."],["A marketing team uses optimization and simulation models to recommend the best advertising budget allocation. The type of analysis is ...",["Prescriptive analysis","Predictive analysis","Inferential analysis","Descriptive analysis"],"Prescriptive analysis goes a step beyond predictive by recommending actions; examples are optimization models and simulation."],["A company buys a competitor's product and compares its features and quality with its own. This competitive marketing intelligence technique is ...",["Monitoring social media buzz","Observing consumers firsthand","Benchmarking competitors' products","Quizzing the company's own employees"],"Benchmarking competitors' products is one of the competitive marketing intelligence techniques on the slide."],["According to the sample sizes table, the minimum sample size for problem identification research (e.g., market potential) is ...",["1,000","500","150","200"],"Table: problem identification research minimum 500, typical range 1,000-2,500; problem-solving, product tests, and test marketing minimum 200."],["A team conducts expert surveys and pilot surveys with a small sample to generate ideas before the main research. This design is best called ...",["Causal research","Exploratory research","Longitudinal design","Descriptive research"],"Exploratory research aims at the discovery of ideas and insights, is flexible, uses small non-representative samples, and its methods include expert surveys and pilot surveys; results are tentative."],["According to the slide's notes on experiments, the strongest conclusion that can be drawn about causality is ...",["X and Y are never related","X is proven to be the only cause of Y","X can be inferred to be one of the causes of Y","Y is proven to cause X"],"Slide: the scientific notion of causality implies that we can never prove that X causes Y; at best we can only infer that X is one of the causes of Y."],["Respondents are asked to complete the sentence \"The person who uses this brand is ...\". In the classification of qualitative research procedures, this technique is ...",["Indirect (disguised) - projective techniques","Direct (nondisguised) - focus groups","Quantitative - survey data","Direct (nondisguised) - depth interviews"],"Diagram: direct consists of focus groups and depth interviews; indirect is projective techniques (association, completion, construction, expressive techniques)."],["Distinguish a management decision problem from a marketing research problem with three examples.",0,"A decision problem asks what the decision maker needs to do; a research problem determines the information needed and how to obtain it. Examples: introduce a new product -> preferences and purchase intentions; change the advertising campaign -> effectiveness of the current campaign; raise the price -> price elasticity and impact on sales/profit."],["Explain the advantages and disadvantages of secondary data and when it should be used.",0,"Advantages: helps identify and clarify the problem, develop the approach and research design, answer some questions, and interpret primary data. Disadvantages: limited relevance and accuracy, the purpose/collection methods may not fit, and it may not be current."],["Explain competitive marketing intelligence: its techniques, benefits, and ethical issues.",0,"The systematic monitoring, collection, and analysis of information about competitors and the marketing environment. Techniques: observing consumers, quizzing employees, benchmarking competitors' products, online research, monitoring social media. Benefits: insight into consumer opinion, early warning of competitor strategies, protecting one's own information. Issues: the ethics of information gathering."],["The main steps in designing a customer-driven marketing strategy are ...",["Market segmentation, targeting, differentiation, positioning","Product, price, place, promotion","Awareness, interest, evaluation, trial","Analysis, planning, implementation, control"],"STP: segmentation, targeting, differentiation, and positioning."],["The four main bases for segmenting consumer markets are ...",["Product, price, channel, people","Size, growth, margin, competitors","Cost, volume, profit, risk","Geographic, demographic, psychographic, behavioral"],"Plus multiple/hybrid segmentation, e.g., geo-demographic."],["In the requirements for effective segmentation, \"Substantial\" means ...",["Size and purchasing power can be measured","Segments respond differently to the marketing mix","The segment is large and profitable enough to serve","The segment can be reached"],"Substantial: markets are large and profitable enough to serve."],["A targeting strategy that targets several segments with a separate offer for each, at a higher cost than mass marketing, is called ...",["Concentrated marketing","Differentiated marketing","Undifferentiated marketing","Micromarketing"],"Differentiated marketing aims for higher sales and a stronger position, but is more expensive."],["MASAD analysis: Segments 1 and 2 pass all criteria; Segment 3 fails Substantial and Differentiable; Segment 4 fails Accessible. The appropriate decision is ...",["Choose all segments","Choose Segments 1 and 2, eliminate Segments 3 and 4","Choose Segments 3 and 4","Choose only Segment 4"],"Segments that fail the criteria for effective segmentation should be eliminated."],["Category points-of-parity (PoP) are ...",["Premium prices","Associations to negate competitors' PoD","Unique attributes that competitors do not have","Associations considered necessary for an offering to be legitimate and credible within a category"],"Competitive PoP negates competitors' PoD; category PoP is the requirement for legitimacy within a category."],["The statement \"Our brand is the best detergent\" does not meet the positioning statement format. The missing elements are ...",["Slogan","Logo","Price","Target segment and need, and point of difference"],"Format: To (target segment and need) our (brand) is (concept) that (point of difference)."],["Which of the following does the slide name as a benefit of market segmentation?",["It is easier to design promotional activities for specific segments","The company can avoid all competitors","All customers receive the same offer","The company no longer needs to understand customer needs"],"Benefits on the slide: focus on customers with similar needs, easy to design promotions for specific segments, saves investment, and segments are easy to manage."],["A company adapts its products, advertising, and promotions for each city and region. The segmentation basis used is ...",["Geographic","Demographic","Behavioral","Psychographic"],"Geographic segmentation divides the market into geographic units and localizes products, advertising, promotion, and sales efforts to regional needs."],["Dividing buyers into light, medium, and heavy users is segmentation based on ...",["Occasion","Usage rate","User status","Loyalty status"],"Slide: usage rate divides buyers into light, medium, and heavy product users."],["Dividing buyers into ex-users, potential users, first-time users, and regular users is segmentation based on ...",["Usage rate","Social class","Benefit sought","User status"],"Slide: user status divides buyers into ex-users, potential users, first-time users, and regular users."],["Undifferentiated marketing is also known as ...",["One-to-one marketing","Mass marketing","Niche marketing","Local marketing"],"Undifferentiated marketing targets the whole market with one offer (mass marketing) and focuses on common needs."],["In the requirements for effective segmentation, \"Measurable\" means ...",["The size, purchasing power, and profiles of segments can be measured","Segments can be reached and served effectively","Segments respond differently to the marketing mix","Segments are large and profitable enough"],"Measurable: you can measure the size, purchasing power, and profiles of the segments. The other options are accessible, substantial, and differentiable."],["A set of buyers who share common needs or characteristics that the company decides to serve is called a ...",["Value proposition","Perceptual map","Target market","Potential market segment"],"Slide: a target market is a set of buyers who share common needs or characteristics that the company decides to serve."],["Attributes or benefits that consumers strongly associate with a brand, evaluate positively, and believe cannot be found to the same extent in competitors are called ...",["Category points-of-parity","Positioning map","Competitive points-of-parity","Points-of-difference"],"That is the definition of points-of-difference (PoD); points-of-parity are associations that may be shared with other brands."],["Eiger divides its market based on consumers' lifestyle and personality. This segmentation basis is ...",["Demographic","Geographic","Behavioral","Psychographic"],"The slide gives Eiger as an example, dividing the market into psychographic segments: lifestyle and personality."],["Zalora gives different offers to new customers and existing customers. The segmentation basis used is ...",["Demographic","Psychographic","Geographic","Behavioral"],"The behavioral segmentation slide gives Zalora as an example, giving different offerings to new and existing customers (user status)."],["A cookie manufacturer creates special packaging and promotions ahead of Eid (Lebaran). This is an example of ...",["Occasion segmentation","Usage rate segmentation","Geographic segmentation","Benefit segmentation"],"Occasion segmentation divides buyers according to the occasions when they get the idea to buy, make a purchase, or respond to a product, and helps build product usage."],["Concentrated (niche) marketing is most suitable for a company that ...",["Wants to reach the whole market with one offer","Wants to customize the product for each individual","Has a big budget for many segments at once","Has limited resources but knows a particular market well"],"Slide on concentrated marketing: limited company resources, knowledge of the market, more effective and efficient."],["A supermarket chain tailors its product selection and promotions at each store to the needs of the surrounding neighborhood. This strategy is ...",["Differentiated marketing","Local marketing","Undifferentiated marketing","Individual marketing"],"Local marketing tailors brands and promotions to the needs of local segments, covering cities, neighborhoods, stores."],["Individual marketing on the slide is also known as ...",["Mass marketing and undifferentiated marketing","Niche marketing and concentrated marketing","Segmented marketing and differentiated marketing","One-to-one marketing and mass customization"],"Individual marketing tailors products and programs to the needs of individuals; also called one-to-one marketing and mass customization."],["A brand adds a feature that has been a competitor's signature advantage so that the competitor's advantage no longer differentiates. This feature functions as a ...",["Category PoP","Points-of-difference","Value proposition","Competitive PoP"],"Competitive PoP is associations designed to negate a competitor's PoD."],["A company assesses the target country's government stability, openness to foreign firms, monetary regulations, and bureaucracy. This international market segmentation basis is ...",["Political and legal factors","Geographic location","Economic factors","Cultural factors"],"Slide: political and legal factors include type and stability of government, receptivity to foreign firms, monetary regulations, and amount of bureaucracy."],["An industrial supplier segments its business customers by order urgency and order size. According to the slide, these variables are ...",["Operating characteristics","Situational factors","Personal characteristics","Purchasing approaches"],"Additional variables for segmenting business markets: situational factors include urgency, specific application, and size of order."],["According to the segment evaluation table, why is a segment with a high growth rate more attractive?",["The company can gain share from primary demand without having to win business from established competitors","The segment requires no marketing investment at all","The segment is always free of indirect competitors","The segment surely has the highest profit margin"],"Slide: segments with strong growth rates are more attractive as firms can gain market share from primary demand, as opposed to needing to win business from established competitors."],["The Perceptual Map slide stresses that positioning is ...",["Based on price alone, absolute, and static","Based on perception, relative, and dynamic","Determined by the company, fixed, and unaffected by competitors","Based on product features, absolute, and permanent"],"The slide says: It is about perception; it is relative; it is dynamic. Product position is the way consumers define the product on important attributes."],["A segment is attractive in size, but to serve it the company would have to develop entirely new expertise and technology. According to the capability criterion on the slide, this segment should ...",["Be served with undifferentiated marketing to cut costs","Generally be avoided because the company is better off leveraging existing expertise","Be chosen because new expertise automatically becomes points-of-parity","Always be chosen because segment size is the only criterion"],"Slide: firms seek target markets where they can leverage existing skills, capabilities, and technologies; target markets that require the firm to develop new expertise are generally best avoided."],["Explain the bases for segmenting consumer markets, with example variables for each basis.",0,"Geographic (region, country, climate); demographic (age, gender, income, occupation, family size); psychographic (social class, lifestyle, personality); behavioral (occasion, benefit sought, user status, usage rate, loyalty); multiple/hybrid (geo-demographic, geo-psychographic)."],["Explain the process of evaluating and selecting target segments using the criteria in the material, with an example.",0,"Evaluate segment size and growth, profit margin, competition, distribution channels, fit with strategic direction and objectives, and resources, capabilities, and brand strategy. Score each segment, then choose segments that are attractive and aligned with the company's capabilities; eliminate those that fail the MASAD criteria."],["Explain the four steps of determining positioning and the criteria for a good competitive advantage, and write one positioning statement for a fictional product.",0,"Steps: evaluate the perceptual map, choose a competitive advantage, find the value proposition, write the positioning statement. The advantage must be important, distinctive, superior, communicable, preemptive, affordable, profitable. Example format: To (segment and need) our (brand) is (concept) that (point of difference)."],["OSCM is defined as ...",["Sales and marketing activities","Human resource management","The design, operation, and improvement of the systems that create and deliver a firm's primary products and services","The company's financial management"],"OSCM = the design, operation, and improvement of the systems that create and deliver a firm's primary products and services."],["The five supply chain processes in Exhibit 1.3 are ...",["Design, build, test, ship, service","Buying, storing, selling, billing, auditing","Plan, do, check, act, review","Planning, sourcing, making, delivering, returning"],"Planning, Sourcing, Making, Delivering, Returning."],["The triple bottom line covers three criteria: ...",["Economic, social, environmental","People, process, product","Cost, quality, delivery","Revenue, profit, cash"],"Triple bottom line = a business strategy that includes social, economic, and environmental criteria."],["Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of services according to the material?",["Heterogeneous","Can be stored as inventory","Intangible","Perishable and time dependent"],"Services cannot be stored; other characteristics: requires interaction and a package of features."],["The difference between efficiency and effectiveness is ...",["Efficiency: doing something at the lowest possible cost; effectiveness: doing the right things to create the most value for the customer","They are identical","Efficiency: value for the customer; effectiveness: low cost","Efficiency applies only to services"],"Efficiency = lowest possible cost; effectiveness = doing the right things to create the most value for the customer."],["Southwest turns its aircraft in 45 minutes per turn and flies 6 flights per day. The total time an aircraft spends on the ground per day is ...",["6.0 hours","2.25 hours","4.5 hours","3.0 hours"],"6 × 45 minutes = 270 minutes = 4.5 hours on the ground."],["A company outsources most of its manufacturing. The most likely impact on revenue per employee and asset turnover, and its interpretation, is ...",["Both tend to rise because employees and assets decrease, but it does not necessarily mean operations have truly improved","Both surely fall","No change","They rise and definitely mean operating efficiency improved"],"Revenue per employee is sensitive to outsourcing and asset turnover is affected by technology and outsourcing; the figures need to be interpreted together with operational metrics."],["Among the three elements of OSCM, the one that configures the network, facilities, and process steps before anything is produced is ...",["Operation","Design","Improvement","Sourcing"],"Design configures the network, facilities, and process steps before production; operation runs capacity, labor, and materials; improvement raises quality and removes waste."],["According to the material, a \"process\" is ...",["A standard procedure document approved by management","All sales activities from marketing to billing","The production machinery used to make physical goods","Any step that transforms inputs (materials, labor, information) into outputs valued by customers"],"A process is defined as steps that transform inputs (materials, labor, information) into outputs valued by customers."],["The activities of selecting suppliers, receiving and verifying shipments, and authorizing payment belong to the supply chain process of ...",["Planning","Making","Sourcing","Delivering"],"In Exhibit 1.3, sourcing covers selecting suppliers, receiving and verifying shipments, transferring them, and authorizing payment."],["The \"Returning\" process among the five supply chain processes means ...",["Returning capital to shareholders","Re-sending late orders to the central warehouse","Taking back worn-out, defective, and excess products and supporting customers after the sale","Recalculating demand forecasts every month"],"Returning = take back worn-out, defective, and excess product; support customers after the sale."],["Because service innovations are intangible and cannot be patented, the implication according to the material is ...",["Services must be produced to strict specifications without variation","A new service concept must expand rapidly before competitors copy it","Services should be stored as inventory for peak periods","Service firms do not need to design facilities for customers"],"Service innovation cannot be patented, so a new concept must expand rapidly before competitors copy it."],["On the goods-services continuum (Exhibit 1.4), an example of a \"core goods\" business is ...",["Food products and mining","Hotels and airlines","Automobiles and appliances","University and investment"],"Core goods: appliances, automobiles, data storage systems. Food/mining = pure goods; hotels/airlines = core services; university/investment = pure services."],["The company named as a pioneer of product-service bundling that views physical goods as a small part of \"business solutions\" is ...",["IBM","Southwest Airlines","Honda","L.L.Bean"],"The material cites IBM as a pioneer that treats its business as a service business."],["The professional organization related to project management in the list of OSCM professional societies is ...",["APICS","CSCMP","ISM","PMI"],"PMI = The Project Management Institute; APICS = Association for Operations Management; CSCMP = supply chain; ISM = supply management."],["Which of the following elements is an \"implicit service\" in the service package?",["Waiting time and atmosphere","Training of service personnel","Food items accompanying the service","Location and decoration of the facility"],"Implicit services: the server's attitude, atmosphere, waiting time, status, privacy, security, convenience. Training = explicit, food = facilitating goods, decoration = supporting facility."],["The service characteristic \"heterogeneous\" means ...",["Services vary from day to day and hour to hour depending on the attitudes of the customer and the provider","Services cannot be measured or weighed","Services cannot be stored for later use","Services are evaluated as a package of features that affect the senses"],"Heterogeneous: service varies with the attitudes of the customer and the server, unlike goods, which can be made with near-zero variability."],["Why do pure goods industries add services to their offerings, according to Exhibit 1.4?",["Because services can be patented more easily than goods","Because regulation requires after-sales service","Because pure goods industries have become low-margin commodity businesses and need to differentiate","Because services always increase profit as a percentage of revenue"],"Pure goods industries have become low-margin commodity businesses, so they add services to differentiate."],["Receivables turnover in the Wall Street efficiency measures shows ...",["How quickly the firm collects what customers owe it","How many times inventory is sold and replaced in a period","Revenue relative to total assets","Net income generated per employee"],"Receivables turnover = how quickly the firm collects what customers owe it."],["What is genuinely new in the business analytics movement according to the material?",["Using data to solve business problems for the first time","Far more data is captured and mathematical tools are now readily available, adding statistical analysis, forecasting, and optimization that may be real time","Standard and ad hoc reports with drill-down to transactions","Eliminating the manager's role in pricing decisions"],"Data has always been used; what is new is the volume of data and the availability of mathematical tools for statistics, forecasting, and optimization."],["According to Exhibit 1.5, the concept that was popular in the late 1990s was ...",["Just-in-time (JIT)","Supply chain management (SCM)","Business analytics","Manufacturing strategy"],"Late 1990s: SCM. JIT in the early 1980s, manufacturing strategy in the late 1970s, business analytics in the mid-2010s."],["A jet flies 6 times per day. If turnaround time is cut from 45 to 35 minutes per turn, how much ground time is saved per day?",["10 minutes","45 minutes","60 minutes","270 minutes"],"Saving per turn is 45 − 35 = 10 minutes; 6 turns × 10 minutes = 60 minutes per day."],["A company has revenue of $1.2 billion and 2,000 employees. What is revenue per employee?",["$60,000","$240,000","$6,000,000","$600,000"],"Revenue per employee = sales / headcount = 1,200,000,000 / 2,000 = $600,000."],["Firm X: revenue $50 million, total assets $40 million. Firm Y (different industry): revenue $30 million, assets $20 million. The most accurate statement is ...",["Asset turnover of X = 1.25 and Y = 1.5, but comparing them is of little use because ratios vary widely across industries","Asset turnover of X = 1.25 and Y = 1.5, so Y is surely managed better","Asset turnover of X = 0.8 and Y = 0.67, so X is more efficient","Asset turnover cannot be computed without inventory data"],"Asset turnover = revenue / assets: 50/40 = 1.25 and 30/20 = 1.5. The material stresses that ratios vary significantly by industry, so cross-industry comparison is not useful."],["In Example 1.1, GM has the highest asset turnover. The explanation the material gives for this figure is ...",["GM turns inventory fastest in the industry","GM outsources all of its production to Honda","GM has the highest income per employee","GM recently went bankrupt and was recapitalized, which could explain the higher figure"],"Slide: GM recently went bankrupt and was recapitalised, which could explain that higher figure; so the best ratio does not necessarily mean the best company."],["Based on the reading of Example 1.1, the statement consistent with the material is ...",["Honda is strongest on all ratios compared with Toyota, GM, and Ford","Ford is strongest in inventory turn but weakest in receivables turnover","Honda is weaker than Ford in asset turnover","Toyota is the weakest in asset turnover among the four"],"Slide: Honda appears stronger than Ford but weaker than Toyota and GM; Ford is strongest in inventory turn, weakest in receivables turnover."],["A factory has the lowest unit cost in its industry, but its products are no longer wanted by customers. By the material's definitions, this factory is ...",["Effective but not efficient","Both efficient and effective","Neither efficient nor effective","Efficient but not effective"],"Efficiency = lowest possible cost (met); effectiveness = doing the right things to create the most value for the customer (not met)."],["Distinguish operations processes from supply chain processes with examples.",0,"Operations: manufacturing and service processes that transform resources into products (e.g., car assembly, a call center). Supply chain: processes that move information and material to and from operations processes (logistics, warehousing, input supply, delivering outputs to customers)."],["Explain product-service bundling, how successful companies implement it, and the trade-off found.",0,"Bundling: adding service activities to a product offering (maintenance, spare parts, training, system design). Implementation: combine services within one organization, evolve from improving product performance to developing systems, then move up into new value streams. Trade-off: higher revenue but a lower percentage profit margin than focused firms."],["List the five Wall Street management efficiency measures, explain why asset turnover is the starting point, and state the cautions when comparing companies.",0,"Five ratios: income per employee, revenue per employee, receivables turnover, inventory turnover, asset turnover. Asset turnover is the most comprehensive because it covers all assets and is affected by technology/outsourcing. Comparisons should be within the same industry, over time, and accompanied by operational metrics."],["The three pillars of sustainable strategy (triple bottom line) in the material are ...",["People, Profit, Planet","Plan, Source, Make","Price, Product, Place","Cost, Quality, Time"],"People = social responsibility; Profit = economic prosperity; Planet = environmental stewardship."],["The productivity formula is ...",["Inputs / Outputs","Revenue − Cost","Outputs − Inputs","Outputs / Inputs"],"Productivity = outputs / inputs; it can be a partial, multifactor, or total factor measure."],["An order winner is ...",["The lowest cost","A screening criterion for a product to be considered","Minimum quality","A criterion that differentiates one firm's product from another's and is the basis on which buyers choose"],"An order qualifier only places the firm on the shortlist; an order winner wins the sale."],["In the example of buying a notebook computer (14-inch screen, under three pounds, latest OS, under $1,000), the order winner is ...",["Price under $1,000","14-inch screen","The longest battery life among the machines that qualify","Weight under three pounds"],"The other items are order qualifiers; order winner = longest battery life among those that qualify."],["In the four actions framework, the pair of actions that lowers the cost structure below competitors' is ...",["Eliminate and Reduce","Raise and Create","Eliminate and Raise","Reduce and Create"],"Eliminate + Reduce lower cost; Raise + Create raise value for buyers and create demand."],["A study of 108 business launches showed that ...",["86% of launches were line extensions, 14% created new markets, and that 14% generated 61% of total profits","50% of launches created new markets","Line extensions generated most of the profits","All launches generated equal profit"],"New markets were only 14% of launches but generated 61% of total profits."],["Labor productivity rose from 2.20 to 2.33 and materials from 2.75 to 2.80, but total productivity fell from 1.05 to 1.04. The right conclusion is ...",["Total productivity must have been miscalculated","Only partial measures are valid","The company is more productive","Improvements in partial measures can mislead; other inputs may have risen by more, so the total measure should be used"],"A partial measure looks at only one input; a total factor measure covers all outputs and all resources."],["Operations effectiveness is defined as ...",["Setting policies and plans for the broad use of resources","Creating uncontested market space","Measuring output divided by input for a single resource","Performing activities in the manner that best implements strategic priorities at minimum cost"],"Operations effectiveness = performing activities in a manner that best implements strategic priorities at minimum cost."],["According to the material, operations effectiveness initiatives typically show results within ...",["12 to 24 months","1 to 3 months","3 to 6 months","5 to 10 years"],"Effectiveness initiatives show results in 12 to 24 months, so strategy must anticipate needs well beyond that."],["The three stages of strategy formulation in the material are ...",["Eliminate, reduce, raise","Sensing, seizing, transforming","Plan, source, make","Strategic analysis, initiatives, implementation projects"],"Formulating the strategy: (1) strategic analysis, (2) initiatives, (3) implementation projects."],["The two characteristics of the quality dimension among the competitive dimensions are ...",["Delivery speed and delivery reliability","Cost and price","Design quality and process quality","Flexibility and new-product speed"],"Quality: design quality (features that match customer targets) and process quality (defect-free output)."],["The competitive dimension \"Deliver it when promised\", described as the cornerstone for FedEx, is ...",["Delivery speed","Cost or price","Coping with demand changes","Delivery reliability"],"Delivery reliability = supplying on or before the promised date; for FedEx it is the cornerstone."],["Which of the following characteristics belongs to blue ocean (not red ocean)?",["Compete inside existing market space","Make the competition irrelevant","Exploit existing demand","Accept the value-cost trade-off"],"Blue ocean: create uncontested space, make competition irrelevant, create new demand, break the value-cost trade-off."],["On a strategy canvas, the line connecting the offering level for each competing factor is called the ...",["Learning curve","Value curve","Demand curve","Cost curve"],"Horizontal axis = competing factors, vertical axis = offering level; the resulting line is the value curve."],["The three factors Southwest emphasizes in the \"focus\" test are ...",["Meals, lounges, and seating choices","Friendly service, speed, and frequent point-to-point departures","Hub-and-spoke, low prices, and a loyalty program","Star performers, animal shows, and multiple arenas"],"Southwest emphasizes only friendly service, speed, and frequent point-to-point departures, with no investment in meals, lounges, or seating choices."],["The seventh competitive dimension, which is product-specific, \"Support it\", covers ...",["Price discounts for large-volume purchases","Technical liaison during design, meeting a launch date, and after-sale support","Changing production volume when demand rises","Offering a wide variety of products"],"Seventh dimension: technical liaison during design, meeting a launch date, and after-sale support."],["On the \"coping with demand changes\" dimension, where does the hard decision lie according to the material?",["When scaling up, because unit cost always rises","When setting prices for commodity products","When scaling back, because it involves layoffs and asset reductions","When choosing suppliers with 24-hour delivery"],"When demand rises, scale cuts cost; scaling back is where the hard decisions live: layoffs and asset reductions."],["According to the material, coordination risks in the supply chain are usually managed with ...",["Natural disaster insurance only","Safety stock, lead time, and overtime","Eliminating all inventory","Waiting, because these risks cannot be predicted"],"Coordination risks are managed with safety stock, lead time, and overtime; disruption risks are random and nearly impossible to predict."],["The 1996 GM example in the material shows that ...",["A rise in steel prices made GM close 26 plants","A natural disaster halted all of GM's suppliers for a year","An 18-day strike at one brake supplier idled 26 assembly plants, with about $900 million in lost earnings","GM lost $900 million because of an ERP system failure"],"GM 1996: an 18-day strike at one brake supplier idled 26 assembly plants, around $900 million in lost earnings (an example of disruption risk)."],["An operation produces output worth $10,000 with labor costs of $3,000 and materials of $2,000. What is its multifactor productivity?",["3.33","5.0","0.5","2.0"],"Multifactor = output / (labor + material) = 10,000 / (3,000 + 2,000) = 2.0."],["According to the material, a value curve that zigzags with no pattern (low-high-low-low-high) signals ...",["An incoherent strategy, usually caused by divisional or functional silos running their own sub-strategies","A blue ocean strategy","Overdelivery without payback","An internally driven company"],"Signal 4: incoherent strategy, reflecting divisional/functional silos with independent sub-strategies."],["A value curve whose competing factors are written in operational jargon rather than terms buyers understand and value signals ...",["An internally driven company (an inside-out rather than outside-in view)","Caught in the red ocean","Strategic contradictions","A blue ocean strategy"],"Signal 6: internally driven company; factors written in operational jargon show an inside-out vision."],["In Cirque du Soleil's ERRC grid, a factor in the CREATE box is ...",["Star performers and animal shows","Fun and humor, and thrill and danger","Theme and refined environment","Aisle concession sales"],"Create: theme, refined environment, multiple productions, artistic music and dance. Star performers and animal shows were eliminated; fun/humor and thrill/danger were reduced."],["More than 1,600 US wineries have converging value curves: premium and cheap wines have the same curve shape at different \"heights\". The strategic implication drawn by the material is ...",["Benchmarking competitors and offering slightly more at a slightly lower price will never open up new market space","Wineries must choose between premium and budget to succeed","The wine industry is already a blue ocean","The best strategy is to raise all factors above the industry standard"],"The wine strategy canvas shows convergence; competing by being slightly better/cheaper does not open new space."],["Last year a factory's total factor productivity was 1.25. This year output is $12,000 with labor input of $4,000, material of $5,000, and capital of $1,000. The right conclusion is ...",["Total factor productivity rose to 3.0","Total factor productivity stayed at 1.25","Total factor productivity fell to 1.2","Total factor productivity rose to 2.4"],"Total factor = 12,000 / (4,000 + 5,000 + 1,000) = 1.2, lower than 1.25. Productivity is a relative measure against the same operation over time."],["The \"capital-allocation lock\" mechanism at Enel works by ...",["The investment committee, including the Chief Innovability Officer, screening every project against sustainability and innovation criteria in addition to economic criteria","The CEO automatically approving all projects submitted by countries","Buying shares in every startup that is scouted","Funding projects based on each country's market size"],"Projects that fail to meet the criteria are not funded; billions of euros were rejected because they were considered disruptable or unsustainable."],["For the question \"What do buyers value?\", Blue Ocean's addition to the classic operations strategy tools is ...",["Order qualifiers and order winners from current customers","The activity-system map and fit among activities","Measures of operations effectiveness and productivity","The strategy canvas, which shifts focus from competitors to alternatives and from customers to noncustomers"],"Classic tools: order qualifiers/winners from current customers; Blue Ocean adds the strategy canvas, with a focus on alternatives and noncustomers."],["Explain order qualifiers and order winners with an example of an office supplies supplier.",0,"Qualifiers: able to supply the required items, delivery within 24 hours, warranted items, a private web catalog. Order winner: a volume purchase price discount scheme. Qualifiers get the firm onto the consideration list; the winner determines the final choice, and the thresholds shift, so today's winner can become tomorrow's qualifier."],["Explain why IKEA's activity system is hard to imitate and how it differs from a typical furniture retailer.",0,"Typical retailer: sample showrooms, salespeople who accompany customers, orders sent to third-party factories, delivery in 6-8 weeks (high customization and service, high cost). IKEA: self-service, room displays, cheap self-assembled furniture design, a self-service warehouse, paid add-on services. The advantage comes from the fit and mutual reinforcement of all activities, not any single activity."],["Explain value innovation and distinguish red ocean from blue ocean. Use the [yellow tail] ERRC grid as an example.",0,"Value innovation: a leap in value for buyers and the company at the same time, breaking the value-cost trade-off through alignment of the whole activity system. Red ocean: competing in existing markets; blue ocean: creating markets without competitors. [yellow tail]: eliminate enological terms, aging, above-the-line marketing; reduce wine complexity and range; raise price vs. cheap wines and retail store engagement; create easy drinking, ease of selection, fun and adventure."],["The correct order of the six phases of generic product development is ...",["Planning, detail design, concept, system design, ramp-up, testing","Detail design, planning, concept, testing, system design, ramp-up","Planning, concept development, system-level design, detail design, testing & refinement, production ramp-up","Concept, planning, testing, detail, system, ramp-up"],"Phases 0-5: Planning, Concept Development, System-Level Design, Detail Design, Testing and Refinement, Production Ramp-Up."],["Quality Function Deployment (QFD) through the House of Quality aims to ...",["Translate the voice of the customer into concrete engineering/operations targets","Lower taxes","Calculate product NPV","Select suppliers"],"The House of Quality is a cross-functional matrix that converts weighted customer requirements into engineering targets."],["The product development process variant that starts from a new proprietary technology and then looks for a market (e.g., Gore-Tex, Tyvek) is ...",["Platform","Technology-push","Quick-build","Customized"],"Technology-push: the firm begins with a new technology and looks for a market."],["Concurrent engineering addresses the \"over-the-wall problem\" by ...",["Halting production","Reducing testing","Handing design drawings to the factory after they are finished","Involving manufacturing engineers from the design stage and developing the product and process simultaneously with cross-functional teams"],"Designers and manufacturing work together to avoid late design changes, costs, and delays."],["Which of the following is NOT a value analysis/value engineering question?",["How can weight be reduced?","Are there unnecessary design features?","How can the selling price be raised as high as possible?","Can two or more components be combined?"],"VA/VE: unnecessary features, combining components, reducing weight, and eliminating nonstandard components."],["In service design, \"divergence\" is ...",["The number of steps in the service","The number of ways the customer-provider interaction can vary at each step","The number of customers","The cost of the service"],"Complexity = the number of steps and the possible actions at each step; divergence = the variation in interaction at each step."],["Reducing development cost by $1 million raises NPV by $964,000 from $8.20 million. The relative change is ...",["+1.0%","−11.8%","+50%","About +11.8% (NPV becomes about $9.17 million)"],"964,000 / 8,200,000 = 11.8%; the material presents a new NPV of about $9.17 million (rounded)."],["The output of Phase 0 (Planning) in generic product development is ...",["Product architecture","Process plan and tooling","Project mission statement","Preproduction prototypes"],"Planning starts from corporate strategy and assesses technology and market goals; its output is the project mission statement."],["In the Production Ramp-Up phase, the product is ...",["Conceived based on target market needs","Made in several preproduction versions for testing","Decomposed into subsystems and components","Made using the intended production system to train the workforce and work out remaining problems before launch"],"Phase 5: made using the intended production system to train the workforce and iron out remaining problems, then launched."],["The \"quick-build\" product development process variant is exemplified by ...",["Pharmaceuticals and space systems","Software and mobile phones","Semiconductors and chemicals","Airplanes and jet engines"],"Quick-build: rapid prototyping allows many design-build-test cycles, e.g., software and mobile phones."],["Pharmaceutical products and space systems belong to the process variant ...",["Customized","High-risk","Platform","Quick-build"],"High-risk: technical or market uncertainties create a high probability of failure."],["Which of the following is NOT one of the three ecodesign principles in the material?",["Lowest-price approach","Whole life cycle","Product as a system","Multicriteria approach"],"The three ecodesign principles: whole life cycle, product as a system, multicriteria approach."],["The first step in Design Thinking in the material is ...",["Define","Prototype","Empathize","Launch"],"Order: Empathize, Define, Ideate, Prototype, Test and Refine, Launch."],["The letter \"R\" in SCAMPER and its Apple example is ...",["Reduce: Apple shrank the iPod Nano's capacity","Replace: aluminum replaced polycarbonate","Reverse: when everyone moved to clicks, Apple opened physical stores (bricks)","Reuse: NeXT was used by Berners-Lee to build the Web"],"R = Reverse (flip the script); the Apple example is opening retail stores. Nano/Shuffle = Modify, aluminum = Substitute, NeXT for the Web = Put to another use."],["Black & Decker is used as an example of a core technological competency because ...",["All of its production is outsourced to suppliers","It only sells products without designing them","It uses the House of Quality for all products","Almost all of its products are built on a 200-600 watt electric motor that serves several markets"],"One competency (the 200-600 watt motor) serves the home workshop, home cleaning and maintenance, and kitchen appliance markets."],["The firm-boundary model \"design in, build out\" is exemplified by ...",["A pharmaceutical firm that buys genetic targets and keeps the patents","Apple, which designs the iPhone but subcontracts fabrication while retaining IP ownership","A company that only sells, with all design and manufacturing outsourced","A company that handles all activities down to component fabrication"],"Design in, build out = Apple. The other options are orchestrated network, pure marketer, and fully vertically integrated."],["The activities of defining the product architecture, decomposing it into subsystems, and preparing a preliminary process flow for final assembly take place in ...",["Concept Development (Phase 1)","Detail Design (Phase 3)","Testing and Refinement (Phase 4)","System-Level Design (Phase 2)"],"Phase 2: product architecture defined and decomposed into subsystems; geometric layout and preliminary process flow for final assembly."],["According to the material, Toyota claims QFD helped ...",["Cut car costs by over 60 percent through shorter design times","Double sales in the US market","Eliminate all nonstandard components","Reduce car weight by 60 percent"],"QFD is credited by Toyota with cutting car costs by over 60 percent through shorter design times."],["In service design, \"complexity\" is defined as ...",["The number of steps in the service and the possible actions at each step","The number of ways the customer-provider interaction can vary at each step","The level of technology used in the service","The number of customers served per hour"],"Complexity = the number of steps and possible actions at each step; divergence is the variation in interaction at each step."],["Grocery home delivery looks like a logical extension but demands entirely new skills. This relates to the service design factor ...",["Similarity to current processes","Similarity to current services","Financial justification","Divergence"],"Slide example: grocery home delivery requires new skills (picking perishables, delivering frozen food), relating to similarity to current processes."],["The measures of product development success in the material are ...",["Number of patents, number of engineers, and R&D budget","Time-to-market, productivity, and quality (design and conformance)","Market share, share price, and NPS","Number of prototypes, number of meetings, and number of features"],"Measuring development success: time-to-market, productivity, and quality, including conformance quality in defects per million opportunities."],["In the new-product sensitivity analysis, the factor described as the most powerful lever on profit and also the most exposed if the market forecast is wrong is ...",["Development cost","Development time","Sales volume","Ramp-up cost"],"Slide: sales volume (± 25%) is the most powerful lever on profit and the most exposed to forecast errors."],["The purpose of the \"multicriteria approach\" principle in ecodesign is ...",["To include all marketing criteria in the design","To ensure the product is the cheapest on the market","To assess all environmental impacts across the life cycle together so that resource depletion is not traded for greenhouse effect or toxicity","To calculate NPV with several discount rates"],"Multicriteria: all environmental impacts across the life cycle are assessed together to avoid trade-offs among impact types."],["As of June 2018, the Shell Opportunity Hub had 229 Seekers and 16 posted projects. What is the average number of Seekers per project, and what is the diagnosis?",["About 14 Seekers per project: supply far exceeds demand","About 7 Seekers per project: demand exceeds supply","About 1.4 Seekers per project: supply and demand are balanced","About 14 projects per Seeker: too many projects"],"229 / 16 = 14.3 Seekers per project; the slide says far more supply than demand."],["A snack food maker finds that its product's properties (texture, taste) are largely determined by the frying and packaging processes. The most suitable product development process variant is ...",["Customized","Technology-push","Platform","Process-intensive"],"Process-intensive: product characteristics are highly constrained by the production process, so product design cannot be separated from process design; the slide's examples include snack foods."],["The statement consistent with the Case A (Apple) data is ...",["Post-PC devices accounted for 25% of fourth-quarter 2011 revenue","Apple always relied on conventional market research","The App Store reached 25 billion downloads nine months after launch","After 1997 the product lines were cut from 15 to 3 and 70% of new projects were eliminated"],"Slide: product lines 15 to 3, 70% of new projects eliminated; post-PC devices 76% of Q4 revenue; 1 billion downloads nine months after launch, 25 billion by March 2012."],["From the Shell Opportunity Hub case, which of the following is listed as \"what worked\"?",["Owners easily write clear project briefs","Seekers want to develop new skills, not just use skills they already have","Line managers easily release their employees","Project scope is controlled because the day job always comes second"],"What worked: reaching people outside the owner's network, Seekers wanting new skills, owners getting resources. The other options include problems (unclear briefs, managers holding back staff, scope creep)."],["Explain platform products with the Apple example and the benefits for the firm, suppliers, employees, and customers.",0,"Platform: a family of products built on the same architecture. Examples: OS X/NeXTStep on the Mac and iPhone; the iPod family; iPhone-iPod Touch-iPad. Benefits: initial investment is leveraged, derivative ramp-up is faster, shared components raise reliability and lower cost (firm and suppliers), employee knowledge carries over, and customers learn more easily."],["Explain the seven letters of SCAMPER and give an Apple action example for each.",0,"Substitute: aluminum replaced polycarbonate on the iPod Mini. Combine: iTunes + hard drive + store. Adapt: OS X adapted NeXTStep. Modify: Nano and Shuffle reduced size and capacity. Put to another use: NeXT was used by Berners-Lee to build the Web. Eliminate: the floppy disk slot was removed. Reverse: clicks versus bricks, Apple opened retail stores."],["The Shell Opportunity Hub has 229 Seekers and 118 Owners but only 16 projects. Diagnose the failure using the three service design factors and propose improvements.",0,"Three factors: similarity to current services, similarity to current processes, and financial justification. Problems: Owners struggle to write clear briefs, line managers hold back employees, scope creep, supply far exceeds demand; variability enters through unstructured briefs. Improvements: a brief template, an employee release policy, scope control, and KPIs measuring impact."],["The three main project constraints are ...",["Profit, assets, debt","Suppliers, customers, competitors","Quality, price, brand","Scope, time, cost"],"Scope, time, cost are interrelated; changing one changes the others, while quality sits in the middle and absorbs the difference."],["In a matrix project structure, each team member ...",["Reports only to the functional manager","Has no boss","Reports only to the project manager","Has two bosses: the project manager (what & when) and the functional manager (who & how)"],"The matrix combines the strengths of pure and functional structures with the consequence of two reporting lines; authority must be written down before resource conflicts arise."],["The slack of an activity is calculated as ...",["ES + duration","EF − ES","LF + EF","LS − ES or LF − EF"],"Slack = LS − ES = LF − EF. Zero slack means the activity is critical."],["A three-point estimate: a = 2, m = 5, b = 14 weeks. The expected time (TE) and the activity variance are ...",["TE = 7; variance = 4","TE = 6; variance = 4","TE = 5; variance = 12","TE = 6; variance = 2"],"TE = (2 + 4×5 + 14)/6 = 36/6 = 6; variance = ((14−2)/6)^2 = 4."],["The difference between fast tracking and crashing is ...",["Fast tracking adds resources; crashing overlaps activities","Fast tracking overlaps sequential activities with no additional direct cost but risks rework; crashing adds resources to critical activities at a higher direct cost","They are the same","Both remove scope"],"Fast tracking = parallelization (risk of rework & overallocation); crashing = adding resources (cost rises)."],["Critical activity A can be shortened from 10 to 8 weeks; normal cost is $20,000 and crash cost is $26,000. The crash cost per week is ...",["$3,000","$1,000","$2,000","$6,000"],"(26,000 − 20,000)/(10 − 8) = $3,000 per week."],["PV = $100,000; EV = $80,000; AC = $90,000. The correct interpretation is ...",["CPI = 1.12 and SPI = 1.25: under budget and ahead of schedule","CPI = 0.80 and SPI = 0.89","CV = +10,000 and SV = +20,000","CPI = 0.89 and SPI = 0.80: over budget and behind schedule"],"CV = 80 − 90 = −10,000; SV = 80 − 100 = −20,000; CPI = 80/90 = 0.89; SPI = 80/100 = 0.80. Both are < 1."],["Which of the following is NOT one of the four major types of projects according to the material?",["Product change","Process change","Daily mass-production operations","Alliances and partnerships"],"The four types: product change, process change, research and development, and alliances and partnerships; daily production is a repetitive process, not a project."],["The main weakness of the pure project structure is ...",["Each member has two bosses","The project is considered lower priority than routine work","Communication is slow and authority is unclear","Duplication of resources and specialists drifting away from their home function"],"Pure project: clear authority and fast communication, at the price of duplicated resources."],["A milestone in a WBS is ...",["The smallest work package with the longest duration","A zero-duration event that marks a decision","The activity with the largest slack","The budgeted cost of work scheduled"],"Milestones are zero-duration events marking a decision, not work."],["The critical path is ...",["The sequence of activities with the lowest cost","The sequence of activities with the largest slack","The sequence of activities done earliest","The sequence of activities that forms the longest chain in terms of time"],"CPM: the critical path = the longest chain; delays within it delay the whole project."],["The Early Start (ES) of an activity is determined by ...",["Late finish minus duration","Early finish minus slack","The largest early finish time among all its immediate predecessors","Project completion time minus the activity's duration"],"ES = the earliest time the activity can start, i.e., the largest EF of its immediate predecessors; LS = LF − duration."],["According to the material, the relationships among activities on a Gantt chart become hard to visualize when the number of activities exceeds about ...",["5","100","25","250"],"A Gantt chart is practical for small projects but hard to use when activities exceed about twenty-five."],["An SPI value below 1.0 means the project is ...",["Over budget","Ahead of schedule","Under budget","Behind schedule"],"SPI = EV / PV; below 1.0 means behind schedule. CPI below 1.0 means over budget."],["The execution method that visualizes work and limits work-in-progress for a constant but unpredictable flow of work is ...",["Kanban","Waterfall","Stage-gate","Critical chain"],"Kanban: constant, unpredictable work flow, visualized work, low bureaucracy; limitation: no delivery rhythm."],["Network: A (3 weeks) precedes B (4) and C (6); B and C precede D (2). What are the project duration and the slack of B?",["Duration 9 weeks; slack of B = 0","Duration 11 weeks; slack of B = 2 weeks","Duration 15 weeks; slack of B = 2 weeks","Duration 11 weeks; slack of B = 6 weeks"],"Path A-B-D = 3+4+2 = 9; A-C-D = 3+6+2 = 11 (critical). Slack of B = 11 − 9 = 2 weeks (LS of B = 5, ES of B = 3)."],["Activity K lasts 4 weeks and has two predecessors whose EFs are 7 and 9. What are the ES and EF of activity K?",["ES = 9; EF = 13","ES = 7; EF = 11","ES = 16; EF = 20","ES = 9; EF = 11"],"ES = the largest predecessor EF = max(7, 9) = 9; EF = ES + duration = 9 + 4 = 13."],["An activity has LF = 20, duration 5, and ES = 12. What are its LS and slack?",["LS = 15; slack = 3","LS = 15; slack = 8","LS = 17; slack = 5","LS = 25; slack = 13"],"LS = LF − duration = 20 − 5 = 15; slack = LS − ES = 15 − 12 = 3."],["An activity's three-point estimate: a = 4, m = 6, b = 14 days. What is the expected time (TE)?",["6 days","8 days","7 days","9 days"],"TE = (a + 4m + b) / 6 = (4 + 24 + 14) / 6 = 42 / 6 = 7 days."],["A critical path consists of three activities with variances 4, 9, and 3. What is the standard deviation of the critical path duration?",["16","5.33","4","3"],"Variances add along the critical path: 4 + 9 + 3 = 16; standard deviation = √16 = 4."],["Critical activity: normal time 12 weeks, cost $30,000; crash time 9 weeks, cost $36,000. What is the crash cost per week?",["$2,000","$3,000","$6,000","$500"],"(36,000 − 30,000) / (12 − 9) = 6,000 / 3 = $2,000 per week."],["PV = $60,000, EV = $50,000, AC = $40,000. The correct interpretation is ...",["CPI = 0.8 (over budget) and SPI = 1.2 (ahead of schedule)","CPI = 1.25 and SPI = 1.2: the project is healthy in every respect","CPI = 0.83 and SPI = 0.67: over budget and behind schedule","CPI = 1.25 (under budget) and SPI = 0.83 (behind schedule)"],"CPI = EV / AC = 50/40 = 1.25; SPI = EV / PV = 50/60 = 0.83."],["According to the time-cost model, the optimal project duration is ...",["The shortest duration achievable by crashing","The duration that minimizes the sum of direct and indirect costs","The normal duration without any crashing","The duration that minimizes indirect costs only"],"Direct costs rise as duration is shortened and indirect costs fall; the optimum minimizes their total, not the shortest schedule."],["A project needs to be shortened by one week. Critical activity X has a crash cost of $1,500/week, critical activity Y $900/week, and non-critical activity Z (2 weeks of slack) $500/week. Which activity should be crashed first?",["Y, because it is critical and has the lowest crash cost per week among critical activities","Z, because its crash cost is the cheapest","X, because the highest crash cost means the greatest impact","X and Y together to be safe"],"Crash only activities on the critical path with the lowest cost per period; speeding up Z, which has slack, does not shorten the project."],["The sum of TE along the critical path = 30 weeks with a path variance of 16. What is the probability the project finishes within 34 weeks (assuming a normal distribution)?",["About 50%","About 84%","About 65%","About 98%"],"SD = √16 = 4; Z = (34 − 30) / 4 = 1.0; P(Z < 1) = 0.8413, about 84%."],["The lesson from the Whoosh case about Earned Value Management is ...",["CPI and SPI are enough to decide whether to stop a project","EVM cannot be used on state infrastructure projects","An SPI above 1 guarantees the project is financially viable","CPI and SPI measure how well the plan is being executed, not whether the plan is still worth continuing"],"Case summary: Earned Value asks a narrow question; stopping metrics need to be set before investing."],["The Whoosh feasibility study used 50,000 passengers per day as the pessimistic scenario, while the actual figure was about 17,000. The conclusion the material draws is ...",["The gap is normal because of random variability in demand","A pessimistic scenario roughly three times reality shows institutionalized optimism bias, not just a statistical error","The problem lies only in the 76,000 optimistic scenario","Forecast errors do not matter because the project is technically sound"],"50,000 / 17,000 is roughly 2.9 times; slide: optimistic estimates are a risk, institutionalised optimism bias."],["Explain why crashing is done only on the critical path and on activities with the lowest cost per period.",0,"Project duration is determined by the critical path; speeding up activities with slack does not shorten the project and only wastes money. Choosing the lowest crash cost per period minimizes the increase in direct cost per unit of time. After each reduction the critical path is recalculated because a parallel critical path may appear. The optimal duration minimizes total cost (direct + indirect), not the shortest schedule."],["Choose an execution method (Waterfall, Agile/Scrum, Kanban, Hybrid) for: (a) Whiz Gambit (fixed requirements, known technology, schedule constraint), (b) Foghorn (cost, technology, and market all changing). Explain why.",0,"(a) Whiz Gambit is complicated/technically simple: requirements are clear and the technology is known, so Waterfall/CPM with milestone gates fits; focus on schedule, with limited fast tracking if needed. (b) Foghorn is complex to chaotic: stabilize with Kanban (limit WIP, visualize flow) and then Scrum with short iterations and fast feedback; rigid network planning is ineffective. Methods can be combined (hybrid) by phase."],["Why can a project that appears far under budget be misleading, and how does Earned Value Management address this?",0,"Comparing budget with actual spending does not show how much work has been completed; the project may simply not have done much work yet. EVM adds EV (the budgeted value of work actually completed), so CV = EV − AC, SV = EV − PV, CPI = EV/AC, SPI = EV/PV; an index < 1 signals over budget or behind schedule. EVM combines scope, schedule, and cost in a single measure."],["Capacity utilization rate is defined as ...",["Capacity used divided by the best operating level (same units and period)","Maximum capacity divided by demand","Output divided by input","Profit divided by assets"],"Book example: 480 / 500 = 96%."],["The best operating level is the volume at which ...",["Average cost per unit is at a minimum","Inventory is zero","Profit is maximum","Demand is highest"],"The average cost curve is U-shaped; its lowest point is the best operating level."],["Squares and circles in a decision tree represent, respectively, ...",["A chance event and a decision point","Costs and revenues","Start and end","A decision point and a chance event"],"Square = decision, circle = random event with probabilities."],["Expected annual demand is $10 million and design capacity is $12 million. The capacity cushion and utilization rate are ...",["Cushion 20%; utilization 120%","Cushion 20%; utilization 83%","Cushion 16.7%; utilization 120%","Cushion 83%; utilization 20%"],"Cushion = (12 − 10)/10 = 20%; utilization = 10/12 = 83%."],["Unlike in manufacturing, a reasonable utilization target for face-to-face services is close to ...",["100%","About 70% of maximum capacity","30%","There is no target"],"Arrivals are random and capacity cannot be stored; above ~70% queues build and service quality drops."],["The correct order of the VRIN test for sustained competitive advantage is ...",["Variable, Real, Innovative, New","Value, Revenue, Income, Net","Valuable, Rare, Inimitable, Non-substitutable","Visible, Reliable, Intangible, Neutral"],"Failing at any stage limits the outcome: disadvantage, parity, temporary advantage, or unexploited potential."],["In the Hackers Computer Store case (undiscounted), the expected values are: Large $585,000, Small without expansion $660,500, Small then expand $703,750. The optimal decision and reason are ...",["Build small then expand if demand is high, because the EV is highest ($703,750)","Build large, because of scale","Small without expansion, because it is cheapest","No decision"],"The tree is solved from right to left; choose the branch with the highest EV. With 16% discounting the ranking stays the same, with narrower margins."],["According to the material, capacity should be understood as ...",["A rate of output over a period, so the period must always be stated","A stock of finished-goods inventory","The number of permanent employees in the plant","The book value of the company's fixed assets"],"Capacity is the output a system can achieve over a period of time, a rate, not a stock."],["The intermediate-range capacity planning horizon covers ...",["More than one year","Less than one month","5 to 10 years","6 to 18 months"],"Long range is more than a year, intermediate is 6-18 months, short range is within a month."],["Economies of scope occur when ...",["Several products made together in one facility cost less than made separately","Average cost falls because the volume of a single product rises","Average cost rises because of coordination complexity","The plant pursues only one production objective"],"Economies of scope: joint production is cheaper, the opposite logic to scale."],["The focused factory concept states that ...",["A factory should excel at cost, quality, speed, reliability, and flexibility at once","The largest factory is always the cheapest","A factory works best when it pursues a limited set of production objectives","A factory should always operate at 100% capacity"],"A focused factory works best when it pursues a limited set of production objectives; no factory excels at everything."],["A plant within a plant is ...",["Building a second plant at the same site","Dividing one facility into areas with their own equipment and rules, each with its own best operating level","Outsourcing part of the volume to suppliers","Renting a competitor's capacity at peak times"],"A plant within a plant divides one facility into focused units, each with its own competitive priorities."],["A decision tree is solved by ...",["Left to right: choosing the branch with the highest probability","Summing all payoffs without probabilities","Right to left: computing the expected value at each chance node and then pruning all but the best branch at each decision point","Choosing the branch with the single maximum payoff"],"Solve from the right-hand side backwards."],["The three components of dynamic capabilities according to Teece are ...",["Sensing, seizing, transforming","Valuable, rare, inimitable","Forecast, convert, project","Eliminate, reduce, create"],"Dynamic capabilities: sensing, seizing, transforming; ordinary capabilities are best practice in running existing capacity."],["According to the material, the capacity planning increment in services is ...",["Weekly","10 to 30 minutes","Monthly","Yearly"],"Services: ten to thirty minutes (the lunch-hour rush is a capacity event); manufacturing is weekly or monthly."],["A line produces 360 units per day, while its best operating level is 400 units per day. What is the capacity utilization rate?",["111%","40%","10%","90%"],"Utilization = capacity used / best operating level = 360 / 400 = 90%."],["Expected annual demand is $8 million and design capacity is $10 million. What are the capacity cushion and utilization rate?",["Cushion 20%; utilization 80%","Cushion 25%; utilization 80%","Cushion 25%; utilization 125%","Cushion 2%; utilization 80%"],"Cushion = (10 − 8) / 8 = 25% (same logic as the slide 12 vs 10 = 20% example); utilization = 8 / 10 = 80%."],["A capacity option yields $800,000 if demand is high (p = 0.55) and $200,000 if demand is low (p = 0.45). What is its expected value?",["$500,000","$590,000","$530,000","$1,000,000"],"EV = 0.55 × 800,000 + 0.45 × 200,000 = 440,000 + 90,000 = $530,000."],["Jaguar produces 126,122 cars with 8,560 workers. Roughly how many cars per worker is that, and what is the material's point?",["About 15 cars per worker; scale is not productivity","About 68 cars per worker; scale guarantees productivity","About 1.5 cars per worker; Jaguar is the most productive","About 29 cars per worker; the same as Volvo"],"126,122 / 8,560 = 14.7, about 15; Volvo Torslanda 29 and BMW Mini 39, so scale is not productivity."],["What happens when a manufacturing operation runs above its best operating level?",["Fixed costs are spread over too small a volume","Overtime, equipment wear, and defect rates push the cost per unit back up","Cost per unit reaches its minimum","Idle capacity becomes a non-earning asset"],"Above the best level: overtime, wear and defects push unit cost back up."],["To maintain system balance when stages rarely have the same best operating level, the material suggests ...",["Adding capacity at the stage that is cheapest to buy","Reducing the capacity of all stages evenly","Ignoring bottlenecks because average utilization is sufficient","Adding capacity at the bottleneck, holding buffer inventory in front of it, or duplicating the facility it depends on"],"Maintaining system balance: add capacity at the bottleneck, hold buffer inventory in front of it, or duplicate the facility it depends on."],["Regarding location, the difference between service and manufacturing capacity planning is ...",["Services produce centrally and then distribute to customers","Services can store output to sell next month","Services can always operate near 100% utilization","Services must distribute the capacity to the customer first, then produce the service"],"Manufacturing: produce centrally, then distribute; services: distribute the capacity to the customer first, then produce."],["A resource is valuable but owned by many competitors. According to the VRIN test, the result is ...",["Sustained competitive advantage","Temporary advantage","Competitive disadvantage","Competitive parity"],"Valuable but not rare yields competitive parity. Capacity that anyone can buy at a known price is usually only parity."],["Option A: cost $200,000, payoff $900,000 (high demand, p = 0.6) or $300,000 (low, p = 0.4). Option B: cost $80,000, payoff $600,000 (high) or $400,000 (low). Which option is chosen based on net expected value?",["B, net EV $520,000 vs $460,000","A, net EV $460,000 vs $440,000","B, net EV $440,000 vs $420,000","A, net EV $660,000 vs $520,000"],"A: 0.6×900 + 0.4×300 = 660 − 200 = 460 thousand. B: 0.6×600 + 0.4×400 = 520 − 80 = 440 thousand. Choose A."],["When the Hackers decision tree is recalculated with a 16% discount rate, the correct statement is ...",["The ranking holds (small then expand wins), but all values shrink and later expansion cash flows are discounted most heavily","The ranking reverses so the large plant becomes the best choice","Values fall by less than 5 percent","Discounting increases the value of the expansion option"],"Slide: ranking holds, margins compress, timing now matters because expansion cash flows arrive later."],["Throughput at TPK Semarang in 2025 was about 1.0 million TEU against an installed capacity of 1.1 million TEU. What is the annual utilization, and what is the lesson of the Pelindo case?",["About 110%; the terminal is overloaded all year","About 91%; a 9% cushion is surely enough","About 91%; the annual figure can hide ships being turned away in peak weeks, so the period must be stated","About 10%; the terminal is largely idle"],"1.0 / 1.1 = 0.909, about 91%. Debrief: 91% annual utilization and turning ships away every peak week can happen at the same time."],["The Energy East case, in which an underused gas pipeline is converted into an oil pipeline, is categorized by the material as a dynamic capability problem of ...",["Sensing","Seizing","Transforming","Ordinary capability"],"Slide: Energy East is a transforming problem, an underused gas mainline becomes an oil pipeline; Playa Dorada is sensing and seizing."],["Explain the trade-off between adding capacity in small, frequent steps versus large, infrequent steps.",0,"Small steps: capacity tracks demand, idle capacity is low, the risk of shortage is higher, but there are repeated operational disruptions, more frequent capital calls, and no scale per step. Large steps: economies of scale and years of headroom, a single disruption, but idle capacity is costly and capital commitment is large up front. The choice depends on confidence in the forecast and which direction of error is more costly."],["Why can a service with 47% annual utilization still turn customers away every day at 9 a.m.? Explain the capacity design implications.",0,"Service capacity is perishable and must be available at the time and place the customer asks; it cannot be inventoried. The annual average hides daily peaks; the planning increment is 10-30 minutes. Implications: reprice peaks, reshape demand, extend service hours, labor flexibility; the utilization target depends on uncertainty and the cost of waiting."],["Compare Five Forces, the Resource-Based View, and Dynamic Capabilities in assessing capacity decisions. What question does each ask?",0,"Five Forces (outside-in): will the industry pay for the capacity to be built? RBV (inside-out, VRIN): is that capacity rare and hard to imitate, or merely a requirement to compete? Dynamic capabilities (sensing, seizing, transforming): can the firm reconfigure its assets if the forecast is wrong? The three complement each other; they are not rivals."],["The customer order decoupling point is ...",["The point where inventory is positioned so parts of the process can run independently and customer orders enter","The warehouse location","The break-even point","The delivery date"],"The closer it is to the customer, the faster the response but the more expensive the inventory cost."],["Dell, with 17 components yielding 384 configurations, is an example of the ... production environment",["Continuous","Make-to-stock","Engineer-to-order","Assemble-to-order"],"Modules are assembled to specification; what is forecast is the 17 modules, not the 384 configurations."],["The layout that groups similar machines together (all drilling in one area), also called a job shop, is the ...",["Assembly line","Project","Continuous process","Workcenter"],"A workcenter suits low volume, high variety."],["Options: Buy at $200/unit; semiautomatic lathe $80,000 + $75/unit; machining center $200,000 + $15/unit. The break-even points for lathe vs. buy (B) and machining center vs. lathe (A) are ...",["B = 640 units; A = 4,000 units","B = 1,067 units; A = 2,000 units","B = 640 units; A = 2,000 units","B = 400 units; A = 1,000 units"],"B = 80,000/(200 − 75) = 640; A = (200,000 − 80,000)/(75 − 15) = 2,000. Below 640 buy; 640-2,000 lathe; above 2,000 machining center."],["In the customer contact classification, a process with high contact tends to have ...",["High process efficiency, low sales opportunity","Low process efficiency, high sales opportunity","Both high efficiency and high sales opportunity","Both low"],"The greater the customer influence, the lower the efficiency; face-to-face offsets this with sales opportunity."],["In BPMN 2.0, the gateway that activates all outgoing branches simultaneously and waits for all incoming branches is the ...",["Event-based","Inclusive","Parallel","Exclusive"],"Exclusive selects one branch; parallel activates all of them. A common mistake: using parallel when exclusive should be used."],["The \"uncompromised reduction\" strategy for handling customer-introduced variability is exemplified by ...",["Lowering prices off-peak","Adding staff at peak hours","Redbox charging per day so DVDs are returned on time","Refusing certain customers"],"Uncompromised reduction uses knowledge about customers to design procedures that reduce the impact of variation without sacrificing service."],["The production environment with the fastest response but the highest inventory cost is ...",["Assemble-to-order","Make-to-order","Engineer-to-order","Make-to-stock"],"Make-to-stock serves from finished goods: fastest response, highest inventory cost."],["In engineer-to-order (e.g., Lockheed Martin's satellite division), the main constraint is ...",["Finished-goods inventory","Assembly line capacity","Availability of pre-assembled modules","Engineers' capacity"],"Engineer-to-order: the firm designs with the customer, then builds; the capacity of engineers is the constraint."],["The layout in which the product stays in place because of its size or weight and equipment is brought to it is ...",["Project","Workcenter","Manufacturing cell","Assembly line"],"Project layout: construction sites and film shooting locations."],["A gasoline refinery is an example of the ... layout structure",["Assembly line","Workcenter","Continuous process","Project"],"Continuous process: the sequence is fixed and the flow is continuous, not discrete."],["The process flow design tool that shows an exploded view of a product's components is the ...",["Assembly drawing","Assembly chart","Operation and route sheet","Flow process chart"],"Assembly drawing = an exploded view of the product showing its component parts."],["The \"Three Ts\" of service poka-yoke are ...",["Time, team, technology","Task, time, training","Task, treatment, tangibles","Trust, treatment, timing"],"Service poka-yokes are grouped into The Task, The Treatment, and The Tangibles."],["In BPMN 2.0, pool and lane mean ...",["A pool is a data store; a lane is a data object","A pool is a gateway; a lane is an event","A pool is a participant; lanes subdivide the pool by role","A pool is a task; a lane is a sub-process"],"A pool is a participant; lanes subdivide it (the role inside it)."],["Extent of customer contact is defined as ...",["The number of customers served per hour","The percentage of time the customer must be in the system relative to the total time needed to perform the service","The number of frontstage employees per customer","The frequency of customer visits per year"],"The larger the percentage, the greater the interaction and the harder the system is to control."],["Option X: fixed cost $50,000 + $30/unit. Option Y: fixed cost $110,000 + $18/unit. At what volume do they cost the same?",["5,000 units","2,000 units","3,667 units","1,667 units"],"(110,000 − 50,000) / (30 − 18) = 60,000 / 12 = 5,000 units."],["Buy a component at $40/unit, or make it in-house with a fixed cost of $24,000 + $28/unit. For a requirement of 1,500 units, the cheaper decision is ...",["Buy, because $60,000 is cheaper than $66,000","Make, because $42,000 is cheaper than $60,000","Make, because break-even is at 1,000 units","Equivalent, because 1,500 units is the break-even point"],"Buy = 1,500 × 40 = 60,000; make = 24,000 + 1,500 × 28 = 66,000. Break-even = 24,000 / 12 = 2,000 units, so below that, buy."],["In BPMN 2.0, the difference between message flow and sequence flow is ...",["Sequence flow crosses pools; message flow stays within a pool","Message flow crosses pools, whereas sequence flow stays within a single pool","They are the same and interchangeable","Message flow is used only for data stores"],"Message flow crosses pools, sequence flow does not."],["The BPMN gateway that is always followed by catching events or receive tasks, with the flow going to whichever event occurs first, is the ...",["Exclusive gateway","Parallel gateway","Inclusive gateway","Event-based gateway"],"Event-based is always followed by catching events or receive tasks; the flow goes wherever the first event happens."],["In a service blueprint, the line of visibility separates ...",["The customer from the organization during the interaction","Frontstage, visible to the customer, from backstage, which is not visible","Contact employees from support functions","Evidence from the customer journey"],"Above the line of visibility the customer can see; below it they cannot. The line of interaction and the line of internal interaction separate other things."],["On the customer contact continuum, the worker requirement for low-contact services (e.g., cheque processing) is ...",["Diagnostic, trade","Verbal, procedural","Client/worker teams","Clerical"],"Worker requirements: low contact = clerical, mixed = verbal/procedural, high contact = diagnostic/trade."],["A patient who cannot describe their symptoms is an example of customer-introduced variability of the ... type",["Capability","Arrival","Request","Effort"],"Capability: customers differ in what they are able to do; the slide's example is a patient who cannot describe symptoms."],["A mirror by the telephone to make sure the voice is smiling is an example of poka-yoke in the ... category",["Task","Tangibles","Treatment","Arrival"],"Treatment (is the manager polite?): a mirror by the phone, a scripted greeting. Task: indented surgical trays; tangibles: height bars, pictures of a clean room."],["KAI's face recognition gate handles about 14,400 people per hour, while one registration desk handles about 80 people per hour. If there are 2 registration desks, what is the capacity of the new-user lane (registration then gate) per hour?",["14,400 people per hour, because the gate determines capacity","160 people per hour, because capacity is set by the slowest resource","14,560 people per hour, the sum of both stages","80 people per hour, because there is only one gate"],"2 × 80 = 160 per hour at registration, far below 14,400 at the gate; capacity is set by the slowest resource, not the fastest."],["For arrival variability, the \"classic reduction\" strategy according to Exhibit 9.4 is ...",["Extra staff at peak times","Reservation systems","Off-peak pricing and part-time labor","Segment by arrival pattern"],"Arrival: classic accommodation = extra staff; low-cost accommodation = off-peak pricing, part-time labor; classic reduction = reservation systems; uncompromised reduction = segment by arrival pattern."],["In the BPMN diagram of the Albright case, the improvement modeled as poka-yoke is ...",["Extra instructions in the representatives' training manual","Adding a parallel gateway so all calls are escalated","A mandatory step to attach the patient record context on transfer, so escalation cannot be completed without that record","Removing the CRM system lane from the diagram"],"The escalation cannot complete unless the record travels with it; in BPMN this is a mandatory step on the gateway's outgoing path, not an instruction in a manual."],["A company moves the decoupling point from make-to-stock to assemble-to-order (further upstream). The most accurate consequence is ...",["Faster response and higher inventory cost","Response and inventory cost are unchanged","The company must design every product with the customer","Slower response to customers, but lower inventory cost because finished goods carry both material and conversion cost"],"The closer to the customer, the faster the response and the more expensive the inventory; moving it upstream trades speed for cost."],["Explain the product-process matrix and why positions off the diagonal are risky.",0,"The matrix plots volume (horizontal) and standardization (vertical); processes along the diagonal: project, workcenter, cell, assembly line, continuous. Low-volume nonstandard products suit a workcenter; high-volume commodities suit a line. Off the diagonal is not impossible but costly/inefficient; mass customization sits at the upper right, and the cell spans the widest range."],["Compare the three on-site service designs (production-line, self-service, personal-attention) with examples and their positions on the service-system design matrix.",0,"Production-line (McDonald's): service as a manufacturing process, tight specifications, everything built into the machinery. Self-service (ATM, gas station): the customer becomes a partial worker, must be trained and fail-safed, with cost/speed/convenience incentives. Personal-attention (Nordstrom, Ritz-Carlton): high customization; Nordstrom is loose and relationship-based, Ritz-Carlton is almost entirely scripted with an information system storing preferences."],["When should BPMN be used, and when a service blueprint / flow process chart? State four advantages of BPMN over ordinary flowcharts.",0,"BPMN for processes that cross organizational boundaries: ownership via pools/lanes, exception paths via boundary events, executable by a process engine, and a shared grammar. For physical flow within a single plant, an operation and route sheet is faster. A service blueprint is used for service encounters with separating lines (evidence, frontstage, backstage, support), and poka-yoke complements its design."],["The decision-making approach that describes what people actually do is ...",["Rational","Descriptive","Prescriptive","Normative"],"Descriptive = what people do; normative = what should be done (theory); prescriptive = what can and should be done."],["The three main heuristics according to Tversky and Kahneman are ...",["Anchoring, sunk cost, overconfidence","Representativeness, hindsight, confirmation","Availability, representativeness, anchoring and adjustment","Availability, framing, loss aversion"],"Tversky and Kahneman identified these three as sources of systematically biased judgment."],["A characteristic of System 1 according to the material is ...",["Automatic, effortless, associative, fast and parallel","Follows rules deductively","Slow, serial, conscious","Controlled and effortful"],"System 1 is intuitive and automatic; System 2 is reflective, slow, and effortful."],["A manager believes motorcycle accidents cause more deaths than stomach cancer because of media exposure. This bias relates to ...",["The availability heuristic (ease of recall rather than probability)","Representativeness","Anchoring","The planning fallacy"],"The ease of recalling events from the media makes them seem frequent."],["According to Gigerenzer, when do simple rules of thumb (heuristics) outperform complex models?",["Under risk: all alternatives and probabilities are known","Under uncertainty: alternatives, outcomes, or probabilities are unknown and the world is dynamic","When data is very complete","Never"],"Complex optimization models are good for risk; under uncertainty, simple robust rules are more reliable."],["The situation that most needs intuitive decision making is ...",["Ample time and complete data","Rapid change, unstructured problems, ambiguous information, and no precedent","Decisions that are easy to calculate","Decisions governed by regulation"],"Intuition suits situations that need a fast response and factors that are hard to formalize."],["If 5 machines take 5 minutes to make 5 widgets, the time for 100 machines to make 100 widgets is ...",["500 minutes","20 minutes","5 minutes","100 minutes"],"Each machine makes 1 widget in 5 minutes; 100 machines make 100 widgets in 5 minutes. The quick answer of 100 is a System 1 trap."],["Herbert Simon received the 1978 Nobel Prize in Economics for ...",["Pioneering research into the decision-making process within economic organizations","Developing game theory for negotiation","Integrating psychological research into economics on judgment under uncertainty","Discovering the SMART method for multicriteria decisions"],"The slide says Simon was awarded the 1978 Nobel for his pioneering research into the decision-making process within economic organizations."],["Daniel Kahneman (Nobel 2002) was honored for ...",["Formulating the six-step model of decision making","Introducing the concept of Simple Heuristics that Make Us Smart","Integrating insights from psychological research into economic science, especially human judgment and decision-making under uncertainty","Studying the decision process in economic organizations in 1947"],"The 2002 Nobel citation for Kahneman: integrating insights from psychology into economics concerning judgment and decision-making under uncertainty."],["According to the material, a heuristic is best described as ...",["A mathematical optimization model to find an exact solution","A big data algorithm to predict probabilities","Rules of thumb, educated guesses, intuitive judgments, or common sense","A formal procedure that guarantees the best answer"],"A heuristic is an informal method that is fast and usually close enough to the best answer: rules of thumb, educated guesses, intuitive judgments, common sense."],["Statistically, air travel is safer than motorcycle travel, but many people fear flying more. The material uses this example to explain ...",["The representativeness heuristic","The availability heuristic","Anchoring and adjustment","Bounded rationality in the consistent style"],"The Availability Heuristics (1) slide uses the plane vs. motorcycle example: events that are easy to recall are judged to be more frequent."],["The games in the first session (guess the outcome, equal-length lines, S__P) aim to show that ...",["In real decisions people have heuristics, biases, and bounded rationality, and make mistakes","People always choose the alternative with the highest expected value","Group decisions are always better than individual decisions","Mathematical ability determines decision quality"],"Slide WHY: In the actual decision making, people have heuristics, bias, bounded rationality and make mistakes."],["According to Tryfos (2001), decision making concerns methods for determining the optimal course of action when ...",["A number of alternatives are available and their consequences cannot be forecast with certainty","The consequences of all alternatives are known for certain","The decision is programmed and routine","Only one alternative is available"],"Tryfos's definition: determining the optimal course of action when a number of alternatives are available and their consequences cannot be forecast with certainty."],["According to Peter Drucker as quoted in the material, the role of a manager is ...",["Setting the vision without being involved in implementation","Delegating all decisions to the leader","Do the right thing, i.e., decide what to do","Do things right, i.e., decide how to do things"],"Leaders do the right thing (what to do), managers do things right (how to do)."],["Industry 4.0 according to the material is characterized by ...",["Steam engines, hydropower, wind, and solar","Electric power for mass production","Digital, wireless, and big data technologies massively integrated with manufacturing activities","Information technology and electronics for automated production systems"],"Industry 1.0 steam engines, 2.0 electricity for mass production, 3.0 IT and electronics for automation, 4.0 digital, wireless, big data integrated with manufacturing."],["A manager is sure a new candidate will succeed because their appearance and background resemble those of earlier successful employees. The heuristic at work is ...",["The representativeness heuristic","The availability heuristic","Illusory correlation due to ease of recall","Anchoring and adjustment"],"Representativeness: judging the likelihood of an event by its similarity to the stereotype of similar events."],["After several celebrities divorce, people associate all married celebrities with divorce. This bias is called ...",["Anchoring","Representativeness","Ease of imagination","Illusory correlation"],"The material includes illusory correlation (the celebrities and divorce example) as a bias related to the availability heuristic."],["In anchoring and adjustment, the starting value (anchor) can come from ...",["The results of a sensitivity analysis","Historical precedent, the way a problem is presented, or random information","Only verified statistical data","Normalized criteria weights"],"Slide: the starting point may be suggested from historical precedent, from the way a problem is presented, or from random information; the adjustment is often insufficient."],["How does intuition work according to the material?",["The conscious mind processes information serially, step by step","The subconscious mind links a new situation to patterns from past experience and sends a message in the language of feelings","Intuition arises from explicit probability calculations","Intuition works only when all alternatives are already known"],"The subconscious mind finds links to patterns of past experience, and the message then appears as an inner voice in the language of feelings."],["According to Gigerenzer, the risk condition means ...",["The world is dynamic so outcomes cannot be predicted","All alternatives, outcomes, and their probabilities are known","No alternatives are available","Alternatives are known but probabilities are not"],"Risk: you know all the alternatives, outcomes and their probabilities; here complex mathematical models and optimization work."],["Checkland (1993) described management as ...",["A series of routine decisions that can be automated","The art of influencing employees without planning","The process of statistically controlling production costs","The process of taking decisions in a social system in the face of problems that may not be self-generated"],"Checkland: it is the process of taking decisions in social system in the face of problems which may not be self-generated."],["Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of strategic decision making according to the material?",["Challenges the status quo, creating a political situation","A long time lag before impact, with widening uncertainty","Broad scope with cross-organizational implications","Short-term effects that are easily reversed through trial and error"],"Strategic decisions have enduring effects, possibly irreversible, with little or no scope for trial and error."],["In the What to do vs. How to do chart, the issue that belongs to What to do (framing the decision) is ...",["What problems hinder the achievement of goals","How to implement the best alternative","What alternatives are available","What criteria describe the ideal condition"],"What to do: framing the decision (context), i.e., what one wants to achieve and the criteria for the ideal condition. The other three options belong to How to do."],["A quick estimate for 8×6×2×7×4×1×3×5 tends to be larger than for 1×4×5×6×3×2×7×8 even though the result is the same. The explanation most consistent with the material is ...",["Representativeness: the first sequence resembles the stereotype of large numbers","Anchoring and adjustment: the initial numbers presented serve as an anchor and the adjustment is insufficient","System 2 works more carefully on the first sequence","Availability: the first sequence is easier to recall from the media"],"An anchor can come from the way a problem is presented; a sequence beginning with large numbers gives a high anchor and the adjustment is often insufficient."],["In the Decisions Approach chart, the role of the prescriptive approach is ...",["To bridge the gap between normative and descriptive with a systematic model of the decision process and guidance to be more rational","To assume all criteria and alternatives are already known","To describe the biases and intuition that actually occur","To replace the normative model with heuristics"],"Prescriptive contains a systematic model of the decision process and guidance to be more rational, to improve non-optimal solutions toward optimal."],["The order of the rational decision-making process on the last slide is ...",["Choose the highest option, weight the criteria, analyze options, formulate criteria","Evaluate option utilities, choose an option, then formulate criteria and weights","Analyze all options, formulate criteria and weight them, evaluate options by perceived utility, choose the highest rating","Formulate criteria, choose an intuitive option, weight, evaluate outcomes"],"Slide: consciously analyze options, formulate criteria and weights, evaluate options by perceived utility, then choose the option with the highest rating."],["A participant spontaneously answered 100 minutes on the widget problem, then recalculated and got 5 minutes. This correction is best described as ...",["Anchoring that was over-adjusted","System 1 replacing System 2 because it is faster","Controlled, deductive System 2 correcting the automatic answer of System 1","Intuition drawing on patterns of past experience"],"The spontaneous answer comes from System 1 (automatic, fast); recalculating is System 2 (controlled, effortful, rule application)."],["Explain the difference between System 1 and System 2 and when each is likely to cause decision errors.",0,"System 1: intuitive, automatic, fast, associative, with a non-transparent process; prone to heuristics and biases (the 8×6×2... vs. 1×4×5... problem, line illusions, widgets). System 2: reflective, serial, controlled and effortful; accurate but slow and cannot be the main actor. Errors arise when System 1 is used for problems that need analysis, or System 2 is used in situations that demand a fast response."],["Explain the three Tversky-Kahneman heuristics with business decision examples.",0,"Availability: judging probability by how easily examples come to mind (an engineer imagines every cause of delay and so overestimates the risk). Representativeness: judging likelihood by similarity to a stereotype. Anchoring and adjustment: an estimate starts from an initial value (historical or random) and the adjustment is usually insufficient. All can give quick estimates but with systematic bias."],["Why is decision making considered a core competency of leaders, and what characterizes a strategic decision?",0,"Leaders determine what to do and how; decision making is the process of determining both. Strategic decision: broad scope, complex and interrelated, long-term and often irreversible effects, a long lag before impact, disagreement on direction, and challenges the status quo, making it political."],["The decision-making style that is fast, without gathering all the information, is ...",["Reflective","Consistent","Reflexive","Systematic"],"Reflexive: decisive but can cause waste and duplication."],["A problem exists when ...",["Goals are not achieved: there is a gap between the actual and the desired condition","The budget runs out","There is conflict","There are many alternatives"],"Problem-solving is corrective action; decision making chooses the action that solves the problem."],["The Reflective style, whose downside is ...",["It can procrastinate and miss opportunities and waste time and resources","It ignores data","It is hasty","It is always wrong"],"Reflective is thorough but can procrastinate."],["Programmed vs. non-programmed decisions is a classification based on ...",["Type (rational/bounded)","Level (individual/group)","Condition (certainty/risk/uncertainty)","Decision-making structure"],"Four classification dimensions: structure, condition, level, and type of decision."],["In setting criteria, the difference between MUST and WANT is ...",["MUST is mandatory; WANT is a preference that is weighted","WANT is mandatory, MUST is optional","They are the same","MUST applies only to costs"],"An alternative that fails a MUST is not viable (no go); WANTs are weighted with the Kepner-Tregoe method."],["The techniques used to generate creative alternatives are ...",["Break-even, ROI, NPV","Brainstorming, synectics, nominal grouping, consensus mapping, Delphi","Gantt, PERT","SWOT, PEST"],"These five techniques are named in the generate creative alternatives stage."],["Decisions often fail to be carried out mainly because ...",["There are too few alternatives","There is no detailed plan (when, what, who) and no control method","There is too much data","There is too much discussion"],"Stage six of the model: plan, implement, and control with checkpoints."],["The decision-making style that is not rushed, does not waste time, and knows when enough information and resources are available is ...",["Intuitive","Reflexive","Consistent","Reflective"],"Consistent style: makes decisions without rushing or wasting time; they know when they have enough information and resources."],["The three levels of decision making in the material are ...",["Strategic, tactical, and operational","Individual, group, and organizational learning","Personal, managerial, and multiparty decision making","Programmed, non-programmed, and bounded"],"Slide Level of Decision Making: personal, managerial, multiparty."],["According to the material, innovation is ...",["The Delphi technique for generating alternatives","The implementation of a new idea","A way of thinking that produces new ideas","The incubation stage of the creative process"],"Creativity is a way of thinking that generates new ideas; innovation is their implementation."],["When performing the organizing function, the decisions a manager must make are ...",["What goals to achieve, when, and where","What to delegate and how to coordinate the department's resources","Methods for monitoring progress and corrective actions","How to influence employees"],"Organizing: decide what to delegate and how to coordinate the department's resources."],["The definition of decision making as a formalization of common sense was put forward by ...",["North (1968)","Simon (1947)","Wang and Ruhe (2004)","Hansson (1994)"],"Definition slide: North (1968) called decision making a formalization of common sense."],["The stages in the creative process according to the material are ...",["Define, generate, implement","Brainstorming, synectics, Delphi","Preparation, incubation and illumination, evaluation","Planning, organizing, leading"],"Stages in the creative process: Step 1 Preparation, Step 2 Incubation and Illumination, Step 3 Evaluation."],["A programmed decision is characterized as ...",["Nonsignificant, recurring, routine, with a shorter time to decide","Always made under uncertainty","Always requiring quantitative techniques","Significant, nonrecurring, and not routine"],"Decision structure continuum: programmed = nonsignificant, recurring, routine, shorter time to make decisions."],["The positive side of the reflexive style is ...",["Having the best track record following the model","Gathering the most complete information","Being decisive and not procrastinating","Considering many alternatives"],"Reflexive: POSITIVE decisive, does not procrastinate; NEGATIVE waste and duplication."],["On the decision condition continuum, the uncertainty end means ...",["The outcomes of alternatives are unpredictable and the chance of making a poor decision is greater","The probability of each outcome is known for certain","Decisions are routine and recurring","The outcomes of alternatives are predictable and the chance of a poor decision is small"],"Uncertainty: the outcome of alternatives is unpredictable, with greater chances of making a poor decision; certainty is the opposite."],["Qualitative (intuition) techniques are best suited to ...",["Group decisions that follow all steps of the model","Significant non-programmed decisions under risk and uncertainty","Decisions requiring linear programming","Nonsignificant programmed decisions under certainty and low risk, generally individual decisions"],"Continuum table: qualitative technique for nonsignificant programmed decisions under certainty and low risk, may skip steps, commonly individual."],["An example of a mixed technique on the continuum of analysis techniques is ...",["Queuing theory","Intuition","Linear programming","Cost-benefit analysis"],"Mixed technique: cost-benefit analysis, based on objective information or mathematics together with subjective judgment."],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an impact of different decision-making styles?",["Data gathering","The size of the organization's budget","Interpretation of data","The number of alternatives considered"],"Impact of different styles: data gathering, interpretation of data, number of alternatives, the way the group interacts."],["In the set objectives and criteria step, one criteria-weighting method named in the material is ...",["The Kepner-Tregoe method","Nominal grouping","Break-even analysis","The Delphi method"],"Weighting criteria: one method to weight the criteria is the Kepner-Tregoe method."],["The role of the decision tree in step 4 of the decision-making model is ...",["To set up checkpoints to control implementation","To generate creative alternatives like brainstorming","To classify the problem as programmed or not","To visualize alternatives and also serve as a tool to help select an alternative"],"Step 4 slide: Decision Tree is one way to visualize alternatives, not to mention a tool to help select an alternative."],["According to the material, control methods such as checkpoints should be developed ...",["Only if the decision is programmed","After the decision fails to be implemented","Before the problem is classified","During planning, to measure and monitor the decision"],"Control methods should be developed while planning to measure and monitor the decision (e.g., checkpoints)."],["A characteristic of quantitative techniques according to the continuum table is ...",["Decisions based on experience and subjective judgment","May skip steps of the model and are generally individual","Used only under certainty","Objective decisions based primarily on mathematics, following all steps of the model, generally group decisions"],"Quantitative: the decision is objective, based primarily on math; used for significant nonprogrammed decisions under uncertainty and risk; follows all steps; commonly group."],["A warehouse supervisor reorders consumable supplies each week with the same pattern. The most appropriate classification and technique are ...",["Non-programmed decision; Delphi technique","Programmed decision; must use all steps of the model and big data","Programmed decision; qualitative technique and may be made individually","Non-programmed decision; quantitative technique by a group"],"Routine, recurring decisions are programmed; the table specifies a qualitative technique for nonsignificant programmed decisions, which can skip steps and are generally individual."],["The board decides to enter a new market it has never entered and whose results are hard to predict. The most suitable combination is ...",["A non-programmed decision under uncertainty, analyzed with quantitative or mixed techniques by a group","A programmed decision under certainty with individual intuition","A programmed decision under risk with qualitative techniques","A non-programmed decision under certainty with intuition"],"A significant, nonroutine decision is non-programmed; for risk and uncertainty the table recommends quantitative or mixed techniques, which are generally group decisions."],["The correct pairing of a decision-making model step and its activity is ...",["Step 4: brainstorming to classify the problem","Step 3: using the Delphi technique to generate alternatives","Step 1: determining criteria weights with Kepner-Tregoe","Step 6: setting MUST and WANT criteria"],"Delphi is one of the generate creative alternatives techniques (step 3). Criteria weighting and MUST/WANT belong to step 2; control is in step 6."],["According to the material, criteria are ...",["A detailed plan of when, what, and who","The standards an alternative must meet to be selected as the decision that will accomplish the objective","The final result the organization wants to achieve","A list of alternatives generated from brainstorming"],"Criteria are the standards that an alternative must meet to be selected as the decision that will accomplish the objective; criteria set performance levels."],["List the six steps of the decision-making model and the function of each step.",0,"1) Classify and define the problem; 2) Set objectives and criteria (must/want, weights); 3) Generate creative and innovative alternatives; 4) Analyze alternatives and select the most feasible (decision tree, quantitative/qualitative/mixed techniques); 5) Plan and implement the decision; 6) Control/monitor with checkpoints and corrective action."],["Compare the reflexive, reflective, and consistent styles, including their strengths and weaknesses, and their impact on data gathering and group interaction.",0,"Reflexive: fast, decisive, but wasteful and duplicative. Reflective: unhurried, but procrastinates and misses opportunities. Consistent: neither rushes nor wastes time, follows the model with the best track record, but it is hard to be sure information and alternatives are sufficient. Style affects data gathering and interpretation, the number of alternatives, and how the group interacts."],["Explain the relationship between management functions (planning, organizing, leading, controlling) and problem solving and decision making.",0,"Every function requires decisions: planning (goals and means), organizing (delegation and coordinating resources), leading (how to influence employees), controlling (monitoring and choosing corrective action). Problem solving closes the gap to goals; decision making chooses the action."],["The three criteria of KT Situation Analysis are ...",["Timing, trend, impact","Is, is not, distinction","Must, want, risk","Cost, quality, time"],"Each problem is rated H/M/L on timing, trend, impact to determine priority and process (PA/DA/PPA)."],["The score in KT Decision Analysis is calculated as ...",["Weight × Rating","Probability × Cost","Weight + Rating","Rating / Weight"],"Weight 1-10 for the importance of the want, rating 0-10 for how well the alternative satisfies it."],["An alternative that fails to meet any one of the MUSTs is declared ...",["Rescored","Go","No go","Given a low weight"],"MUSTs are mandatory; failing even one means no go (the Gun Ho example)."],["In the spray gun example, Paint Right gets ratings (2, 8, 3, 9) and New Spray (9, 6, 7, 2) on Easy service, Durability, Low cost, Experience with weights (7, 6, 4, 4). Their total scores are ...",["Paint Right 110; New Spray 110","Paint Right 110; New Spray 135","Paint Right 120; New Spray 125","Paint Right 135; New Spray 110"],"Paint Right: 14+48+12+36 = 110. New Spray: 63+36+28+8 = 135."],["In KT Problem Analysis, the column that is most important for finding the cause is ...",["IS","Extent","IS NOT","Distinction (what is different between IS and IS NOT)"],"The difference between IS and IS NOT points to possible causes; dimensions: what, where, when, extent."],["In the \"Really Bad Day\" example, the problems rated H on timing, trend, and impact and processed with Decision Analysis are ...",["Broken car","Damaged briefcase","Dog bites and house fire","Tornado only"],"Dog bites and house fire have three H's; the dog wins on impact and possibly trend, so it is first in order."],["Potential Problem Analysis (PPA) focuses on ...",["Preventing future problems with potential causes, preventive actions, and contingent actions","The causes of past problems","Choosing the best alternative","Prioritizing problems"],"PA = past (what went wrong), DA = present (how to choose), PPA = future (prevention)."],["After the real problem has been defined and solutions generated, the three decisions in Deciding the Course of Action are ...",["Determine weights, ratings, and scores","Define the problem, generate solutions, and evaluate solutions","Decide which problem to work on first, choose the best alternative solution, and decide how to implement it","Identify symptoms, find the root of the problem, and punish the cause"],"Slide: 1) decide which problem to work on first; 2) choose the best alternative solution; 3) decide how to successfully implement the solution."],["The key question of Situation Analysis in the KT approach is ...",["Where are we?","How do we prevent future faults?","How do we correct the fault?","What is the fault?"],"Situation Analysis (Where are we?) then branches to PA, DA, and PPA."],["The question \"Is a deadline involved?\" belongs to the evaluation criterion ...",["Trend","Impact","Timing","Process"],"Timing: how urgent is the problem, is a deadline involved, what will happen if nothing is done."],["The question \"What are the effects on people, products, or the organization?\" belongs to the criterion ...",["Trend","Timing","Distinction","Impact"],"Impact: how serious is the problem and what are its effects on people, products, or the organization."],["KT Problem Analysis is most useful for ...",["Choosing the best alternative from several options","Determining the weights of want criteria","Preventing problems that have not yet occurred","Troubleshooting when the cause of the problem is unknown"],"Slide: useful for troubleshooting, where the cause of the problem is not known."],["In root cause analysis, ROOT causes are paired with ... action",["Corrective","Containment","Contingent","Preventive"],"Observed symptoms: containment action; direct causes: corrective action; root causes: preventive action."],["According to the material, the 5 Whys technique can be used ...",["Individually or as part of a fishbone diagram","Only on programmed problems","Only after KT Decision Analysis is complete","Only to calculate weight × rating scores"],"Slide: The 5 Whys can be used individually or as a part of the fishbone diagram."],["The quote \"A problem clearly defined is a half problem solved\" on the iceberg slide comes from ...",["Kepner and Tregoe","Herbert Simon","Ralph Keeney","Charles Kettering"],"The Iceberg of Symptom and Problem slide quotes Charles Kettering, GE."],["In the six-step Problem Solving and Decision Making Model, steps 5 (analyze the outcome) and 6 (improve the process) are part of ...",["Potential Problem Analysis","Decision Analysis","Problem Analysis","Situation Analysis"],"Step 2 = Problem Analysis, steps 3-4 = Decision Analysis, steps 5-6 = Potential Problem Analysis."],["The four stages of Situation Analysis in order are ...",["Set priority, identify concerns, plan involvement, plan next step","Identify concerns, set priority, plan next step, plan involvement","Identify concerns, plan next step, set priority, evaluate","Define problem, generate solutions, decide, implement"],"The SA chart: 1 identify concerns, 2 set priority (H/M/L), 3 plan next step (PA/DA/SA/PPA), 4 plan involvement."],["In the plan next step stage, a problem is taken to Problem Analysis (PA) when ...",["There is a deviation, the cause is unknown, and the cause needs to be known","Further clarification is needed","A plan is needed to protect against a problem","A choice needs to be made among several options"],"SA chart: PA for deviation, cause unknown, need to know cause; DA for choice; SA for further clarification; PPA for action or plan to protect."],["A damaged briefcase is rated M, M, H on timing, trend, impact. The KT process assigned is ...",["Decision Analysis","No processing needed","Problem Analysis","Potential Problem Analysis"],"Briefcase: timing and trend moderate, impact high (does not want to lose the job), next: PPA."],["In the Really Bad Day example, the tornado's rating and subsequent process are ...",["H, H, H with process DA","M, H, H with process DA or PPA","M, M, H with process PPA","L, L, M with process PA"],"Tornado: timing moderate (don't know its direction yet), trend high, impact high; next Decision Analysis or Potential Problem Analysis."],["According to the slide, a good problem statement includes ...",["What is known, what is unknown, and what is sought","Musts, wants, and risks","Weight, rating, and score","Timing, trend, and impact"],"A good problem statement includes: what is known, what is unknown, what is sought."],["The correct statement about WANTS in KT Decision Analysis is ...",["Wants are used only in Problem Analysis","Wants are never weighted","Wants are desirable but not mandatory","Wants must be met for a solution to be a go"],"Musts are mandatory; wants are desirable but not mandatory and are weighted 1-10."],["In the IS/IS NOT table for the flight attendant rash case, the WHERE dimension has IS: new aircraft, IS NOT: old aircraft. The distinction is ...",["Differences in the flight attendants' duties","External contact","Differences in materials","Differences in crew procedures"],"The WHERE row: IS new aircraft, IS NOT previous aircraft, distinction difference in material, possible cause: the material of the new aircraft."],["Spray gun example: want weights Easy service 7, Durability 6, Low cost 4, Experience 4; ratings Paint Right (2, 8, 3, 9) and New Spray (9, 6, 7, 2). If the weight of Durability is raised to 10, the total scores become ...",["Paint Right 134 and New Spray 159","Paint Right 142 and New Spray 159","Paint Right 110 and New Spray 135","Paint Right 142 and New Spray 147"],"Paint Right: 14+80+12+36 = 142. New Spray: 63+60+28+8 = 159. New Spray still comes out ahead."],["In the spray gun example (Easy service weight 7; Paint Right total 110 with an Easy service rating of 2; New Spray 135), the minimum Easy service rating (integer 0-10) for Paint Right's total to exceed New Spray's is ...",["9","6","5","4"],"The gap is 135 − 110 = 25; each rating increase adds 7. A rating of 5 gives 110 + 21 = 131, a rating of 6 gives 110 + 28 = 138 > 135."],["Two alternatives are scored with KT. The want weights are 8, 5, 3. Ratings for X = 6, 9, 4 and Y = 9, 4, 8. However, Y fails one of the MUSTs. The correct decision is ...",["Re-weight until Y wins","Choose X with a score of 105, because Y is no go even though its score is 116","Choose Y because its score of 116 is higher","Choose X because its score is 116"],"X = 48+45+12 = 105; Y = 72+20+24 = 116. An alternative that fails one MUST is no go, so its score is not compared."],["After the dog bites and the house fire, the next priority order of problems in the Really Bad Day example is ...",["Damaged briefcase (4), tornado (5), then broken car (2)","Tornado (5), broken car (2), then damaged briefcase (4)","Tornado (5), damaged briefcase (4), then broken car (2)","Broken car (2), damaged briefcase (4), then tornado (5)"],"Slide: After that, it would seem we'd rank them in the order 5, then 4 and then 2 (tornado M/H/H, briefcase M/M/H, car L/L/M)."],["Explain the three KT processes (PA, DA, PPA) and the key question of each.",0,"Situation Analysis sorts out concerns and priorities. Problem Analysis: what went wrong and its cause (avoid jumping to causes). Decision Analysis: how to choose the best solution, balancing benefits and risks. Potential Problem Analysis: how to prevent future problems (avoid reactive action)."],["Apply 5 Whys and KT Problem Analysis to the case of the new aircraft's flight attendants with red rashes; state the likely true cause.",0,"IS: red rash on flight attendants of the new aircraft, only some attendants, routes over sea/water. IS NOT: old aircraft, land routes. Differences: new aircraft materials, procedures, duties. Likely cause: new materials/new brand different from the old model (root cause, controllable). Verify by testing each possible cause against the facts; the solution fixes the problem, not the symptom."],["Outline the KT Decision Analysis steps (musts, wants, weights, ratings, risks) for choosing a spray gun.",0,"Write the decision statement; set the MUSTS (flow-rate control, paint appearance) and weighted WANTS (easy to service, durable, cheap, personnel experience); screen with the musts (Gun Ho is a no go); rate 0-10 and compute weight x rating (New Spray 135 vs Paint Right 110); do a risk assessment (probability and seriousness 0-10) before deciding."],["Alternative-focused thinking (AFT) starts from ...",["Determining values and objectives","Setting the budget","Looking for the visible alternatives and then choosing the best","Drawing up the criteria"],"AFT is called constrained thinking: a problem arises when there is more than one alternative."],["Fundamental objectives are ...",["Risks","Alternatives","The ways to achieve objectives","The final objectives in a decision context"],"Means objectives are the means of achieving fundamental objectives."],["Value-focused thinking (VFT) is called ...",["Reactive thinking","Constrained thinking","Constraint-free thinking","Linear thinking"],"VFT starts by defining values and criteria, then creates alternatives that fit them."],["To move down the hierarchy from a fundamental objective, the question to ask is ...",["'Of what more general objective is this an aspect?'","'Why is this important?'","'What do you mean by that?'","'How could you achieve this?'"],"Fundamental hierarchy: downward 'What do you mean by that?', upward 'Of what more general objective...'. Means: away from fundamental 'How could you achieve this?', toward fundamental 'Why is this important?'."],["The complete set of 'Smart Choices' elements is ...",["Strength, weakness, opportunity, threat","Problem, objectives, alternatives, consequences, tradeoffs, uncertainty, risk tolerance, linked decisions","Define, measure, analyze, improve, control","Plan, do, check, act"],"The eight PrOACT-URL elements."],["The main difference between AFT and VFT is ...",["AFT solves problems faster with the price paid later; VFT requires mental effort and time but the benefits should be worth it","AFT is always better","VFT does not require values","There is no difference"],"VFT produces alternatives that fit values better but demands upfront effort."],["In John's car-purchase case, comparing only the Karimun, Savvy, and New Aerio is an example of AFT. With VFT, John would first ...",["Compare prices","Postpone buying","Identify what he wants to achieve (long-term fuel consumption, insurance, size; taking the family on picnics) and then look for alternatives such as the Xenia or Avanza","Choose the cheapest car"],"Alternatives are generated from values, not the other way round."],["According to the material, a good decision process emphasizes ...",["Speed of deciding over quality","As many alternatives as possible","What you decide, not how","How you decide, not what you decide"],"Slide: practice and improve this skill by learning a good decision making process (emphasizing how you decide, not what you decide)."],["The saying If you don't know where you're going, you might end up somewhere else is used to stress ...",["The central role of objectives in decision making","The importance of probability in a decision tree","The need for break-even analysis","The danger of anchoring"],"Slide Objectives: The Basics: objectives play a central role in decision making (value-focused thinking)."],["An objective is defined as ...",["A statement of something one desires to achieve in a decision context","A list of visible alternatives","Consequences that cannot be controlled","An attribute measured in rupiah"],"Objectives is a statement of something that one desires to achieve for a decision context."],["According to the slide Values help guide strategic thinking, strategic objectives are ...",["Valid for only one decision","Stable over time, while the means to achieve them change","The same as means objectives","Changed every time the alternatives change"],"Strategic objectives are stable over time although the means to obtain these objectives vary over time."],["From the internet commerce examples list, the one that is a means objective is ...",["Assure system security","Minimize cost","Maximize privacy","Maximize safety"],"Assure system security is in the list of means objectives; the other three are fundamental objectives (Keeney, 1999)."],["The internet commerce fundamental objectives examples in the material come from ...",["Goodwin and Wright, Decision Analysis for Management Judgment, 2000","Bazerman, Judgment in Managerial Decision Making, 1994","Keeney, The Value of Internet Commerce to the Customer, Management Science, 1999","Kepner and Tregoe, The New Rational Manager, 1997"],"The slide cites Keeney, Management Science 45, 1999, pp 533-542."],["Prof. Ralph Keeney's (1996) quote about alternatives is ...",["Alternatives are the starting point of every decision","The best alternative is always the cheapest","Value emerges after alternatives are compared","Alternatives are merely a means to achieve your values better"],"VFT slide: alternatives are merely a means to achieve values better (paraphrased from Keeney 1996)."],["In the Why bother? part of the VFT slide, the reasons to apply VFT are ...",["To speed up decisions without thinking about values","To avoid the need to evaluate alternatives","To reduce the objectives to just one","To recognize decision opportunities, create better alternatives, and have an enduring set of guiding principles for life"],"Why bother: recognize and identify decision opportunities, create better alternatives, enduring set of guiding principles for your life."],["In Guidances to be more rational, the step accurately assess each alternative based on each criterion is part of ...",["Situation analysis","Decision analysis","VFT","AFT"],"Steps 1-4 (define problem, identify criteria, weigh criteria, know alternatives) are VFT; steps 5-6 are decision analysis."],["In the chart How do we generate alternatives? for the car case, the fundamental objective is ...",["Xenia, Avanza, Ertiga, Livina","Maximize activity with friend","Have new car","Maximize quality of life"],"Decision context: have new car; criteria such as maximize available time with family; fundamental objective: maximize quality of life."],["In the ideal job VFT map, professional, compensation, and social serve as ...",["Means objectives","Fundamental objective","Attributes","Alternatives"],"Ideal job = fundamental objective; professional, compensation, social = means objectives; starting salary and the like = attributes; Research Institute and the like = alternatives."],["On the VFT In Practice slide, descriptively, the approach that dominates when someone loses a job, applies, and chooses a job is ...",["Alternative-focused thinking","Value-focused thinking","Kepner-Tregoe","SMART"],"Descriptively: alternative-focused thinking prevails; the slide suggests using VFT to identify decision opportunities and create additional alternatives."],["According to the higher-order thinking (HOT) skills slide, critical thinking, creative thinking, problem solving, and decision making require ...",["The Delphi technique","Big data and complex algorithms","Break-even analysis","Problem-structuring methods, namely VFT"],"The HOT chart leads to requiring problem-structuring methods, then VFT."],["In the car-case VFT Map, the attribute for measuring minimize maintenance cost is ...",["Annual number of mechanical problems and accidents","Annual number of activities with friends","Annual maintenance and repair costs at the garage","Purchase price of a new car"],"Minimize maintenance cost is broken down into repair and upkeep costs, measured by annual maintenance and repair costs at the garage."],["The difference in step order between AFT and VFT on the comparison slide is ...",["AFT determines values first, VFT identifies alternatives first","Neither evaluates alternatives","AFT identifies alternatives before determining values, whereas VFT determines values and then creates alternatives","VFT does not start from recognizing the problem"],"AFT: recognize problem, identify alternatives, determine values, evaluate, choose. VFT: recognize problem, determine values, create alternatives, evaluate, choose."],["According to the material, what is NOT among the places thinking about values leads to is ...",["Uncover hidden objectives","Eliminating the need to evaluate alternatives","Improving communication","Guiding information collection"],"The chart mentions: improving communication, involving multiple DAs, uncover hidden objectives, creating alternatives, identify decision opportunities, guiding strategic thinking, inter-connecting decisions, guiding information collection."],["According to the slide, articulating fundamental objectives for specific decisions helps you ...",["Guide decisions and create decision opportunities in general","Speed up decisions at no mental cost","Better understand the decision and create fruitful alternatives","Eliminate the need for strategic objectives"],"The slide distinguishes: clarify strategic objectives helps guide decisions and create decision opportunities; articulate fundamental objectives for specific decisions helps better understand the decision and create fruitful alternatives."],["The correct pair (fundamental objective, means objective) according to the internet commerce example is ...",["(Minimize fraud, Maximize product quality)","(Assure reliable delivery, Minimize cost)","(Maximize shopping enjoyment, Limit impulsive buying)","(Enhance comparison shopping, Maximize convenience)"],"Maximize shopping enjoyment is in the fundamental list; limit impulsive buying is in the means list. The other options swap the fundamental and means positions."],["A procurement manager only compares the three vendor offers received without first formulating what the company needs. According to the material, this is ...",["Value-focused thinking because there is more than one alternative","Constrained alternative-focused thinking; they should define values first","Constraint-free thinking because vendors are free to bid","Already-rational decision analysis steps 5-6"],"Just like John comparing the Karimun, Savvy, New Aerio without knowing the values he wants: that is AFT (constrained thinking). VFT starts from defining values."],["In the car-case VFT Map, the indicator annual number of mechanical problems and accidents measures the means objective ...",["Maximize quality of life","Minimize the number of problems (mechanical and incidental)","Maximize enjoyment of life","Minimize maintenance cost"],"The minimize number of problems branch is split into mechanical problems and incidental matters, measured by the annual number of mechanical problems and accidents."],["Explain the difference between fundamental objectives and means objectives with the internet commerce example.",0,"Fundamental: maximize product quality, minimize cost, minimize time to receive product, maximize shopping enjoyment, privacy, safety, convenience. Means: minimize fraud, assure reliable delivery, maximize access to product information, limit impulsive buying; forming a means-ends network toward the fundamental objectives. The question 'Why is this important?' moves from means to fundamental."],["Why can VFT produce better decisions than AFT? State other benefits of VFT.",0,"VFT makes objectives clear so alternatives are created to achieve them, not limited to the visible ones. Other benefits: uncovering hidden objectives, guiding information collection, linking decisions, involving multiple decision makers, improving communication, and guiding strategic thinking. The price is time and mental effort."],["In the repair vs replace case of a 1992 Camry (repair Rp12 million, new car Rp120 million with Rp3 million/month installments), explain why break-even analysis is not enough without values.",0,"Rp12 million divided by 4 months = Rp3 million shows the monthly cost break-even point, but does not answer what is really wanted from a car (reliability, image, family). Without defining objectives, the decision only compares existing alternatives (AFT). Need to define the problem, criteria, weights, and relevant alternatives."],["SMART stands for ...",["Simple Measurable Achievable Relevant Time-bound","Simple Multi-Attribute Rating Technique","Strategic Management and Reporting Tool","Systematic Multi-Alternative Ranking Technique"],"A compensatory MCDM method by Edwards (1971), a simplification of MAUT."],["The number of main SMART stages is ...",["5","8","10","6"],"Eight stages, from identifying the decision maker to sensitivity analysis."],["In direct rating, the best alternative is given the value ... and the worst ...",["50 and 0","10 and 1","100 and 0","1 and 0"],"Other alternatives are rated between 0 and 100; suitable for subjective attributes such as image."],["The original weights of six attributes are Closeness 100, Visibility 80, Image 70, Size 30, Comfort 20, Car parking 10 (total 310). The normalized weights of Closeness and Visibility are ...",["0.32 and 0.26","0.40 and 0.30","0.10 and 0.08","0.25 and 0.20"],"100/310 = 0.32; 80/310 = 0.26."],["Dago's values: Closeness 40, Visibility 60, Image 90, Size 100, Comfort 60, Parking 70; weights 0.32; 0.26; 0.23; 0.10; 0.06; 0.03. The total weighted value is ...",["72.4","80.8","64.8","55.0"],"12.8+15.6+20.7+10+3.6+2.1 = 64.8 (the slide shows 64.8)."],["The value function (bisection method) is used to ...",["Calculate costs","Choose locations at random","Assign weights","Convert actual values (for example, area) into the decision maker's preference values 0-100"],"Example: v(400) = 0; v(550) = 30; v(800) = 55; v(1,000) = 75; v(1,500) = 100."],["In trading benefit vs cost, moving from Mohammad Toha (Rp6,700; 47.4) to Lingkar Selatan (Rp12,000; 60.2) gives a cost per point of about ...",["$5,300 per point","$1,117 per point","$414 per point","$100 per point"],"Cost difference 5,300 divided by benefit difference 12.8 = 414. If the decision maker's extra value per point is < 414, choose Mohammad Toha; otherwise Lingkar Selatan."],["According to the material, the SMART approach was designed to ...",["Calculate expected value in a decision tree","Provide an easy way to implement the origins of MAUT","Screen alternatives with musts and wants","Replace all MCDM methods"],"Slide SMART: this approach was designed to provide an easy way to implement the origins of MAUT."],["SMART is widely applied because ...",["It always yields an objectively optimal solution","It does not need attribute weights","It needs only one attribute","It is relatively simple and transparent, so it is easy to apply and understand"],"Slide: widely applied because of its relative simplicity and transparency; decision makers from various backgrounds can apply it and understand the recommendations."],["In the office location case, the decision maker is ...",["All restaurant employees","The business owner","The city government","A property consultant"],"Stage 1: In our problem is the business owner."],["How many locations does the restaurant owner consider in the SMART case?",["7","8","6","5"],"The owner considers seven locations: Braga, Buah Batu, Mohammad Toha, Soekarno Hatta, Dago, Veteran, Lingkar Selatan."],["In SMART, the term value describes ...",["The number of attributes in the value tree","The subjective desirability of the corresponding attribute level","The location's annual rent","The probability that an attribute occurs"],"Slide Value Function: Value describes subjective desirability of the corresponding attribute level."],["The three initial relevant attributes in the office location case (Stage 3) are ...",["Rent cost, quality of working condition, and size","Timing, trend, and impact","Closeness, image, and parking only","Probability, seriousness, and risk"],"Stage 3 mentions attribute 1 rent cost, attribute 2 quality of working condition, attribute 3 size deserve to consider."],["In the benefits value tree, the turnover branch consists of ...",["Image, comfort, and size","Rent, electricity, and cleaning","Size, comfort, and car parking","Closeness to customer, visibility, and image"],"Benefits: turnover (closeness to customer, visibility, image) and working condition (size, comfort, car parking)."],["The three meanings of sensitivity analysis according to the SMART material are ...",["Ranking, rating, and normalization","Plots, surveys, and interviews","Stability of solutions when weights change, a measure of robustness, and a study of the effect of input changes on output","Screening musts, weighting, and computing scores"],"Sensitivity analysis is: stability of solutions, robustness, input perturbation to output."],["The original attribute weights: Closeness 100, Visibility 80, Image 70, Size 30, Comfort 20, Car parking 10. The normalized weight for Image is ...",["0.26","0.23","0.70","0.10"],"Total 310; 70/310 = 0.2258, rounded to 0.23."],["Buah Batu's values: Closeness 20, Visibility 80, Image 10, Size 30, Comfort 100, Car parking 30. With weights 0.32; 0.26; 0.23; 0.10; 0.06; 0.03, its aggregate weighted value is ...",["36.9","41.2","39.4","47.4"],"6.4 + 20.8 + 2.3 + 3 + 6 + 0.9 = 39.4."],["Mohammad Toha: rent 5,000, cleaning 1,000, electricity 700 per year. The total cost used in the trading benefit vs cost stage is ...",["7,700","6,000","5,000","6,700"],"5,000 + 1,000 + 700 = 6,700, matching the total cost table on the slide."],["From the bisection interview: v(400)=0; v(550)=30; v(700)=50; v(800)=55; v(1,000)=75; v(1,500)=100. The area the owner regards as the preference midpoint between 400 and 1,500 ft2 is ...",["950 ft2","800 ft2","550 ft2","700 ft2"],"The preference midpoint has value 50; v(700) = 50, not the arithmetic midpoint 950."],["In the direct rating of image, Soekarno Hatta gets 20 and Lingkar Selatan 10. The correct interpretation is ...",["We cannot say that Soekarno Hatta's image is twice as preferable as Lingkar Selatan's","A 10-point difference means a 10 percent difference in rent","Lingkar Selatan is the location with the worst image","Soekarno Hatta's image is exactly twice as preferable"],"Slide IMAGE: We cannot say that image of Soekarno Hatta is twice as preferable as that Lingkar Selatan. The value 0 belongs to Mohammad Toha."],["The SMART advantage of interdependence means ...",["Weights are calculated from the best alternative","The model applies to only one alternative","The decision model is independent of the alternatives","All attributes depend on each other"],"Pros: Interdependence - the decision model is independent of the alternatives."],["When identifying attributes, the material mentions a tradeoff between ...",["Cost and benefit","Ranking and rating","Operationality and decomposability","Completeness and minimum size"],"Slide Stage 3: it takes tradeoff between completeness & minimum size (all relevant attributes vs a small number to keep analysis easy)."],["In the provisional decision stage, for the cost attribute ...",["Best is the highest value and worst the lowest","Cost has no best and worst","Best is the lowest value and worst the highest value","Best and worst are determined at random"],"Slide Stage 7: best (lowest) cost, worst (highest) cost; for benefit best (highest), worst (lowest)."],["Lingkar Selatan (cost 12,000; benefit 60.2) and Braga (cost 35,000; benefit 80.8) are on the efficient frontier. The cost per additional benefit point from Lingkar Selatan to Braga is about ...",["$1,117 per point","$414 per point","$652 per point","$1,700 per point"],"(35,000 - 12,000) / (80.8 - 60.2) = 23,000 / 20.6 = 1,116.5, about $1,117 per point."],["The owner values moving from the worst to the best image (23 points) at $15,000. With the cost per point C to G of $414 and G to A of $1,117, the location chosen is ...",["Lingkar Selatan","Braga","Mohammad Toha","Dago"],"Extra value = 15,000/23 = $652 per point. Since 652 > 414, move from Mohammad Toha to Lingkar Selatan; since 652 < 1,117, do not move to Braga."],["In the aggregate benefit vs total cost graph, the efficient frontier is formed by the locations ...",["Veteran, Buah Batu, and Dago","Dago, Braga, and Soekarno Hatta","Mohammad Toha, Lingkar Selatan, and Braga","Mohammad Toha, Soekarno Hatta, and Dago"],"The efficient frontier line connects C (Mohammad Toha), G (Lingkar Selatan), and A (Braga); the other locations lie below the line."],["Soekarno Hatta's values: Closeness 70, Visibility 50, Image 30, Size 55, Comfort 30, Car parking 90. With weights 0.32; 0.26; 0.23; 0.10; 0.06; 0.03, its aggregate weighted value is ...",["60.2","52.3","55.1","47.4"],"22.4 + 13 + 6.9 + 5.5 + 1.8 + 2.7 = 52.3."],["List the eight SMART stages and explain what is done in stages 4-6.",0,"1) Identify decision maker; 2) Identify alternatives; 3) Identify attributes; 4) Assess performance of each alternative per attribute (direct rating or value function); 5) Determine attribute weights (rank swing from worst to best, 100 for the most important, normalize); 6) Compute aggregate weighted value (sum of value x normalized weight); 7) Provisional decision (trade benefit vs cost, efficient frontier); 8) Sensitivity analysis."],["State the advantages and disadvantages of SMART.",0,"Advantages: unity (linear value function), applicability, interdependence (model independent of alternatives), operation (only lowest-level attributes), relevance. Disadvantages: complexity grows with the number of criteria, accuracy (less good alternatives may be screened out), sensitivity (scale range not handled), consistency (subjectivity). Hence sensitivity analysis is needed."],["In the sensitivity analysis of the restaurant location case, how does the weight of the turnover attribute affect the choice, and what does it mean?",0,"As long as the total turnover weight is < 52.1, Dago is the most attractive; above 52.1, Braga is the most attractive. This means the decision is sensitive to the turnover weight; the decision maker must be sure of the weights and understand the stability and robustness of the solution to input changes."],["In a decision tree, a square represents ...",["A decision node (a choice the decision maker can control)","A payoff","An event node","A probability"],"A circle = event node (uncontrollable; probabilities p1, p2, p3)."],["The key assumption of decision analysis with a decision tree is ...",["The probabilities of consequences can be estimated","All outcomes are certain","There is no risk","There is only one alternative"],"Suitable for uncertainty when probabilities are available."],["The three graphical tools of sensitivity analysis are ...",["Pie, bar, line","Pareto, fishbone, histogram","Plot graph, spider graph, tornado graph","Gantt, PERT, CPM"],"Built with the Sensit software."],["The correct steps to build a decision tree are ...",["Draw nodes without decisions","First decision, states of nature after each decision, draw nodes, complete probabilities and payoffs","Probabilities first, then decisions","Payoffs, probabilities, nodes, decisions"],"Identify decisions, SON, draw nodes, probabilities, payoffs."],["A tornado diagram is used to ...",["Choose criteria","Analyze parameters with different degrees of variability against the expected payoff","Draw the tree","Calculate NPV"],"Spider graph: parameter changes of ±10% against the expected payoff; plot graph: the effect of prior probabilities."],["A decision tree is solved by ...",["Right to left: compute the expected value at each chance node and then choose the best branch at each decision node","Choosing the largest payoff","Choosing the largest probability","Left to right without EV"],"Backward induction by rolling back EV."],["Alternative A: payoff 100 (p 0.6) and 20 (p 0.4). Alternative B: certain 70. The decision based on expected value is ...",["A (EV 68), choose A","Equal, EV 70","A (EV 68) is lower than B (70): choose B","A (EV 80), choose A"],"EV(A) = 0.6x100 + 0.4x20 = 68; B = 70. So B."],["The course objective of the decision tree topic is the ability to ...",["Choose the best alternative among the available alternatives by identifying their consequences","Determine attribute weights with swing weights","Eliminate all uncertainty","Find the root of problems with 5 Whys"],"Slide: ability to choose the best alternative among available alternatives by identifying their consequences."],["In the simple decision tree on the Problems of Choice slide, O1 and O2 respectively represent ...",["Two event probabilities","A desirable outcome and an undesirable outcome","Two action alternatives","Two decision makers"],"What the symbols mean: O1 desirable outcome; O2 undesirable outcome."],["According to the material, decision analysis is designed to handle ...",["Routine decisions without uncertainty","Prioritization using timing, trend, impact","Interpersonal conflict in negotiation","Decision making in the face of great uncertainty"],"Slide: Decision analysis is designed to address decision making in the face of great uncertainty."],["In the example of choosing a mix of crops and livestock for next season, the main uncertainty is ...",["The presence of oil","Competitors' reactions","Interest rates","Weather conditions"],"Example selecting the mix of crops and livestock: What will be the weather conditions?"],["The decision node in a decision tree structure contains ...",["The final payoff of each branch","Events that cannot be controlled","The alternatives available and controllable by the decision maker","The probability of each event"],"Structure of decision tree: alternatives available for decision maker to choose; situation controllable by decision maker."],["The first step in building a decision tree is ...",["Drawing the event nodes","Completing the payoff information","Identifying what decisions the decision maker must make, including the first and subsequent decisions","Completing the probability information"],"Building decision tree: 1) identify decisions; 2) identify SON; 3) draw nodes; 4) probabilities; 5) payoff."],["In sensitivity analysis, parameters are ...",["Alternatives that can be controlled","Payoffs that are already certain","The decision makers involved","Key parameters that may change in the future"],"Slide Sensitivity Analysis: Parameters - key parameters that may change in the future."],["In the decision analysis model, P(i,j) represents ...",["The payoff of alternative i","The probability of event E(i,j)","The j-th action alternative","The i-th decision maker"],"Key elements: D decision maker, C alternatives, O outcomes, E(i,j) events (SON), P(i,j) probabilities."],["A decision is called strategic or interactive when ...",["There is only one alternative","Probabilities cannot be estimated at all","The consequences of each option depend on the actions and reactions of the other side","Complete information is available from the start"],"Complex rational choice: the consequences of each option depend on the actions and reactions of the other side (Strategic/Interactive Decision)."],["In the securities investment example, the uncertainty questions mentioned in the material are ...",["How is the economy and what about interest rates","How much oil is there","How will prospective customers react","What are the weather conditions"],"Investing in securities: How is the economy? How about interest rates?"],["Why is an event node said to contain incomplete information?",["Because events always occur before actions","Because payoffs are never known","Because the decision maker only has probability information about each event","Because the decision maker can choose the event"],"Event node: only probability information is available, meaning no complete information; events cannot be controlled by the decision maker."],["In the Model of Decision Analysis diagram (2 alternatives, each with 2 events), the number of probabilities P(i,j) to fill in is ...",["6","2","8","4"],"There are P(1,1), P(1,2), P(2,1), P(2,2): 2 alternatives x 2 events = 4."],["Product launch: success (p = 0.3) payoff 500, failure (p = 0.7) payoff -100. Not launching has payoff 0. Based on expected value, the decision and its value are ...",["Launch, EV = 80","Do not launch, EV = 0","Do not launch, because the chance of failure is 0.7","Launch, EV = 200"],"EV(launch) = 0.3x500 + 0.7x(-100) = 150 - 70 = 80 > 0."],["Oil drilling: oil found (p = 0.25) payoff 800, dry (p = 0.75) payoff -300. Not drilling has payoff 0. The decision based on expected value is ...",["Do not drill, because the EV of drilling = -25","Drill, because EV = 500","Drill, because EV = 200","Do not drill, because the EV of drilling = -100"],"EV(drill) = 0.25x800 + 0.75x(-300) = 200 - 225 = -25 < 0."],["The sensitivity analysis tool for seeing the change in expected payoff when one parameter (cost or revenue) changes by up to plus or minus 10% is ...",["Plot graph","Value tree","Spider graph","Tornado diagram"],"Spider graph: how the expected payoff would change if one of parameters changes (the cost or revenues) by up to plus or minus 10%."],["According to the slide, the output studied by sensitivity analysis is ...",["The priority order of problems","How changed assumptions affect the recommended decision","Which decision maker is most influential","The minimum number of alternatives"],"Sensitivity analysis: the study of how different assumptions about future (parameters) would affect the recommended decision."],["Stock investment: boom (p = 0.3) payoff 400, normal (p = 0.5) payoff 100, recession (p = 0.2) payoff -300. The bond alternative gives a certain payoff of 90. The decision and its EV are ...",["Bonds, because 90 is safer than the stock EV of 66.7","Bonds, because the stock EV = 80","Stocks, EV = 200","Stocks, EV = 110"],"EV(stocks) = 0.3x400 + 0.5x100 + 0.2x(-300) = 120 + 50 - 60 = 110 > 90."],["Three alternatives: Expand (high demand p = 0.6 payoff 300; low p = 0.4 payoff -100), Wait (high 0.6 payoff 180; low 0.4 payoff 60), Sell (certain 120). By rolling back expected value, the best choice is ...",["Wait with EV 132","Expand with EV 140","Sell with value 120","Expand with EV 200"],"EV(Expand) = 180 - 40 = 140; EV(Wait) = 108 + 24 = 132; Sell = 120. Choose the highest value at the decision node: Expand."],["Alternative A: payoff 100 with probability p, or 20 with probability 1 - p. Alternative B: certain 70. At what value of p do A and B have the same expected value?",["0.5","0.625","0.6","0.7"],"100p + 20(1 - p) = 70, so 80p = 50 and p = 0.625. If p > 0.625 choose A; if lower choose B. This is an example of sensitivity analysis on probability."],["A student computes the value of an event node by taking the highest payoff of its branches. The mistake is ...",["An event node cannot be controlled, so its value must be the expected value (sum of probability x payoff)","The lowest payoff should have been taken","The probabilities should have been added without payoffs","There is no mistake because the decision maker chooses the best branch"],"Choosing the best branch applies only at a decision node. An event node is uncontrollable and only its probabilities are known, so it is valued with expected value."],["Explain the components of the decision analysis model (D, C, E, p, O) and the function of a decision tree.",0,"D = decision maker; C = courses of action (alternatives); E = events/state of nature; p = probability of the event; O = outcomes/payoff. The tree visualizes the sequence of decisions, events, and consequences so the expected value can be calculated and the best alternative chosen."],["What is sensitivity analysis and why is it important in a decision tree?",0,"It studies how different assumptions or parameters about future conditions affect the recommendation. It is important because the future is uncertain; it shows whether the decision is robust. Plot graphs, spider graphs, and tornado diagrams can be used to identify the most influential parameters."],["Why does a decision tree help with 'complex rational choice' when information is incomplete and outcomes are influenced by others? Give example problems from the material.",0,"The information needed to predict consequences may not yet be available, and consequences depend on the actions/reactions of others (strategic/interactive decision). Assuming probabilities can be estimated, the tree forces you to write down alternatives, events, probabilities, and payoffs, then choose the best alternative. Examples from the material: launching a new product (customer and competitor reactions), securities investment (economy and interest rates), choosing crops and livestock (weather), oil drilling (chance of finding oil and its amount)."],["Business analytics is defined in the material as ...",["Producing monthly reports","Building dashboards","Collecting as much data as possible","Using records of what happened to make the next decision better"],"Only analytics ends in a decision; reporting and BI answer what happened."],["The four types of analytics questions, in order, are ...",["Reporting, BI, AI, ML","Descriptive, predictive, diagnostic, prescriptive","Descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, prescriptive","Prescriptive, predictive, diagnostic, descriptive"],"What happened, why, what will happen, what should we do."],["Data that has an order but unequal distances between values (bronze, silver, gold) is of type ...",["Ratio","Nominal","Interval","Ordinal"],"Ordinal can be counted and ranked; the mean is not appropriate."],["In the Walmart and Hurricane Frances case, the change that was made was ...",["Buying as much stock as possible","Raising prices","Loading trucks based on what was actually bought during Hurricane Charley, not managers' expectations (beer and Pop-Tarts showed up)","Closing stores"],"The decision was based on earlier ticket receipts."],["Temperature in Celsius and calendar year are of data type ...",["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval (zero does not mean none)","Ratio"],"Interval: equal distances but zero does not mean none. Ratio: zero means none (sales value, distance)."],["The difference between observation, insight, and decision according to the material is ...",["A decision is just an observation","An insight is a decision","An observation states what is seen; an insight explains (usually naming a cause); a decision is an action with an owner, a date, and a measure","All three are the same"],"If a finding does not change an action, it is still an observation."],["A store has high customer traffic between 12:00 and 14:00 but transactions drop by more than half. The correct diagnosis and decision are ...",["Customers were recorded incorrectly","The single cashier takes a break at 12:00 and the queue makes customers leave; move the break to 15:00","Demand is simply low at midday","Prices are too high"],"Diagnostic finds the cause; the decision changes the break time and midday transactions rose the following week."],["In the example of leaving for the airport, the 'Answer' step contains ...",["Allow 70 minutes","The traffic app","Leave at 14:20","Not missing the flight"],"Five steps: Data = traffic app, Question = when to leave, Answer = allow 70 minutes, Decision = leave at 14:20, Result = catch the flight."],["What data did Walmart use to decide what to load on trucks ahead of Hurricane Frances?",["A survey of store managers about expected needs","Till receipts from Hurricane Charley a few weeks earlier","Weather data and storm track forecasts","Commodity prices from suppliers"],"Walmart used till receipts from Hurricane Charley in stores in a similar situation, so trucks were loaded based on what was actually bought."],["On average, how many addresses does a UPS driver visit per day according to the material?",["About 40 addresses","About 300 addresses","About 120 addresses","About 1,000 addresses"],"UPS slide: every driver visits about 120 addresses a day, and the visiting order was originally left to the driver."],["Products that were clues to pregnancy in the Target case included ...",["Diapers, formula, and strollers","Vitamin C, masks, and wet wipes","Beer and strawberry Pop-Tarts","Unscented lotion, calcium and magnesium supplements, and extra-large cotton balls"],"Target inferred pregnant customers from ordinary purchases such as unscented lotion, calcium and magnesium supplements, and extra-large cotton balls."],["According to the material, data produced by others and then bought, shared, or taken from public sources (for example weather and competitor prices) is called ... data.",["External","Internal","Transactions","Traces"],"Internal is produced by your own operations (sales, payroll); external is produced by others, for example weather, commodity prices, census, competitor prices."],["Data that has one row for each thing that exists, such as a customer, employee, or branch, is part of the ways data is produced called ...",["Observation","Records","Transactions","Traces"],"Records: one row for each thing that exists. Transactions: one row every time something happens."],["The question 'Why did it happen?' belongs to the ... type of analytics.",["Descriptive","Predictive","Diagnostic","Prescriptive"],"Descriptive: what happened; diagnostic: why did it happen; predictive: what will happen; prescriptive: what should we do."],["The methods that count as descriptive analytics according to the material are ...",["Optimisation under constraints and scenario simulation","Regression, classification, and machine learning","Segmentation and peer comparison","Counting, totals and shares, comparison over time, and breakdown by category"],"Descriptive: counting, totals and shares, comparison over time, breakdown by category. Optimisation is prescriptive, regression is predictive, segmentation is diagnostic."],["Store 14 sold 1,240 bottles last week compared with about 890 per week before. Further analysis shows that three days account for about 270 of the 350 extra bottles. This second finding is ...",["Diagnostic, because it breaks the total increase into its parts","Descriptive, because it only counts sales","Predictive, because it estimates next week's sales","Prescriptive, because it recommends a stock allocation"],"The material uses the finding '270 of the 350 extra bottles happened on three days' as an example of diagnostic: breaking a total into parts."],["The output of predictive analytics according to the table 'The four, side by side' is ...",["A report","An estimate with a range","A ranked list of causes","A recommended action"],"Descriptive: a report; diagnostic: a ranked list of causes; predictive: an estimate with a range; prescriptive: a recommended action. Example: 900 to 1,300 bottles, midpoint 1,100."],["Sales wants a price of 48, Finance wants 54. Analysis shows maximum profit at a price of 50. This is an example of ... analytics.",["Descriptive","Diagnostic","Prescriptive","Predictive"],"Prescriptive example 'what price to set': profit peaks at 50, and both proposals move away from it in opposite directions."],["A store with one cashier sees transactions fall by more than half between 12:00 and 14:00 even though visitor traffic stays high. Which part is the 'insight'?",["Peak sales happen in the afternoon","The break time was moved to 15:00","Midday transactions rose the following week","The only cashier takes a midday break, so the queue makes customers leave"],"An insight explains the cause (the cashier's break at 12:00). Peak afternoon sales is only an observation; moving the break is a decision; the rise in transactions is a result."],["In the table 'Why the type matters', the data type that can be counted and ranked but not properly averaged is ...",["Ordinal","Nominal","Interval","Ratio"],"Nominal: count only. Ordinal: count and rank, average not properly. Interval: count, rank, average. Ratio: everything, including ratios."],["In the data type mini quiz, 'the number of items in a shopping basket' is of type ...",["Nominal","Ratio","Ordinal","Interval"],"The number of items has equal distances and zero means no items, so it is ratio. Cold storage temperature in Celsius is interval."],["The question 'Which number will show that it worked, and when do we look at it?' is part of the question ...",["So what?","Now what?","How do we know?","What could go wrong?"],"So what: meaning and size of the impact; Now what: action, who, when; What could go wrong: risk; How do we know: the number that shows success."],["The belief 'An accurate model is a useful model' is corrected in the material to ...",["An accurate model always produces the best decision","Accuracy only matters to the data team","Models must be built with as much data as possible","A model that no existing process can act on changes nothing"],"Fact on the slide: a model that no existing process can act on changes nothing."],["In the power outage case, outage minutes rose 18% and an upgrade of the whole network was proposed. Diagnostic analysis found that seven of every ten outage minutes came from equipment more than 20 years old. The most appropriate implication is ...",["Focus repairs on the old equipment, not a whole-network upgrade","A whole-network upgrade is still needed because of the 18% rise","No action is needed because the cause is known","Build a failure prediction model before acting"],"The diagnostic finding shows the cause is concentrated in equipment over 20 years old, so the proposed full upgrade can be replaced with more targeted action."],["A depot asks to buy three trucks because route C is always late. Prescriptive analysis suggests moving three trucks from routes A and D. Why is this prescriptive rather than predictive?",["Because it uses historical lateness data","Because the result is a recommended action under fleet constraints, not an estimate of what will happen","Because it explains the causes of route C's lateness","Because it counts the total trucks per route"],"Prescriptive answers 'what should we do' through optimisation under constraints; the result is that the fleet does not have to grow."],["Target's analysis of pregnant customers was accurate, yet the material calls it 'still the wrong thing to do'. The main lesson is ...",["Target's analysis was actually inaccurate","Loyalty card data must not be used for analytics","An accurate analysis does not automatically make the action appropriate; the impact on customers must be considered","Analytics only suits operational decisions"],"Slide 29: the analysis was correct, it was still the wrong thing to do; Target then disguised baby offers among other offers."],["The BetonPrima case: sales in West Java fell for three quarters, three people gave three explanations, and none could be checked against records. This problem is closest to which way decisions are usually made?",["Decisions based on prescriptive optimisation","Decisions based on an outdated prediction model","Decisions that already use a data dictionary","Decisions from 'the voice in the room' and seniority that are hard to check"],"The material: decisions are often made from memory, from the room, from last year, from seniority; none is wrong, all are hard to check. BetonPrima illustrates that situation."],["Distinguish reporting, business intelligence, and business analytics.",0,"Reporting: periodic reports on what happened, received passively. BI: dashboards to investigate what happened and where. Business analytics: answers what to do, with a recommended action; the user decides and is accountable for the result. Only analytics ends in a decision."],["Why can Target's accurate analysis still be the wrong thing to do? What is the lesson for managers?",0,"Target predicted pregnancy from purchases (unscented lotion, supplements, large cotton balls) and sent coupons; a father complained because his teenage daughter received baby coupons. The analysis was right but the privacy and reputation impact was ignored. Lesson: ask 'What could go wrong?' in addition to 'So what? Now what? How do we know?'; the decision and its risk belong to the manager."],["Explain the four misconceptions about analytics and the correct facts.",0,"(1) Analytics starts from data: actually from the decision you want to improve. (2) More data is better: what matters is data that fits the question. (3) Analytics is the data team's job: problem framing and ownership of results stay with the manager. (4) An accurate model is surely useful: a model that no existing process can act on changes nothing."],["Which of the following is NOT one of the six things a column's metadata must have (name, meaning, unit, source, refresh, owner)?",["Source, refresh, and owner","Name","The company's share price","Meaning and unit"],"The six: name, meaning, unit, source, refresh, owner."],["A key in a table is ...",["A column or combination of columns that distinguishes each row from the others","The first column","The value column","The date column"],"Without a key, the same thing can be recorded twice without anyone noticing."],["Data such as call recordings and project photos is ...",["Structured","Nominal","Semi-structured","Unstructured"],"Unstructured has no fields; most organizational data is not rows and columns."],["On-time delivery rose from 80% to 84%. The correct statement is ...",["Up 4 percentage points, or 5% relatively","Up 20%","Up 4%","Up 0.4 points"],"4/80 = 5% relative; both are correct but only one is meant."],["The average order is 340 bags, the median is 120 bags. The correct explanation is ...",["The mean is always better","A few very large orders pull the mean upward; nine out of ten orders are smaller than the mean","The data is wrong","The median is always wrong"],"When a column is skewed, the mean describes almost no one; include the median, spread, count, and shape."],["The three places to fix a data problem and their owners are ...",["Capture, report, audit","Definition, storage, archive","Capture (process owner), definition (whoever chairs the meeting that argues about the number), pipeline (data team)","Pipeline, dashboard, cloud"],"Fixing at capture is cheapest and fixes all future rows; the pipeline is never finished."],["If Marketing counts 840,000, Finance 610,000, and Operations 1,200,000 'active customers', the problem and solution are ...",["Three unwritten definitions of 'active'; agree on one definition and write it in the data dictionary","A new server is needed","Someone miscounted","Data is missing"],"Undefined: this is management work, not data work."],["In the five-row table of orders SO-4471 to SO-4473, how many orders are there?",["Three orders","Five orders","Two orders","One order"],"Five rows, three orders, two days; the number of customers cannot be known from the table."],["If one row is one order line, the question answered is ...",["How large is a typical order?","Which products move together?","Who is buying from us?","How busy is the fleet?"],"Order line (41,800 rows): which products move together. Order (18,400): order size. Customer (3,150): who is buying."],["A complaint form containing a date, branch, and one free-text paragraph is ... data.",["Structured","Unstructured","Semi-structured","Metadata"],"Semi-structured: some fixed fields, some free, for example a complaint form with date, branch, and paragraph."],["The metadata element 'Refresh' of a column describes ...",["Who to contact if the number looks wrong","The system and field the data came from","The unit the column uses","How often the data changes and how old it is now"],"Six things: name, meaning, unit, source, refresh (how often it changes, how old now), owner (who to ask)."],["According to the material, the definition of a term such as 'active customer' should live in ...",["A data dictionary that reports refer to","The head analyst who builds the report","The footnote of one slide","Emails between divisions"],"Not in the analyst's head, not in the footnote of one slide, in a dictionary that the report itself points to."],["The material's quote about a report without metadata is ...",["That report is always wrong","That report cannot be checked, so it has to be believed","That report is faster to read","That report can still be audited from its numbers"],"A report without metadata cannot be checked, so it has to be believed."],["Why is it wrong to average branch numbers 3, 7, and 12 to get 7.3?",["Because the rounding is wrong","Because the median should be used","Branch numbers are nominal; there is no branch 7.3 and branch 12 is not four times branch 3","Because there is too little data"],"The data type determines the calculation; branch numbers are just names with no meaningful order."],["The data defect when a report mixes yesterday's despatch with last year's branch list is called ...",["Duplicate","Wrong grain","Inconsistent","Stale"],"Stale: out-of-date data with no sign; first move: print the age of every source on the report."],["The date 06/11/2023 can mean 6 November in Jakarta but 11 June in New York. This shows that dates ...",["Are not plain text; the format depends on a country's convention","Can be summed like numbers","Have no time zone","Are always the same as the input date"],"Dates are not text (formats differ between countries), not numbers (can be subtracted, not summed), carry a zone, and come in pairs."],["A transaction at 23:40 in Makassar is recorded as the previous day in Jakarta. The aspect of dates illustrated is ...",["Dates come in pairs","Dates carry a time zone","Dates must not be summed","Date formats differ between countries"],"They carry a zone: a 23:40 transaction in Makassar (WITA) is the previous day in Jakarta (WIB)."],["Average marketing spend per branch is IDR 14 million, yet 30 of 40 branches spend nothing. What is the average of the 10 branches that spend?",["IDR 14 million","IDR 35 million","IDR 56 million","IDR 140 million"],"Total = 14 x 40 = 560 million, divided by 10 branches = 56 million. Lesson: count the zeros before you divide by the rows."],["One customer is recorded as C-1042 'CV Karya Baja', C-3318 'CV. Karya Baja', and C-7756 'KARYA BAJA CV'. The recommended first step is ...",["Delete two rows at random","Leave it because the names differ","Average the sales targets of the three","Match on a tax number, then prevent new duplicates at the entry screen"],"Duplicate: match on a tax number, then stop new duplicates at the entry screen; first move: find a key that does not vary."],["The plant reports 1,240 tons, the depot 31,000 bags of 40 kg for the same shipment. The fix recommended by the material is ...",["Put the unit in the column name, for example value_idr_thousand","Use the plant's figure because it is smaller","Delete one of the sources","Average the two figures"],"Wrong unit: put the unit in the column name; fixed at definition."],["One order is worth forty times the other orders. The right action according to the material is ...",["Delete the row because it spoils the average","Read the record first, then correct it, or keep it and explain","Replace it with the average value","Divide its value by 40"],"Outlier: read the record before touching the number; never remove a row because it spoils the average."],["Deleting rows whose customer number is blank (cash sales) risks ...",["Creating duplicate data","Changing the column's unit","Deleting your smallest customers","Making the data stale"],"Missing: ask why it is blank, fill from the source or report separately; deleting the rows with blanks deletes your smallest customers."],["According to the table 'The eight, side by side', the 'wrong grain' defect is fixed at ...",["Capture, with the entry screen","Pipeline, by the data team","It does not need fixing","Definition, with one grain per table"],"Wrong grain: first move one grain per table, fix it at definition; the region total is computed, not stored beside the branches."],["Branch Bandung 340, Cirebon 260, Bekasi 200, and a West Java row of 800 are in the same column. If the column is summed the result is ...",["1,600 tons, because West Java is counted twice","800 tons, the correct total","1,400 tons","1,200 tons"],"340 + 260 + 200 + 800 = 1,600; the region total is mixed with the branches (wrong grain), so West Java is counted twice."],["Changing the definition of 'active customer' to 'at least one paid invoice in the last 12 months, in all reports' is a fix at the definition level. Who owns it and what does it cost?",["The data team; never finished","Whoever chairs the meeting where the number is argued about; cheap to write but expensive to agree","The process manager; cheapest and fixes all following rows","The analyst; free and quick"],"Definition: owned by whoever chairs the meeting where the number is argued about; costs nothing to write, a great deal to agree."],["Why is fixing data in the pipeline called 'repair work' that does not stop the leak?",["Because the data team has no access to the systems","Because the pipeline always deletes outliers","The same cleaning must be repeated every month for every new row","Because fixes in the pipeline are the most expensive to write"],"Pipeline: never finishes, the same cleaning runs again every month on every new row; a fix at capture prevents bad values from the start."],["Of the five problems in the table 'Which of these is yours', how many are NOT the data team's to fix?",["One of five","Five of five","None","Three of five"],"Slide 71: three of these five are not the data team's to fix. Owners include the process manager, management, commercial, and the report designer; the data team holds the spelling of names in the pipeline."],["List the eight data defects (eight ways a table lets you down) and the first move for two of them.",0,"Missing, duplicate, inconsistent, wrong unit, stale, wrong grain, outlier, undefined. Missing: ask why it is blank (do not delete the rows because that deletes the smallest customers). Duplicate: match on a key that does not change, such as a tax number. Outlier: read the record before changing the number; do not delete it because it spoils the average."],["Why is 'average satisfaction of 3.7 out of 5' problematic and what replaces it?",0,"A 1-5 scale is ordinal; the distances between categories are not known to be equal, so the average is not appropriate. Use the percentage answering good or better, because it is a count, which is safe."],["What should a manager ask for and ask back when receiving data?",0,"Ask for: the decision it will change, what one row is (customer/order/shipment), the period, columns with meanings, definitions of ambiguous words. Ask back: how old the extract is, how many rows were dropped, the source system and whether another system differs, who to contact if something looks odd."],["The three types of business decisions according to the material are ...",["Individual, group, team","Financial, marketing, HR","Strategic, tactical, operational","Short, medium, long"],"They differ in time horizon, complexity, and management level."],["The order of the DIKW pyramid from bottom to top is ...",["Data, Information, Knowledge, Wisdom","Wisdom, Knowledge, Information, Data","Information, Data, Wisdom, Knowledge","Data, Knowledge, Information, Wisdom"],"Wamena example: 20, 25, 22 degrees -> average temperature -> August is warm -> festival in August."],["Data-driven decision making (DDDM) is ...",["Decisions purely from intuition","Storing data","Using data to inform and validate actions before they are implemented","Decisions made by data scientists"],"DDDM is based on objective evidence, not just assumptions."],["The relationship between data and intuition according to the material is ...",["Data replaces intuition","They conflict with each other","They complement each other: intuition raises questions, data corrects bias; intuition warns when data is incomplete","Intuition is always better"],"Together they form a feedback loop."],["Descriptive analytics answers ...",["Why it happened","What happened, when, how often","What will happen","What should we do"],"It summarizes historical data to see patterns and trends."],["Choosing the right chart size and type and avoiding clutter aims to ...",["Highlight the main message and prevent confusion","Decorate the report","Add color","Reduce data"],"Clutter is unnecessary elements that obscure the insight."],["The Jennifer Aaker quote in the data storytelling material: a story is ...",["Has no effect","22 times more memorable than facts alone","2 times more memorable","The same as facts"],"Data storytelling combines analysis, visualization, and narrative."],["The decision to set the goal of becoming a market leader is a ... decision.",["Strategic","Tactical","Operational","Diagnostic"],"Strategic: senior managers, long-term, complex; for example setting the goal of becoming a market leader."],["The decision to launch a new product or open a new branch is usually made by ...",["Senior managers","Middle managers","Operational staff","Shareholders"],"Tactical decision: middle managers, medium-term, less complex than strategic."],["The first stage of decision making according to the material is ...",["Choosing the best alternative","Assessing the probability of consequences","Identifying alternative courses of action","Implementing the decision"],"Order: identify alternatives, identify consequences, assess probability, choose the best, implement."],["Decision making that compares the current situation with similar events in the past is called ...",["Intuition-based decision making","Data-driven decision making","Prescriptive analytics","Experience-based decision making"],"Experience-based draws upon past experiences, comparing current situations to similar past events."],["According to the material, how many bytes of data are generated every day, making data increasingly accessible to businesses of all sizes?",["More than 2.5 quintillion bytes","About 2.5 million bytes","About 2.5 billion bytes","More than 25 terabytes"],"DDDM opportunities: over 2.5 quintillion bytes created daily."],["In the Wamena DIKW example, '20°C, 25°C, 22°C' is at the level of ...",["Information","Data","Knowledge","Wisdom"],"Raw temperature numbers = data; average temperature of the first week of August = information; August tends to be warm = knowledge; holding the festival = wisdom."],["The definition of data visualization according to the material is ...",["The process of cleaning data before analysis","A technique for predicting future data","A graphical representation of information and data to see trends, outliers, and patterns","Storing data in tabular form"],"Data visualization is the graphical representation of information and data, using charts, graphs, and maps."],["In the context of data storytelling, clutter is ...",["Data missing in a table","A narrative that is too long","A chart with too few colors","Unnecessary or distracting elements that obscure the main message"],"Clutter refers to unnecessary or distracting elements that obscure the main message."],["The correct difference between intuition-driven and data-driven decision making according to the comparison table is ...",["Intuition-driven makes best guesses and is high risk; data-driven choices are tested and risk aversing","Intuition-driven is always slower","Data-driven uses no experience at all","Both rely on facts"],"Table: gut feeling vs facts, best guesses vs choices are tested, inherently risky vs risk aversing."],["Launching a new product in a test market to measure market acceptance is a form of ...",["Intuition-based decision making","Data-driven decision making","Experience-based decision making","Descriptive statistics"],"DDDM in business: customer surveys, user testing, test markets, and analysis of demographic changes."],["The factors that determine successful integration of data into decision making according to the material are ...",["The number of data scientists and the IT budget","Internet speed and type of software","Organizational goals; availability, quality, and relevance of data; and the ability to collect and interpret it","Company size and number of branches"],"The DDDM Opportunities slide names three factors: specific goals, availability/quality/relevance of data, and ability to collect, analyze, interpret."],["The role of intuition in the data and intuition cycle according to the material is ...",["Replacing data when data is available","Validating the results of statistical models","Reducing the need for visualization","Raising the right questions and warning when data is incomplete"],"Intuition leads to the right questions and can warn when data is incomplete; data helps recognize bias and changing circumstances."],["In the levels of analytics pyramid, the level that answers 'How can I make it happen?' is ...",["Prescriptive analytics","Predictive analytics","Diagnostic analytics","Descriptive analytics"],"Descriptive: what happened; diagnostic: why; predictive: what will happen; prescriptive: how can I make it happen (optimization)."],["In the levels of analytics pyramid, what increases together when moving up from descriptive to prescriptive?",["Only the amount of raw data needed","Value (competitive advantage) and difficulty","Only the speed of decision making","Reliance on intuition"],"Pyramid axes: competitive advantage/maturity/value and sophistication of intelligence/difficulty, from information to optimization."],["The measure that describes the range of the middle 50% of the data is ...",["Range","Standard deviation","Interquartile range","Mode"],"Interquartile range = third quartile minus first quartile; range = maximum minus minimum."],["The fact '28.75% of Papuans cannot yet read' is used in the material to show that ...",["Papua's education data is inaccurate","Percentages are better than absolute numbers","Charts are more important than narrative","A fact alone is easily forgotten compared with a fact wrapped in a story"],"The slide asks 'How long will you remember this fact?' and then quotes Aaker: a story is 22 times more memorable than facts alone."],["An analyst makes a pie chart with 15 market-share categories. The most appropriate critique based on the material is ...",["Too many categories make a pie chart hard to read; choose a chart that fits the purpose, such as a bar chart for comparison","Pie charts are always forbidden in visualization","More categories should be added to be complete","Use more colors to make it clear"],"The material shows a pie chart with too many categories as a bad graph and recommends choosing a chart by purpose (comparison, part-to-whole, and so on)."],["A manager rejects the analysis results because 'my feeling is different' without checking data quality. According to the material, the more appropriate approach is ...",["Ignore intuition entirely","Use intuition to question the completeness and quality of the data, then test the question with data","Follow intuition because data is always biased","Postpone the decision until the data is perfect"],"Data and intuition are allies: intuition raises questions and warns when data is incomplete, data reveals bias."],["The decluttering steps for the line chart in the material's example include ...",["Add a legend, colors, and 3D effects","Replace the line chart with a pie chart","Remove the border, gridlines, and data markers, tidy the axis labels, then label the data directly","Add gridlines so values are easy to read"],"Avoid Clutter slide sequence: remove border, remove gridlines, remove data markers, clean up axis labels, label data directly."],["The limitations of traditional decision making (intuition and experience) according to the material are ...",["Too slow for fast decisions","It requires large amounts of data","It can only be used by senior managers","Prone to bias and subjectivity, does not consider all information, and is less rigorous than data-driven approaches"],"Limitations: prone to biases and subjectivity, not considering all information or alternatives, suboptimal in complex or novel situations, lacking rigor and objectivity."],["Compare intuition-driven and data-driven decision making.",0,"Intuition-driven: based on experience and instinct; useful when time is limited or with experts, but prone to bias and subjectivity. Data-driven: based on data and statistical analysis, facts can be verified, choices are tested, reduces risk, rooted in measurable historical events. Ideally the two partner."],["Explain the four levels of analytics and their relation to value and difficulty.",0,"Descriptive (what happened), diagnostic (why), predictive (what will happen), prescriptive (how can I make it happen). The higher the level, the greater the competitive advantage/maturity/value and the difficulty; moving from information to optimization. Zara is an example of applying data."],["Why is data storytelling more effective than presenting facts, and how do you manage clutter?",0,"A story with a narrative is easier to remember and understand; it combines analysis, visuals, and narrative for the audience. Clutter (unnecessary elements) is removed to focus on the message, for example removing grids, excess labels, and excess color in the bike sharing case example."],["The three types of prediction are ...",["Descriptive, diagnostic, prescriptive","Training, testing, validation","Clustering, ranking, scoring","Classification, regression, time series"],"Category = classification; number = regression; ordered in time = time series; grouping similar things is not prediction."],["The cheapest forecast ('same as last month/last December') serves as ...",["A baseline the model must beat","The best model","Data leakage","A mistake"],"If the model does not beat 'same as last year', it is not a model, it is a cost."],["Splitting data into train and test is done because ...",["It saves memory","A legal requirement","It speeds things up","It assesses the model on rows it has never seen; otherwise you only test memorization"],"Cross validation fixes the dependence on a single split."],["Titanic confusion matrix: TP 48, TN 93, FP 17, FN 21 (179 rows). Precision and recall are ...",["Precision 48/65 = 0.738; recall 48/69 = 0.696","Precision 0.696; recall 0.738","Both 0.788","Precision 0.845; recall 0.816"],"Precision = TP/(TP+FP) = 48/65; recall = TP/(TP+FN) = 48/69; accuracy = 141/179 = 0.788."],["The column 'Refund processed' in a churn model is an example of ...",["Outlier","Concept drift","Class imbalance","Data leakage"],"The column is only filled in after the answer is known, so the model reads the answer."],["The accuracy paradox occurs when ...",["The model is too complex","The predicted class is rare, so the score is high but the model finds none of the rare cases","The data is outdated","There is too little data"],"Titanic example: 'everyone died' gives 61.5% correct without finding a single survivor."],["Team A closes 80/100 (easy products) and 4/20 (hard); Team B 17/20 and 25/100. Total A 84/120 (70%), B 42/120 (35%). This phenomenon is called ...",["Simpson's paradox: B is better on both products but lower in total because of the product mix","Selection bias","Concept drift","Overfitting"],"Team B was given the hard products; B 85% vs 80% (easy) and 25% vs 20% (hard)."],["According to the material, the probability that someone pays late is calculated from ...",["How many times it happened divided by how many chances it had to happen","The average invoice value","The number of months times the number of delays","The difference between late and on-time months"],"Slide 7: how many times it happened, divided by how many chances it had to happen."],["The question 'How many units will we sell in March?' is a type of ... prediction.",["Classification","Time series","Regression","Not a prediction"],"The answer is a number that comes in time order, so time series."],["According to the material, the question 'Which customers look alike?' ...",["Is classification","Is regression","Is not prediction because there is no answer column","Is time series"],"Which customers look alike: not prediction, there is no answer column."],["In the Titanic data, the method 'say nobody survived' gets how many correct per 100?",["77.7","78.8","82.7","61.5"],"The cheapest answer (everyone died) 61.5; the rule 'women live' 77.7; Naive Bayes 78.8; nearest neighbours k=5 82.7."],["How many rows of Titanic data are used for training and test in the material?",["712 training and 179 test","891 training and 0 test","600 training and 291 test","179 training and 712 test"],"The 891 rows are split into 712 passengers for training and 179 for test."],["The 'recall' metric answers the question ...",["How often is the model right overall","How many of the survivors did the model manage to find","When the model says survived, how often is it right","How many of those who died were correctly left alone"],"Recall: how many of the survivors it found, TP/(TP+FN)."],["The cheapest answer on the California housing data is ...",["The highest median income","The most expensive house price, 500,001","The average price of 207,195 dollars across the 16,512 training neighbourhoods","The linear regression result of 268,186"],"#1 The cheapest answer: 207,195 is the average price across 16,512 neighbourhoods."],["The quote 'All models are wrong, but some are useful' comes from ...",["Jennifer Aaker","Peter Drucker","W. Edwards Deming","George Box"],"Slide 80 quotes George Box, statistician."],["Titanic confusion matrix: TP 48, TN 93, FP 17, FN 21. What is the accuracy?",["0.788","0.738","0.696","0.845"],"Accuracy = (TP + TN) / total = (48 + 93) / 179 = 141/179 = 0.788."],["With the same confusion matrix (TP 48, TN 93, FP 17, FN 21), what is the specificity?",["0.816","0.845","0.716","0.788"],"Specificity = TN/(TN+FP) = 93/110 = 0.845. NPV = 93/114 = 0.816; F1 = 96/134 = 0.716."],["The weakness of the nearest neighbours method according to the strengths and weaknesses table is ...",["Assumes columns are unrelated","Cannot be explained","Slow and needs scaled columns","Depends on who writes the rules"],"Nearest neighbours: no training step, but slow and needs scaled columns. Naive Bayes assumes columns are unrelated; neural networks cannot be explained."],["The algorithm that gives a probability and whose weights can be read, but assumes a straight-line effect, is ...",["Random forest","Gradient boosting","Support vector machine","Logistic regression"],"Logistic regression: gives a probability, weights can be read; con: assumes a straight line effect."],["Five rounds of cross validation on the Titanic data give 76.0; 77.0; 78.1; 77.5; 82.6. What is the average?",["78.2","77.5","78.8","82.6"],"(76.0 + 77.0 + 78.1 + 77.5 + 82.6) / 5 = 391.2 / 5 = 78.24, rounded to 78.2."],["On the California housing data, what is the R-squared of the linear regression and what does it mean?",["0.497, the average error percentage","0.497, the share of price differences between neighbourhoods explained by the columns","37.7, the share of variation explained","0.816, the model's accuracy"],"R squared = 1 - sum gap^2 / sum (real - average)^2 = 0.497; its MAPE is 37.7%."],["The regression metric that expresses the gap as a share of the actual price is ...",["MAE","RMSE","MAPE","R squared"],"MAPE = average of |gap| / real price; for linear regression 37.7%."],["February has 19 working days and March 22. The phenomenon that makes a monthly model wrong because of this is called ...",["Concept drift","Selection bias","Model decay","Calendar effects"],"Calendar effects: peaks shift with dates, not months; 22 vs 19 working days means about 16% more selling days."],["Naive Bayes for a third-class woman: the product for survived is 0.0918 and for died 0.0615. What is P(survived)?",["0.599","0.383","0.689","0.401"],"P(survived) = 0.0918 / (0.0918 + 0.0615) = 0.0918 / 0.1533 = 0.599."],["A model flags 500 customers as about to close their accounts; the team calls all of them and most stay, so the model looks wrong. This phenomenon is called ...",["Self-fulfilling prophecy","Self-defeating prophecy","Data leakage","Accuracy paradox"],"Self-defeating: acting on the prediction prevents the outcome, so the model looks wrong; self-fulfilling is the opposite (a branch has its budget cut and then really underperforms)."],["A recruitment model is trained only on employees who were hired in the past, so it always agrees with old decisions. The way to find out about this problem according to the material is ...",["Add more columns to the model","Remove outliers from the employee data","Accept a small random sample of candidates who would normally be rejected","Use five-round cross validation"],"Selection bias: the only way to find out is to accept a small random sample you would normally reject."],["A model shows that umbrella sales move together with traffic jams. The correct conclusion according to the material is ...",["Reducing umbrella sales will reduce traffic jams","Traffic jams cause people to buy umbrellas","The model suffers from data leakage","A prediction shows what goes together, not what to change"],"Correlation is not causation: a prediction says what goes together, not what to change."],["Explain underfitting, overfitting, and the role of cross validation.",0,"Underfitting: the model is too simple, with low scores on both training and test. Overfitting: the model memorizes the training rows, with a high training score but a low test score. Cross validation (for example five rounds, average 78.2) tests on several hidden parts so that a single split does not give too rosy a picture."],["How can the following three things mislead predictions: self-fulfilling prophecy, selection bias, and concept drift? Give examples.",0,"Self-fulfilling: the model says a branch will fail, the budget is cut, it does fail; conversely self-defeating if 500 at-risk customers are called and stay, so the model looks wrong. Selection bias: only data on accepted applicants, so the model agrees with earlier choices; the fix is to accept a small random sample. Concept drift: a pattern ends because rules, competitors, or the world change (a subsidy is withdrawn, a flood)."],["Explain the regression metrics MAE, RMSE, MAPE, and R-squared on the California housing data (linear regression RMSE 81,173 vs 114,486 for the average).",0,"MAE is the average |gap| (60,332); MSE squares it so large gaps weigh more; RMSE is the square root of MSE back in dollars (81,173); MAPE is the gap relative to price (37.7%); R-squared (0.497) is the proportion of variation between neighbourhoods explained by the columns. Linear regression beats the baseline 'answer the average' (114,486)."],["The three parts of every decision model are ...",["Decisions, objective, constraints","Plan, do, check","Input, process, output","Data, model, report"],"Decisions = what you control; objective = one number to maximize/minimize; constraints = limits that must not be broken."],["In a linear model, the best plan is always at ...",["A corner of the feasible region","The middle of the region","Outside the region","Any point"],"The solver checks corners intelligently."],["The Solver method for straight-line (linear) models is ...",["Goal Seek","Evolutionary","Simplex LP","GRG Nonlinear"],"GRG for smooth curves, Evolutionary for jumps, add int/bin for whole numbers."],["Region 51: max 6 Doc + 1.2 Bed, budget 0.8 Doc + 0.12 Bed <= 40, nurses Doc + 0.5 Bed <= 78. A plan of 38 doctors and 80 beds yields ...",["236 life-years","310 life-years","324 life-years with the budget of 40 and nurses of 78 fully used","288 life-years"],"6x38 + 1.2x80 = 324; budget 30.4+9.6 = 40; nurses 38+40 = 78. The 'half-half' proposal gives 288."],["The budget shadow price is 6.43 life-years per Rp billion. An extra Rp2 billion is worth about ...",["0","3.2 life-years","6.43 life-years","12.9 life-years per year"],"6.43 x 2 = 12.9, valid within the allowable increase range."],["An agency nurse at Rp120 million/year adds 0.86 life-years. The cost in budget = 120/1,000 x 6.43 = 0.77. The decision is ...",["Yes (a small gain); at Rp150 million (0.96) no","No for both","Yes for both","Cannot be calculated"],"The shadow price is the maximum worth sacrificing for one extra unit."],["Nurse roster: 21.4 (fractional), rounded up 27, with Solver's whole-number requirement 22. The main lesson is ...",["Fractions of people can be hired","A roster needs no optimization","Do not just round the Solver result; ask Solver for whole numbers (int)","Rounding is always optimal"],"The whole-number solution saves Rp7.5 million per week (about Rp390 million per year) compared with today."],["According to the material, what does data NOT give you even though it shows Region 51 has the highest death rate?",["What to do","Where the problem is","Which region stands out","What the median is across regions"],"The material's key sentence: data shows where the problem is, but does not tell you what to do. That is the role of prescriptive analytics."],["In the kidney exchange example, what is the model's objective?",["The lowest operation cost","The most transplants","The shortest wait before the donor dies","The most donor pairs on the list"],"Kidney exchange uses decisions (which donor for which patient), constraints (only compatible pairs, short cycles), and the objective of the most transplants."],["A manager writes the constraint \"staff must be enough\". According to the material's three-question test, what is the problem?",["That constraint should be an objective","That constraint is too tight for Solver","That constraint is not yet a number, so it is still a wish","That constraint cannot be used in a linear model"],"Every constraint must be a number. \"Enough staff\" is a wish; \"at least 4 nurses per shift\" is a constraint."],["Why is \"death rate\" not suitable as a decision in a decision model?",["Death rate is not available in the data","Death rate should be a constraint","Death rate is not a number","Death rate is a result, not something you can change directly"],"First test: can the decision really be changed? Death rate is a result, not a decision."],["How are 400 new doctors allocated to 53 regions under current habit, and what is the result?",["Split evenly at about 7.5 doctors per region, yielding about 2,376 life-years per year","All to Region 51, yielding 2,942 life-years","Split by death rate, yielding 2,862 life-years","Split evenly at 8 doctors per region, yielding 3,000 life-years"],"Habit: 400 ÷ 53 ≈ 7.5 doctors per region (\"everyone gets the same\") with a result of about 2,376 life-years per year."],["In the platelet stock case, roughly what is the ratio of the cost of a shortage of one unit to the cost of wasting one unit?",["Equal","A shortage is about 4 times more expensive","Waste is about 2 times more expensive","A shortage is about 10 times more expensive"],"Shortage Rp2.5 million per unit, surplus Rp0.6 million per unit; 2.5 ÷ 0.6 ≈ 4.2, so a shortage is about four times more expensive."],["Which Excel tool is appropriate when you know the result you need and want to find the input value?",["Data Table","Monte Carlo simulation","Goal Seek","Decision tree"],"Goal Seek is used when the required result is known and the input is sought (Data → What-If Analysis → Goal Seek)."],["Solver sends 82 doctors to Region 51, but only 30 doctors have applied to go there. This is an example of which trap?",["False precision","Wrong objective","Bad inputs","Missing constraint"],"The real limit (how many doctors are willing to move) is not in the model. Add that limit, solve again, and build the model together with the people who run it."],["A manager wants a plan that is \"cheap, fast, and the best\". The material's advice is ...",["Pick one as the objective and turn the others into constraints","Make all three objectives at once","Average the three into one score with no limits","Ignore the two that are hard to measure"],"An objective must be exactly one number. \"Cheap, fast and best\" is three; pick one, turn the others into limits."],["The corner point \"doctor room full, then budget\" in Region 51 is 45 doctors and 33.3 beds. How many life-years is that?",["270","About 310","324","288"],"6 × 45 + 1.2 × 33.3 = 270 + 40 = 310 life-years. Still below the 324 from the point of 38 doctors + 80 beds."],["On the Region 51 feasible region chart, the proposal of 30 doctors + 90 beds lies inside the area, not at a corner. This means ...",["The proposal violates a constraint","The proposal is already the optimal plan","Some budget or nurses have not been effectively converted into life-years","The proposal is feasible only if the budget is increased"],"The proposal is inside the feasible area: it does not break the limits, but money and nurses are not fully converted into life-years. The linear optimum is at a corner."],["In the Region 51 sensitivity report, which constraints are binding?",["Room for doctors and room for beds","Budget only","All four constraints","Budget and nurses"],"Budget (40 of 40) and nurses (78 of 78) are fully used with shadow prices of 6.43 and 0.86. Room for doctors and beds still has slack, so their shadow price is 0."],["Regions 26 and 36 have high death rates but receive no new doctors. The correct explanation is ...",["Their number of doctors is already above the cap of 150, so the problem is not a doctor shortage","Their data is blank so they were left out of the model","Solver is wrong because it used Simplex LP","The floor rule of 80 doctors forbids it"],"Region 26 has 153 doctors and Region 36 has 238, above the cap. A high death rate does not mean more doctors would help; look for other causes."],["In allocating 120 doctors to five regions with diminishing returns, a characteristic of the best plan is ...",["All doctors are sent to the region with the highest death rate","The value of the next doctor is the same in every region, about 4.6 life-years","Every region gets the same number of doctors","Region 51 gets all doctors up to the cap"],"At the optimum, the marginal value of the next doctor is equal across regions (±4.6). If not equal, moving a doctor to a higher-value region would increase the total."],["According to the material, what is the main risk of the GRG Nonlinear method in Solver?",["Slow and the result is not guaranteed to be the best for models with IF","It rejects linear models","It can stop at a small hill, so you need to try other starting values","It cannot be used with EXP or LN"],"GRG Nonlinear for smooth curves can stop at a local optimum (small hill); try other start values. Slow and not guaranteed best is a note for Evolutionary."],["If the cost of wasting one unit of stock is higher than the cost of a shortage of one unit, how much stock should be held?",["Above the forecast average","Exactly equal to the average","As much as the busiest week","Below the forecast average"],"The critical ratio will be below 0.5 so the chosen percentile is below the average. The material: if waste costs more than a shortage, stock below the average."],["The nurse shadow price is 0.86 with a constraint of 78 nurses, allowable increase 17.2 and allowable decrease 16.3. Within what range does this shadow price hold?",["About 61.7 to 95.2 nurses","78 to 95.2 nurses only","About 60.8 to 94.3 nurses","Any number of nurses"],"Lower limit 78 − 16.3 = 61.7; upper limit 78 + 17.2 = 95.2. The shadow price holds only within the allowable range."],["Region 29 has no doctor count data and was filled with the median of 113, so room = 37 and Solver sends 37 doctors. If Region 29 actually has 140 doctors (cap 150), how many doctors are misplaced?",["37 doctors","27 doctors","10 doctors","13 doctors"],"The true room is 150 − 140 = 10, so 37 − 10 = 27 doctors are sent to the wrong place. Solver cannot tell a measured number from a guessed one."],["Model assumption: life-years per new doctor = 6 × (death rate ÷ 9.4). Region 51 has a death rate of 12.8. How many life-years does the straight line \"promise\" for 82 doctors, and how many does the curve give?",["Straight line 492, curve 424","Straight line about 670, curve about 670","Straight line about 670, curve about 424","Straight line 954, curve 716"],"6 × 12.8 ÷ 9.4 ≈ 8.17 life-years per doctor; × 82 ≈ 670. With diminishing returns, the curve gives only about 424 because the last doctors add little."],["A regression of death rate on number of doctors gives a slope of +0.003 and R² of 0.004. Why is this line dangerous to feed into Solver?",["R² is too high so Solver overfits","A positive slope means doctors are proven to cause deaths","Solver cannot read regression results at all","Solver would suggest cutting doctors, although doctors gather where many people are sick; correlation is not causation"],"This is the wrong model trap: the slope points the wrong way because doctors go where many are sick. The right benefit curve needs trials, not a cross-section regression."],["Why is the best plan not built around the 'best' option but around the scarcest resource? Use the doctor vs bed comparison.",0,"Per Rp billion of budget: doctor 6/0.8 = 7.5, bed 1.2/0.12 = 10 (bed wins). Per nurse: doctor 6, bed 2.4 (doctor wins). Each wins on one resource and both are scarce, so the best plan uses both (38 doctors + 80 beds = 324)."],["Explain the 'price of fairness' in allocating 400 doctors to 53 regions.",0,"Without a floor, sending all doctors to the regions with the highest death rate yields 2,942 life-years; with a floor of at least 80 doctors per region it becomes 2,862, so the fairness rule costs 80 life-years per year. The model shows the price of a policy; humans decide whether it is worth it. An even split (2,376) is far worse (+20% with the best plan)."],["How many units of platelets should be held if a shortage costs Rp2.5 million and a surplus Rp0.6 million? Explain the critical ratio and the six traps before trusting a plan.",0,"Critical ratio = 2.5/(2.5+0.6) = 0.81; hold the amount that covers demand in 81 of 100 weeks (122 units; average 110). If waste costs Rp1.5 million, the ratio is 0.63 and 114 units. Six traps: wrong objective, missing constraint, bad inputs, wrong model, false precision, nobody follows it."],["The HHI formula is ...",["The average market share","The sum of market shares","The sum of the squared market share of each player (in %)","The largest market share"],"Four equal players: 25^2 x 4 = 2,500; monopoly 10,000."],["In RFM, R, F, M respectively stand for ...",["Reach, Frequency, Media","Retention, Funnel, Market","Recency, Frequency, Monetary","Revenue, Fee, Margin"],"Quintile scores 1-5; 5 means best."],["Lift > 1 in basket analysis (Apriori) means ...",["The products complement each other so bundling is worthwhile","The products substitute each other","There is no relationship","The products do not sell"],"Lift = confidence / share of B; example 50% / 25% = 2.0. Lift < 1 means substitution."],["Cochran with N = 10,000, MoE 5%, confidence 95%, p = 50% gives a sample of about ...",["100","370 respondents","1,000","10,000"],"n0 = 1.96^2 x 0.25/0.05^2 = 384; finite population correction gives 370; for a large population it stays about 384-385."],["In adstock with decay 0.8, spend in week 1 = 100 and week 2 = 200. The accumulated effect in week 2 and week 3 (no spend) is ...",["280 and 224","200 and 0","300 and 240","280 and 280"],"Week 2: 200 + 0.8x100 = 280; week 3: 0 + 0.8x280 = 224."],["Price rises 15% and volume falls 10%. The change in revenue is ...",["Up 15%","Down 3.5%","Down 10%","Up about 3.5%"],"1.15 x 0.90 = 1.035."],["QTY = 200 - 2 x price (Rp thousand), variable cost Rp20 thousand. The prices that maximize revenue and profit are ...",["Revenue at Rp50 thousand; profit at Rp60 thousand","Revenue at Rp60 thousand; profit at Rp50 thousand","Both at Rp50 thousand","Both at Rp60 thousand"],"Revenue = 200P - 2P^2 peaks at 50; profit (P-20)(200-2P) peaks at 60. Optimize contribution margin, not revenue."],["The ordinal data scale (for example satisfaction level or ranking) allows which calculations according to the material?",["Median and order","Only frequency and mode","Differences and mean","All calculations including ratios"],"Nominal: frequency, mode. Ordinal: median, order. Interval: differences, mean. Ratio: everything including ratios and percentages."],["The bias when only customers who stay are observed, while those who have left disappear from the data, is called ...",["Non-response bias","Survivorship bias","Recall bias","Anchoring bias"],"Survivorship: only retained customers are visible; those who left are no longer in the data."],["In STP, targeting is described as ...",["Dividing the market into different parts","A promise that differentiates the brand in customers' minds","A resource decision, not an analysis decision","An automatic result of cluster analysis"],"Segmentation divides the market, targeting chooses which parts to serve (a resource decision), positioning sets the differentiating promise."],["The segment criterion \"differentiable\" means ...",["The segment is large enough to cover costs","The segment can be reached through existing channels","The segment boundaries can be measured","The segment responds to offers differently from other segments"],"Differentiable: responds to an offer differently; if it behaves the same as another segment, it is not a segment. The other options are substantial, accessible, measurable."],["What is the relationship between CLV and customer acquisition cost (CAC) according to the material?",["CLV is the ceiling for CAC, so CAC must stay below CLV","CAC must always equal CLV","CAC is the ceiling for CLV","They are unrelated"],"CLV sets the upper limit on acquisition cost; compute it per segment because a single average hides unprofitable customers."],["Price elasticity with a value of e > 1 means ...",["Inelastic: buyers are not sensitive to price","Elastic: buyers are sensitive to price","Price does not affect sales","Sales rise when price rises"],"A 1% price rise cuts sales by e%. e > 1 is elastic (price sensitive), e < 1 inelastic."],["A Nash equilibrium is a condition in which ...",["The total payoff of all players is always maximal","All players give discounts","No player gains by changing strategy alone","One player controls the entire market"],"Nash equilibrium: no one gains by changing alone. In the Prisoner's Dilemma, that equilibrium is in fact not the best total outcome."],["The main lesson of the 1997 Red River flood case (forecast 49 ft, levee 51 ft, actual peak 54 ft) is ...",["The 49 ft forecast was completely wrong so it was useless","The levee should have been built at exactly 49 ft","Forecast models must always use Bayesian methods","A forecast without a range sounds more certain than reality"],"The ±9 ft range (40-58 ft) was never announced. Decisions follow the communicated number, so an honest forecast always comes with a range."],["Of the three layers (data, model, decision), which jump is said to be the most dangerous in practice?",["From model to decision","From data to model","From data collection to data cleaning","From decision to evaluation"],"The material: the most dangerous jump is not from data to model but from model to decision (what is done Monday morning, with what resources, and who is accountable)."],["Sampling error shrinks in proportion to the square root of n. If the number of respondents is increased fourfold, the sampling error becomes ...",["One quarter","Half","Unchanged","Double"],"√4 = 2, so the error becomes 1/2. Non-sampling error (question design, wrong definitions) does not shrink even when respondents are added."],["In the demo with 1,000 online survey respondents, why is the flip-flopper rule (answers of both 1 and 5 in the same survey) considered too loose?",["The rule only catches speedsters","The rule cannot be calculated in Excel","About 42% of ordinary random 1-5 answer sets already contain both a 1 and a 5","The rule flags only 7 people"],"The descriptive rule flags 498 people to find 151 cheaters; the flip-flop rule is too loose because ±42% of ordinary random answers contain 1 and 5. Combine rules and a model, then check manually."],["According to the material, Jabodetabek is a POOR proxy for which decision?",["Product innovation tests","The premium segment","Urban and e-commerce behavior","Mass-market pricing and national market share claims"],"Jabodetabek is a \"sophisticated microcosm\": good for innovation, premium, urban, and e-commerce, but poor for mass pricing, rural penetration, price sensitivity, and national share claims."],["Customer B on the RFM map: bought 7 months ago, 11 orders, Rp3.6 million (scores R1 F5 M4). The appropriate action is ...",["Can't lose them: win back now","Champions: reward and ask for referrals","New: onboarding for a second purchase","Hibernating: just a cheap reminder"],"Low recency but high frequency and monetary falls in the Can't lose them quadrant, which needs to be won back immediately."],["What is the main difference between LPA and k-means in segmentation?",["LPA chooses the number of groups with a visual elbow","LPA gives soft membership as a probability of 0-1, while k-means gives hard membership","LPA requires missing data to be imputed first","LPA is based on distance optimization"],"LPA is a probabilistic (mixture) model with membership 0-1, number of groups from BIC/AIC, and handles missing data within the model. Elbow, imputation, and distance are features of k-means."],["In the causal impact analysis of Store A's flash sale, why is a simple before-after comparison misleading?",["The pre-promo data is too long","Bayesian structural time series cannot be used","Store B, with no promo, also rose, so the rise would wrongly be credited to the flash sale","A promo always lowers sales"],"Promo impact = actual − counterfactual (prediction without the promo from Store B). The requirement is that the control must not be affected by the promo; without a good control this is just a forecast."],["In the nowcast of sales on day 20, MIDAS-lite has the narrowest 95% interval but the largest error (−4.7%). The lesson is ...",["A model with a leading indicator is always best","Simple scaling must not be used","A nowcast is useless before month end","Narrow is not the same as right"],"All three methods say the target will be reached, but MIDAS-lite, with the narrowest interval, missed the most and its 95% band does not cover the actual: tight is not right."],["An awareness survey in Bekasi has only 14 respondents with a result of 0%, while the all-city average is 15.7%. Hierarchical Bayes (partial pooling) gives 11.6%. Why?",["A city with little data is pulled more strongly toward the overall average (shrinkage)","Bekasi's result is replaced entirely by the overall average","Bekasi is read on its own because its data is the most complete","Bekasi respondents are removed as outliers"],"Partial pooling borrows strength from other cities: Jakarta (n = 300) barely moves, Bekasi (n = 14) moves the furthest. No pooling = 0%, complete pooling = 15.7%."],["Indonesian mobile operator shares in 2023: Telkomsel 44.7%, Indosat 28.1%, XL Axiata 16.5%, Smartfren 10.8%. What is the HHI and its category?",["About 1,884, moderately concentrated","About 3,177, highly concentrated","100, unconcentrated","About 2,500, moderately concentrated"],"44.7² + 28.1² + 16.5² + 10.8² ≈ 1,998 + 790 + 272 + 117 ≈ 3,177, above 2,500, so highly concentrated."],["Cox regression on streaming customers gives a hazard ratio of 0.585 for normal-price customers compared with discounted customers. The interpretation is ...",["The churn risk of normal-price customers is 58.5% lower","Normal-price customers are 41.5% more likely to churn","The churn risk of normal-price customers is 41.5% lower","The median lifetime of normal-price customers is 0.585 months"],"HR 0.585 means a risk of 1 − 0.585 = 0.415, i.e. 41.5% lower. Median survival: 4.7 months (discount) vs 8.2 months (normal)."],["MILP model with a Rp10 million budget: TV ROI 3x (minimum Rp3 million), social 2x, email 5x with a fixed cost of Rp2 million if switched on. What are the maximum revenue and the allocation?",["Rp30 million: the whole Rp10 million to TV","Rp50 million: the whole Rp10 million to email","Rp19 million: TV 3 and social 5","Rp34 million: TV 3, email 5, fixed cost 2, social 0"],"TV minimum 3 (3 × 3 = 9), the remaining 7 less the fixed cost 2 = 5 to email (5 × 5 = 25), total 34. All to TV gives only 30; email cannot take 10 because the fixed cost also uses budget."],["Explain why correct data can mislead, using the example of Package A, which wins in every city but loses overall.",0,"Simpson's paradox: a hidden variable (city) affects the mix; 80% of A's customers are in the less satisfied cities, so the total compares city mixes, not packages. Solution: cross-tabulate by the key variable and show subgroups beside the total; decision: A is superior."],["Explain Marketing Mix Modeling: what it answers, the role of adstock, and the interpretation of the budget optimizer results (YouTube 40->52%, Facebook 30->36%, Newspaper 30->12%; sales 20.5->24.8).",0,"MMM regresses the spend of all channels against sales (weekly/monthly) to measure the size and duration of impact, including channels without click data. Adstock captures the effect that continues after ads stop. The optimizer shifts (not adds) budget from channels with ROI below 1 (newspaper) to channels with a return above 1; this is a model simulation, not a field test, so small-scale tests are needed."],["Summarize the five steps of the decision framework from analysis to decision and the four considerations (value of information, confidence threshold, cost of waiting, accountability).",0,"Five steps: business question (one sentence with a deadline), success metric (one primary, at most two secondary), data sources and quality, method and confidence (descriptive/correlational/causal), decision rule (go/stop threshold plus weekly guardrail). Considerations: value of information (if the gap between a right and wrong decision is small, a long analysis is not worth it), confidence threshold set before seeing results, cost of waiting, and accountability after the decision."]]};
